window.allQuestionsData=window.allQuestionsData||{};window.allQuestionsData["kubernetes"]=[{"id":"lq_33","level":"senior","question":"What is the difference between a Deployment and a StatefulSet?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Architecture","Workloads"],"source":"K8s Production Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">A <strong>Deployment</strong> is designed for stateless applications where Pods are completely fungible, interchangeable, and do not preserve individual network identities or dedicated persistent storage across restarts. A <strong>StatefulSet</strong> manages stateful applications requiring deterministic, unique network hostnames, ordered graceful deployments/scaling/termination, and dedicated persistent storage volumes permanently bound to each individual ordinal index.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Pod Identity:</strong> Deployments create Pods with random hash suffixes (e.g. <code>api-6b7978-x9kz4</code>). StatefulSets assign predictable, zero-indexed ordinal names (e.g. <code>kafka-0</code>, <code>kafka-1</code>, <code>kafka-2</code>).</li>\n  <li><strong>Network Identity:</strong> StatefulSets require a companion <em>Headless Service</em> (<code>clusterIP: None</code>). CoreDNS automatically registers stable A-records formatted as <code>$(pod-name).$(service-name).$(namespace).svc.cluster.local</code>, enabling cluster members to locate specific peers (essential for ZooKeeper, Cassandra, MongoDB).</li>\n  <li><strong>Storage Persistence:</strong> Deployments share PersistentVolumeClaims (PVCs) or mount ephemeral storage. StatefulSets leverage <code>volumeClaimTemplates</code> to dynamically allocate a unique, dedicated PVC per pod ordinal (e.g. <code>data-redis-0</code>, <code>data-redis-1</code>). These PVCs are never automatically deleted on scale-down, preventing catastrophic data loss.</li>\n  <li><strong>Ordering Guarantees:</strong> StatefulSets deploy and scale sequentially (0 → 1 → 2), waiting for the previous Pod to be <code>Running and Ready</code> before launching the next. Scale-down occurs in reverse order (2 → 1 → 0).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>apiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: StatefulSet\nmetadata:\n  name: cassandra\nspec:\n  serviceName: \"cassandra-headless\"\n  replicas: 3\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: cassandra\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels:\n        app: cassandra\n    spec:\n      containers:\n      - name: cassandra\n        image: cassandra:4.1\n        ports:\n        - containerPort: 9042\n          name: cql\n        volumeMounts:\n        - name: cassandra-data\n          mountPath: /var/lib/cassandra\n  volumeClaimTemplates:\n  - metadata:\n      name: cassandra-data\n    spec:\n      accessModes: [ \"ReadWriteOnce\" ]\n      storageClassName: \"gp3\"\n      resources:\n        requests:\n          storage: 100Gi</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Deleting a StatefulSet does NOT delete the bound PVCs or cloud disks (EBS/PV). To clean up storage, you must explicitly delete the PVCs. Furthermore, during node failure, StatefulSets will never forcefully evict a Pod without administrator intervention to prevent Split-Brain scenarios in clustered databases.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">State in an interview: 'Use Deployments for stateless web APIs, microservices, and queue consumers where any replica can handle any request. Reserve StatefulSets for clustered databases, message brokers, and consensus systems (etcd, Kafka, Elasticsearch) where each replica requires a deterministic ordinal, dedicated PVC, and fixed DNS hostname.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"lq_34","level":"junior","question":"Explain the Kubernetes control plane components.","tags":["KUBERNETES","Architecture","ControlPlane"],"source":"K8s Core Architecture","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">The Kubernetes Control Plane makes global decisions about the cluster (e.g., scheduling workloads, detecting and responding to node failures, and maintaining the desired state). It acts as the brain of the cluster, orchestrating all worker nodes and running workloads.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>kube-apiserver:</strong> The central REST API gateway and only component that directly talks to <code>etcd</code>. It handles Authentication, Authorization (RBAC), Mutating/Validating Admission Control, and schema validation. All other components (kubelet, scheduler, controllers) query the API server.</li>\n  <li><strong>etcd:</strong> A strongly consistent, distributed key-value store implementing the Raft consensus algorithm. It holds the entire authoritative state, specifications, metadata, and status of every Kubernetes object.</li>\n  <li><strong>kube-scheduler:</strong> Watches for newly created Pods with no assigned node. It evaluates candidate nodes through a two-phase process: <em>Filtering (Predicates)</em> to discard nodes lacking CPU, RAM, ports, or matching taints, and <em>Scoring (Priorities)</em> to pick the best fit.</li>\n  <li><strong>kube-controller-manager:</strong> A monolithic daemon compiling multiple control loops (ReplicationController, DeploymentController, NodeController, EndpointsController, ServiceAccountController). Each loop watches cluster state via informers and continuously reconciles current state with desired state.</li>\n  <li><strong>cloud-controller-manager (CCM):</strong> Decouples cloud-provider-specific logic (AWS, GCP, Azure) from core K8s, managing Cloud Load Balancers, Route Tables, and Node Lifecycle (detecting terminated cloud instances).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Check Control Plane health and component statuses\nkubectl get componentstatuses # (Legacy)\nkubectl get pods -n kube-system -l tier=control-plane\nkubectl get --raw='/readyz?verbose'\n\n# Verify etcd cluster health\nETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379   --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt   --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt   --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key   endpoint health</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Because etcd relies on write-ahead logging (WAL) and synchronous disk writes across a quorum of nodes, slow disk I/O on control plane nodes is the #1 cause of control plane instability and API server latency. Always provision high-IOPS SSDs (minimum 3000 IOPS / fsync latency < 10ms).</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Highlight the active reconciliation loop design: 'Kubernetes is a declarative system driven by level-triggered reconciliation loops. The control plane never relies on one-time events; controllers continuously watch etcd through the API server and execute diff-and-reconcile steps until reality matches the desired manifest.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"lq_35","level":"mid","question":"What is a readiness probe vs a liveness probe vs a startup probe?","tags":["KUBERNETES","PodLifecycle","Reliability"],"source":"K8s Production Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Health probes are automated diagnostic checks executed periodically by the <code>kubelet</code> on containers inside a Pod. They ensure that traffic only reaches healthy containers, deadlocked containers are restarted, and slow-starting containers are given sufficient boot time.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Liveness Probe:</strong> Detects whether the application has entered a broken or deadlocked state from which it cannot recover on its own (e.g., thread pool deadlock, fatal internal error). If the probe fails <code>failureThreshold</code> consecutive times, kubelet kills the container and initiates its <code>restartPolicy</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Readiness Probe:</strong> Determines whether the container is ready to accept incoming network traffic. If it fails, Kubelet removes the Pod's IP address from all matching Kubernetes Service <code>Endpoints</code> / <code>EndpointSlices</code>. The container is NOT restarted, preventing traffic loss during temporary overload or warmup.</li>\n  <li><strong>Startup Probe:</strong> Designed for legacy, JVM, or slow-starting applications. While the startup probe is evaluating, all liveness and readiness checks are disabled until it passes once. This prevents kubelet from prematurely killing slow-starting apps before they finish initialization.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>apiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: resilient-api\nspec:\n  replicas: 3\n  template:\n    spec:\n      containers:\n      - name: api\n        image: my-company/api:v1.4\n        ports:\n        - containerPort: 8080\n        startupProbe:\n          httpGet:\n            path: /healthz/startup\n            port: 8080\n          failureThreshold: 30 # Gives up to 300s (30 * 10s)\n          periodSeconds: 10\n        readinessProbe:\n          httpGet:\n            path: /healthz/ready\n            port: 8080\n          initialDelaySeconds: 5\n          periodSeconds: 5\n          failureThreshold: 3\n        livenessProbe:\n          httpGet:\n            path: /healthz/live\n            port: 8080\n          periodSeconds: 10\n          failureThreshold: 3</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">A catastrophic anti-pattern: Pointing a Liveness Probe to an endpoint that verifies external dependencies (like PostgreSQL or Redis). If the database experiences a momentary hiccup, ALL pod liveness probes fail simultaneously, causing every pod in the cluster to restart in a massive cascading outage! Liveness should ONLY check container internal health.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Interview Rule of Thumb: 'Liveness determines if the container needs a restart; Readiness determines if the container should receive network traffic; Startup protects slow-booting containers from premature eviction.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"lq_36","level":"senior","question":"How does a Kubernetes Service route traffic to pods?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Networking","KubeProxy"],"source":"K8s Deep Dive","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">A Kubernetes Service is an abstract Layer 4 load balancer that provides a stable virtual IP (ClusterIP) and DNS name for an ephemeral set of Pods. Traffic routing does NOT pass through a physical proxy process; instead, it is handled at the Linux kernel level on every node via <code>kube-proxy</code> or eBPF.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Service & Endpoint Controller:</strong> The API server watches Service objects and Pod label selectors. The <code>EndpointsController</code> maintains <code>Endpoints</code> and <code>EndpointSlice</code> resources containing the IP addresses of all healthy, ready Pods.</li>\n  <li><strong>kube-proxy Daemon:</strong> Runs on every worker node, watching the API server for changes to Services and EndpointSlices. It programs local kernel network translation tables.</li>\n  <li><strong>iptables Mode (Default):</strong> Creates chains of netfilter rules. When a packet targets the Service ClusterIP:Port, PREROUTING and OUTPUT chains apply random destination NAT (DNAT) using the <code>statistic</code> module to balance traffic across pod backend IPs.</li>\n  <li><strong>IPVS Mode:</strong> Uses Linux Virtual Server (IPVS) kernel hash tables ($O(1)$ lookup time) instead of sequential iptables lists ($O(n)$). Essential for large clusters with thousands of services.</li>\n  <li><strong>eBPF (Cilium):</strong> Bypasses kube-proxy and iptables completely, performing socket-level load balancing directly in the Linux kernel for near-zero latency and high throughput.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># View EndpointSlices backing a Service\nkubectl get endpointslices -l kubernetes.io/service-name=my-service\n\n# Inspect iptables NAT rules generated for a Service on a worker node\nsudo iptables -t nat -L KUBE-SERVICES -n -v | grep my-service\nsudo iptables -t nat -L KUBE-SVC-XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX -n -v\n\n# Check kube-proxy operational mode (iptables vs ipvs)\nkubectl logs -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-proxy | grep \"Using\"</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Sequential iptables rule evaluation causes noticeable latency degradation and high CPU usage once clusters exceed 5,000-10,000 Services. Modern high-scale architectures replace kube-proxy with eBPF-based CNI (Cilium) or switch to IPVS mode.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Clarify to the interviewer: 'A Service is not a running container or VM. The ClusterIP is an unreachable virtual IP that exists solely in kernel routing tables. Traffic is intercepted and rewritten before leaving the network interface.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"lq_37","level":"junior","question":"What's the difference between a ConfigMap and a Secret?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Security","Configuration"],"source":"K8s Core Architecture","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Both ConfigMaps and Secrets are Kubernetes objects used to decouple configuration artifacts from container image binaries. The fundamental difference lies in their intended use case, data encoding, memory backing, and security posture.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>ConfigMap:</strong> Stores non-confidential configuration data (properties files, environment variables, command-line arguments, JSON/YAML configuration files) in plain UTF-8 text.</li>\n  <li><strong>Secret:</strong> Intended for sensitive data (API tokens, TLS certificates, database credentials, SSH private keys). In standard Kubernetes, secret values are Base64 encoded (NOT encrypted by default).</li>\n  <li><strong>Memory Backing (tmpfs):</strong> When mounted as volumes, Secrets are written to RAM-backed <code>tmpfs</code> filesystems on worker nodes so sensitive data is never persisted to host disks. ConfigMaps are written to disk.</li>\n  <li><strong>Size Limit:</strong> Both objects are restricted to a maximum size of 1MB due to etcd's default individual request limit.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>apiVersion: v1\nkind: Secret\nmetadata:\n  name: db-credentials\ntype: Opaque\nstringData: # stringData allows plain text in manifest; K8s auto-base64 encodes it in etcd\n  DB_PASSWORD: \"SuperSecretProductionPassword!2026\"\n---\napiVersion: v1\nkind: ConfigMap\nmetadata:\n  name: app-config\ndata:\n  APP_ENV: \"production\"\n  LOG_LEVEL: \"info\"\n  nginx.conf: |\n    server {\n      listen 80;\n      server_name api.company.internal;\n      location / { proxy_pass http://localhost:8080; }\n    }</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Base64 is obfuscation, NOT encryption. Anyone with RBAC access to <code>kubectl get secret -o yaml</code> can decode it with <code>echo '<base64>' | base64 -d</code>. Production clusters MUST enable etcd Encryption at Rest with AWS KMS / HashiCorp Vault, or utilize the External Secrets Operator (ESO).</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Always mention in interviews: 'In production, we avoid storing raw Secret manifests in Git. We utilize GitOps with External Secrets Operator (ESO) syncing from AWS Secrets Manager / Azure Key Vault, or use HashiCorp Vault Agent sidecars with short-lived dynamic credentials.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"lq_38","level":"mid","question":"Explain how Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA) works.","tags":["KUBERNETES","Autoscaling","Production"],"source":"K8s Production Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">The <strong>Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA)</strong> automatically adjusts the replica count of a workload (Deployment, StatefulSet, or ReplicaSet) based on observed CPU utilization, memory consumption, or custom application metrics (e.g. HTTP requests per second, Kafka lag).</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Reconciliation Loop:</strong> The HPA controller in <code>kube-controller-manager</code> runs periodically (default <code>--horizontal-pod-autoscaler-sync-period=15s</code>).</li>\n  <li><strong>Metrics Pipeline:</strong> Queries the Metrics API (backed by Metrics Server for CPU/RAM) or Custom/External Metrics API (Prometheus Adapter, Datadog, KEDA) to calculate average utilization.</li>\n  <li><strong>Scaling Formula:</strong> <code>DesiredReplicas = ceil[ CurrentReplicas * ( CurrentMetricValue / DesiredMetricValue ) ]</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Cooldown & Stabilization:</strong> To prevent rapid thrashing ('flapping'), HPA enforces stabilization windows (default 5 minutes for scale-down, 0 seconds for scale-up) via <code>behavior</code> policies.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>apiVersion: autoscaling/v2\nkind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler\nmetadata:\n  name: checkout-api-hpa\nspec:\n  scaleTargetRef:\n    apiVersion: apps/v1\n    kind: Deployment\n    name: checkout-api\n  minReplicas: 3\n  maxReplicas: 20\n  metrics:\n  - type: Resource\n    resource:\n      name: cpu\n      target:\n        type: Utilization\n        averageUtilization: 70 # Scale when avg CPU &gt; 70% of requests\n  - type: Resource\n    resource:\n      name: memory\n      target:\n        type: AverageValue\n        averageValue: 500Mi\n  behavior:\n    scaleDown:\n      stabilizationWindowSeconds: 300\n      policies:\n      - type: Percent\n        value: 20 # Drop at most 20% of pods per minute\n        periodSeconds: 60</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">HPA CANNOT calculate CPU/memory utilization percentage unless your container defines <code>resources.requests</code>! If <code>requests.cpu</code> is omitted, HPA reports <code><unknown></code> and fails to scale. Furthermore, avoid autoscaling on memory for languages with garbage collection (Java/Node.js) without careful tuning, as JVM will hold memory until major GC.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Staff SRE insight: 'For event-driven microservices processing queues (Kafka, SQS, RabbitMQ), scaling on CPU is a lagging indicator. Pair HPA with KEDA (Kubernetes Event-driven Autoscaling) to scale on queue depth before CPU spikes occur.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"lq_39","level":"senior","question":"What is the difference between a DaemonSet and a Deployment?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Architecture","Workloads"],"source":"K8s Production Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">A <strong>Deployment</strong> manages a specified number of stateless pod replicas distributed across the cluster based on scheduler resource scoring. A <strong>DaemonSet</strong> ensures that an exact copy of a Pod runs on <em>all</em> (or a filtered subset of) worker nodes in the cluster automatically.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Replica Scheduling:</strong> Deployments specify <code>replicas: N</code>. The scheduler places them where capacity exists. DaemonSets have no replica count; when a new node joins the cluster, a DaemonSet pod is immediately launched on it.</li>\n  <li><strong>Node Filtering:</strong> DaemonSets can target specific nodes using <code>nodeSelector</code>, <code>nodeAffinity</code>, or tolerations (e.g., running exclusively on GPU nodes).</li>\n  <li><strong>Toleration of Control Plane & Master Nodes:</strong> System DaemonSets frequently carry tolerations for <code>node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane:NoSchedule</code> to run monitoring or security agents on master nodes.</li>\n  <li><strong>Primary Use Cases:</strong> DaemonSets are used for node-level infrastructure agents: Log shippers (Fluentd, Promtail, Vector), Monitoring node exporters (Datadog agent, Prometheus node-exporter), CNI network plugins (Calico, Cilium, AWS VPC CNI), and Storage daemons (Ceph, GlusterFS, CSI node drivers).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>apiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: DaemonSet\nmetadata:\n  name: node-exporter\n  namespace: monitoring\nspec:\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: node-exporter\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels:\n        app: node-exporter\n    spec:\n      hostNetwork: true\n      hostPID: true\n      containers:\n      - name: node-exporter\n        image: prom/node-exporter:v1.7.0\n        resources:\n          limits: { memory: 200Mi, cpu: 100m }\n          requests: { memory: 50Mi, cpu: 50m }\n      tolerations:\n      - operator: Exists # Run on all nodes regardless of taints</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Because DaemonSets run on every node, their resource requests multiply by the number of nodes in your cluster. If a DaemonSet requests 1GB RAM on a 100-node cluster, it immediately claims 100GB of aggregate cluster capacity.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Explain: 'Deployments represent your business applications that scale dynamically based on user load. DaemonSets represent node-level infrastructure operations (networking, storage, security, observability) that must exist wherever compute exists.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"lq_40","level":"junior","question":"How do you troubleshoot a pod stuck in Pending state?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Troubleshooting","Scheduling"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">A Pod in <code>Pending</code> state means the Pod manifest was successfully accepted by the <code>kube-apiserver</code> and written to etcd, but the <code>kube-scheduler</code> has not yet bound it to a worker node, or container images are currently downloading.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Root Causes:</strong> Insufficient cluster CPU or Memory resources, Node taints with missing tolerations, NodeAffinity or nodeSelector constraints that match 0 nodes, PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC) unbound/pending, or reaching node Pod capacity limits (default 110 pods per node).</li>\n  <li><strong>Diagnostic Step 1:</strong> Run <code>kubectl describe pod &lt;pod-name&gt;</code> and scroll directly to the <strong>Events</strong> section at the bottom. This contains the exact reason from <code>default-scheduler</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Common Event Messages:</strong></li>\n  <li>• <code>0/N nodes available: insufficient cpu/memory</code> → Cluster lacks allocatable resources.</li>\n  <li>• <code>0/N nodes available: node(s) had untolerated taint</code> → Node requires a matching toleration.</li>\n  <li>• <code>0/N nodes available: MatchNodeSelector</code> → No node has the required label.</li>\n  <li>• <code>pod has unbound immediate PersistentVolumeClaims</code> → Storage driver failed to provision volume.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># 1. Inspect Pod Events (The primary troubleshooting command)\nkubectl describe pod &lt;pod-name&gt; -n &lt;namespace&gt;\n\n# 2. Check cluster-wide node allocatable resources vs requests\nkubectl describe nodes | grep -A 8 \"Allocated resources\"\n\n# 3. Check PVC status if mounting persistent volumes\nkubectl get pvc -n &lt;namespace&gt;\nkubectl describe pvc &lt;pvc-name&gt; -n &lt;namespace&gt;\n\n# 4. Filter scheduler error events across the namespace\nkubectl get events -n &lt;namespace&gt; --field-selector reason=FailedScheduling</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">A common trap is excessive resource requests. Developers request <code>cpu: 4</code> for a microservice on nodes that only have <code>4 vCPUs</code> total. After system reservations (kubelet/OS take ~500m), no single node has 4 vCPUs free, leaving the Pod stuck in Pending forever.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Structured interview troubleshooting answer: 'Step 1: Check events with describe pod. Step 2: If insufficient resources, verify node capacity with describe nodes and scale nodes/reduce requests. Step 3: If taints/affinities, check node labels. Step 4: If PVC unbound, inspect StorageClass and CSI driver logs.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"lq_41","level":"mid","question":"What is a taint and toleration?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Scheduling","Security"],"source":"K8s Production Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\"><strong>Taints and Tolerations</strong> work together to ensure that Pods are not scheduled onto inappropriate nodes. A <em>Taint</em> is applied to a Node to repel pods. A <em>Toleration</em> is applied to a Pod, allowing (but not requiring) it to be scheduled onto nodes with matching taints.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Taint Syntax:</strong> <code>key=value:effect</code>. Effects include:</li>\n  <li>• <code>NoSchedule:</code> If a Pod does not have a matching toleration, it will NOT be scheduled on this node (existing running pods remain untouched).</li>\n  <li>• <code>PreferNoSchedule:</code> Soft constraint; scheduler tries to avoid placing untolerated pods on the node, but will place them if no other capacity exists.</li>\n  <li>• <code>NoExecute:</code> Hard eviction; if the node is tainted, any currently running Pod lacking the toleration is immediately evicted from the node.</li>\n  <li><strong>tolerationSeconds:</strong> Used with <code>NoExecute</code> to allow a Pod to stay on an unhealthy/unreachable node for a grace period (e.g. 300s) before being evicted, preventing premature failovers during transient network blips.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># 1. Taint a node dedicated for GPU workloads via CLI\nkubectl taint nodes worker-gpu-01 dedicated=gpu:NoSchedule\n\n# 2. Pod specification with matching toleration\napiVersion: v1\nkind: Pod\nmetadata:\n  name: ml-inference\nspec:\n  containers:\n  - name: cuda-app\n    image: company/cuda-ml:v2\n  tolerations:\n  - key: \"dedicated\"\n    operator: \"Equal\"\n    value: \"gpu\"\n    effect: \"NoSchedule\"\n  # Note: Tolerations ALLOW scheduling; use nodeSelector or nodeAffinity to FORCE it\n  nodeSelector:\n    dedicated: \"gpu\"</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">A critical distinction: Tolerations DO NOT attract pods to a specific node! A toleration merely says 'I am permitted to run on a tainted node.' To guarantee the Pod lands exclusively on that GPU node, you must pair the toleration with <code>nodeSelector</code> or <code>nodeAffinity</code>.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Analogy for interviews: 'Taints are like locked doors on nodes. Tolerations are like keys carried by pods. Having a key allows you to enter the room, but you still need an incentive (NodeAffinity) to choose that specific room.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"lq_42","level":"senior","question":"Explain Kubernetes namespaces and when to use them.","tags":["KUBERNETES","MultiTenancy","Governance"],"source":"K8s Core Architecture","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Namespaces provide a logical partition within a single physical Kubernetes cluster. They create virtual sub-clusters that provide scoping for resource names, role-based access control (RBAC), network segmentation (NetworkPolicies), and compute resource consumption quotas.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Scope of Resources:</strong> Most resources (Pods, Services, Deployments, ConfigMaps) are namespaced. Cluster-wide resources (Nodes, PersistentVolumes, StorageClasses, Namespaces, CRD definitions) exist outside namespaces.</li>\n  <li><strong>DNS Scoping:</strong> Services within the same namespace can be addressed by their bare name (e.g. <code>http://backend</code>). Cross-namespace communication requires the Fully Qualified Domain Name (FQDN): <code>backend.billing.svc.cluster.local</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Resource Quotas:</strong> Administrators apply <code>ResourceQuota</code> objects to limit aggregate CPU, memory, and object counts (e.g. max 10 Services) per namespace.</li>\n  <li><strong>LimitRanges:</strong> Enforce default and maximum/minimum CPU and RAM constraints per individual Pod in that namespace.</li>\n  <li><strong>RBAC Boundaries:</strong> <code>Role</code> and <code>RoleBinding</code> restrict developer permissions to specific namespaces without granting cluster-wide access.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>apiVersion: v1\nkind: ResourceQuota\nmetadata:\n  name: team-quota\n  namespace: checkout-dev\nspec:\n  hard:\n    requests.cpu: \"10\"\n    requests.memory: 20Gi\n    limits.cpu: \"20\"\n    limits.memory: 40Gi\n    pods: \"30\"\n    services.loadbalancers: \"1\"</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Namespaces do NOT provide hard security or network isolation by default! By default, any Pod in namespace A can send network packets to any Pod in namespace B. To enforce isolation, you MUST deploy a CNI plugin that supports <code>NetworkPolicy</code> and apply default-deny policies.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Interview Best Practice: 'Use namespaces for multi-tenancy across teams and environments within non-production (dev/test/staging). For production vs non-production, hard multi-tenancy via physically separate clusters is the recommended enterprise standard for blast-radius containment.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"lq_43","level":"junior","question":"What is an Ingress and how is it different from a Service?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Networking","Ingress"],"source":"K8s Core Architecture","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">A <strong>Service</strong> operates at Layer 4 (TCP/UDP), providing internal load balancing and port forwarding to Pods. An <strong>Ingress</strong> is a Layer 7 (HTTP/HTTPS) routing specification that exposes HTTP/S routes from outside the cluster to internal Services, offering host-based routing, path routing, and SSL/TLS termination.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Ingress vs Ingress Controller:</strong> An Ingress resource is merely a set of routing rules (metadata). For it to function, an <em>Ingress Controller</em> (e.g. Ingress-NGINX, Traefik, AWS ALB Controller) must be running to process rules and configure an actual proxy.</li>\n  <li><strong>Cost Efficiency:</strong> Creating 20 <code>LoadBalancer</code> Services in AWS creates 20 AWS Elastic Load Balancers ($$$). A single Ingress Controller utilizes 1 single LoadBalancer to route traffic across hundreds of microservices using Hostnames (<code>api.domain.com</code>) or Paths (<code>/orders</code>).</li>\n  <li><strong>TLS Termination:</strong> Ingress terminates TLS at the edge using Kubernetes Secrets, decrypting incoming traffic before forwarding plain HTTP to backend pods.</li>\n  <li><strong>Gateway API Evolution:</strong> The modern successor to Ingress is the Kubernetes Gateway API, providing role-oriented, expressive APIs (GatewayClass, Gateway, HTTPRoute, TCPRoute).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: Ingress\nmetadata:\n  name: core-ingress\n  annotations:\n    cert-manager.io/cluster-issuer: \"letsencrypt-prod\"\n    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/ssl-redirect: \"true\"\nspec:\n  ingressClassName: nginx\n  tls:\n  - hosts: [ \"api.company.com\" ]\n    secretName: api-tls-cert\n  rules:\n  - host: api.company.com\n    http:\n      paths:\n      - path: /users\n        pathType: Prefix\n        backend:\n          service:\n            name: user-service\n            port: { number: 80 }\n      - path: /billing\n        pathType: Prefix\n        backend:\n          service:\n            name: billing-service\n            port: { number: 80 }</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Applying an Ingress manifest without an installed Ingress Controller results in zero external traffic routing; the resource will simply sit in etcd with an empty <code>ADDRESS</code> field.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">State: 'Services operate at L4; Ingress operates at L7. Ingress consolidates multiple services behind a single external IP, handling path-based routing, virtual host routing, and TLS termination.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"lq_44","level":"mid","question":"How would you troubleshoot high memory usage causing pod OOMKills?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Troubleshooting","ResourceManagement"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">An <strong>OOMKilled</strong> (Out of Memory) event occurs when a container attempts to allocate more memory than its configured <code>resources.limits.memory</code>, or when the underlying worker node exhausts its total memory and the Linux kernel OOM Killer terminates containers.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Exit Code 137:</strong> Containers terminated by OOMKill exit with status code 137 ($128 + 9$, representing <code>SIGKILL</code>).</li>\n  <li><strong>Container Limit vs Node Pressure:</strong></li>\n  <li>• <em>Container Cgroup Limit:</em> If container memory exceeds <code>limits.memory</code>, the cgroup memory controller immediately sends <code>SIGKILL</code> to processes in that container. Pod status displays <code>OOMKilled: true</code>.</li>\n  <li>• <em>Node-Level Exhaustion:</em> If the worker node runs out of physical RAM, the Linux kernel ranks processes via <code>oom_score_adj</code> and kills pods in the <code>BestEffort</code> or <code>Burstable</code> QoS classes first.</li>\n  <li><strong>Investigation Workflow:</strong></li>\n  <li>1. Identify OOM status: <code>kubectl get pod &lt;pod&gt; -o yaml | grep -A 5 lastState</code>.</li>\n  <li>2. Check current and historical memory usage via Prometheus or <code>kubectl top pod</code>.</li>\n  <li>3. Profile memory leaks (heap dumps, profilers, unclosed database connections).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># 1. Check if pod was terminated with OOMKilled\nkubectl get pod &lt;pod-name&gt; -o jsonpath='{.status.containerStatuses[*].lastState.terminated}'\n\n# 2. Check node kernel dmesg for OOM killer invocations\nkubectl debug node/&lt;node-name&gt; -it --image=busybox -- dmesg -T | grep -i \"oom-killer\"\n\n# 3. View live memory utilization across pods\nkubectl top pods -A --sort-by=memory</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">JVM applications (Java/Kotlin) frequently get OOMKilled if developers don't set <code>-XX:MaxRAMPercentage=75.0</code>. Older JVMs don't detect container cgroup memory limits and attempt to size their heap based on the host node's physical RAM, instantly triggering OOMKill.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Explain: 'When troubleshooting OOMKill, first distinguish between a container-level limit breach (fix: tune application memory/increase limits) versus a node-level memory pressure eviction (fix: configure proper QoS classes and cluster autoscaler).'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"lq_45","level":"senior","question":"What is the difference between resource requests and limits?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Performance","ResourceManagement"],"source":"K8s Production Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\"><strong>Requests</strong> and <strong>Limits</strong> control how Kubernetes allocates compute resources (CPU and Memory) to containers. Requests determine scheduling and guaranteed minimums, while Limits define the maximum ceiling a container is permitted to consume.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Requests (Scheduling & Guarantees):</strong> Used by <code>kube-scheduler</code> to determine which node can accommodate the Pod. The scheduler ensures that the sum of all container requests on a node never exceeds the node's allocatable capacity. Requests are mapped to Linux <code>cpu.shares</code> and <code>memory.min</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Limits (Hard Caps & Throttling):</strong> Enforced by the Linux kernel cgroups runtime:</li>\n  <li>• <em>CPU Limits:</em> Enforced via Completely Fair Scheduler (CFS) quota. CPU is a compressible resource; if a container exceeds its CPU limit, it is <strong>throttled</strong> (slowed down), never killed.</li>\n  <li>• <em>Memory Limits:</em> Memory is incompressible. If a container exceeds its memory limit, the kernel cgroup immediately sends <code>SIGKILL</code> (<strong>OOMKilled</strong>, Exit code 137).</li>\n  <li><strong>Quality of Service (QoS) Classes:</strong></li>\n  <li>• <em>Guaranteed:</em> Requests == Limits for all containers (highest priority, last to be evicted).</li>\n  <li>• <em>Burstable:</em> Requests < Limits (can burst when capacity exists).</li>\n  <li>• <em>BestEffort:</em> No requests or limits set (lowest priority, first to be killed during node pressure).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>resources:\n  requests:\n    cpu: \"250m\"      # 0.25 vCPU (guaranteed for scheduling)\n    memory: \"512Mi\"  # 512 MB guaranteed\n  limits:\n    cpu: \"1000m\"     # 1 vCPU max (throttled if exceeded)\n    memory: \"1Gi\"    # 1 GB max (OOMKilled if exceeded)</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Over-constraining CPU limits can cause severe application latency spikes due to CFS quota period throttling, even when the host node has 80% idle CPU! Many production teams set memory requests == limits, but omit CPU limits or set high ceilings to prevent unnecessary throttling.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Summary: 'Requests are for the Scheduler (where to place the pod); Limits are for the Kernel (how much the pod can consume before throttling CPU or OOMKilling memory).'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"lq_46","level":"junior","question":"Explain how you'd perform a zero-downtime rolling update in Kubernetes.","tags":["KUBERNETES","Deployments","Reliability"],"source":"K8s Production Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">A <strong>Rolling Update</strong> is the default deployment strategy in Kubernetes. It transitions an application from version A to version B incrementally by gradually spinning up new Pods while terminating old ones, ensuring zero downtime for end users.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>maxSurge:</strong> The maximum number (or percentage) of Pods that can be created above the desired replica count during the update (e.g. <code>25%</code>).</li>\n  <li><strong>maxUnavailable:</strong> The maximum number (or percentage) of Pods that can be unavailable during the update process (e.g. <code>0</code> for strict zero-downtime).</li>\n  <li><strong>Readiness Probes (Critical):</strong> The new Pod must pass its readiness probe before the Deployment controller considers it healthy and proceeds to terminate an old Pod. Without readiness probes, Kubernetes immediately sends traffic to unready pods, causing 502/503 errors.</li>\n  <li><strong>Graceful Termination (preStop Hook):</strong> When a pod is terminated, kube-proxy needs time to update iptables across all nodes. A <code>preStop: sleep 10</code> hook keeps the container serving connections while it is removed from Service Endpoints.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>apiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: payments-api\nspec:\n  replicas: 4\n  strategy:\n    type: RollingUpdate\n    rollingUpdate:\n      maxSurge: 1        # Launch 1 new pod before killing an old one\n      maxUnavailable: 0  # NEVER drop below 4 active pods\n  template:\n    spec:\n      containers:\n      - name: api\n        image: company/payments:v2.1.0\n        lifecycle:\n          preStop:\n            exec:\n              command: [\"/bin/sh\", \"-c\", \"sleep 10\"] # Wait for endpoint deregistration\n        readinessProbe:\n          httpGet:\n            path: /health/ready\n            port: 8080</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">If <code>maxUnavailable: 0</code> and <code>maxSurge: 1</code> are configured, your cluster must have enough spare compute capacity to schedule that 1 extra pod. If the cluster is 100% full, the new pod will remain in Pending and the deployment will stall indefinitely.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Key to zero-downtime: '1. Set maxUnavailable: 0. 2. Implement robust readiness probes so pods only receive traffic when warmed up. 3. Add a preStop sleep hook to allow inflight requests to complete and kube-proxy endpoints to drain.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"lq_47","level":"mid","question":"What is a PodDisruptionBudget and why use one?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Reliability","HighAvailability"],"source":"K8s Production Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">A <strong>PodDisruptionBudget (PDB)</strong> limits the number of Pods of a replicated application that can be down simultaneously from <em>voluntary disruptions</em> (such as node drains during cluster upgrades, node maintenance, or cluster autoscaler scale-downs).</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Voluntary vs Involuntary Disruptions:</strong></li>\n  <li>• <em>Involuntary:</em> Hardware failure, kernel panic, out-of-memory kernel kill, network partition (PDB cannot prevent these).</li>\n  <li>• <em>Voluntary:</em> <code>kubectl drain</code>, cloud cluster upgrades (EKS/GKE AMI updates), Cluster Autoscaler removing underutilized nodes.</li>\n  <li><strong>Eviction API Enforcement:</strong> When an automated process or engineer drains a node, it calls the Eviction API. The API checks matching PDBs; if evicting the pod violates <code>minAvailable</code> or <code>maxUnavailable</code>, the eviction is rejected until replacement pods become Ready on other nodes.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>apiVersion: policy/v1\nkind: PodDisruptionBudget\nmetadata:\n  name: payment-pdb\n  namespace: prod\nspec:\n  minAvailable: 2 # At least 2 pods must always be Running and Ready\n  # OR: maxUnavailable: 1\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: payment-service</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Deadlock Danger: If you set <code>minAvailable: 3</code> on a Deployment with <code>replicas: 3</code>, and one pod crashes involuntarily, <code>kubectl drain</code> will be blocked forever because evicting any pod would reduce availability to 2! Always allow at least 1 pod to be disrupted, or size replicas higher than minAvailable.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">State in interviews: 'PDBs protect highly available workloads from being accidentally taken down during automated platform operations like node drains and AMI rolling upgrades.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_1","level":"junior","question":"What is Kubernetes and why is it used?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Fundamentals","Overview"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Kubernetes (K8s) is an open-source container orchestration platform originally designed by Google and maintained by the Cloud Native Computing Foundation (CNCF). It automates the deployment, scaling, networking, and lifecycle management of containerized applications across clusters of physical or virtual machines.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Declarative Configuration:</strong> Instead of writing imperative deployment scripts, operators declare the desired end-state in YAML/JSON, and Kubernetes continually reconciles reality to match.</li>\n  <li><strong>Key Core Capabilities:</strong></li>\n  <li>• <em>Service Discovery & Load Balancing:</em> Exposes containers via DNS and balances traffic across backend pods.</li>\n  <li>• <em>Storage Orchestration:</em> Automatically mounts local, cloud, or network storage systems (EBS, EFS, Ceph, NFS).</li>\n  <li>• <em>Automated Rollouts & Rollbacks:</em> Progressively rolls out changes and reverts instantaneously on failure.</li>\n  <li>• <em>Self-Healing:</em> Automatically restarts failed containers, reschedules pods when nodes die, and terminates unhealthy pods failing probes.</li>\n  <li>• <em>Secret & Config Management:</em> Manages sensitive data outside container images without rebuilds.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Declarative application management\nkubectl apply -f deployment.yaml\n\n# Check cluster info and node states\nkubectl cluster-info\nkubectl get nodes -o wide</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Kubernetes is NOT a PaaS like Heroku. It does not provide built-in CI/CD pipelines, application-level logging backends, or source code builders out of the box; these must be architected as complementary cloud-native tools.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">High-level summary: 'Kubernetes abstracts underlying compute, network, and storage infrastructure into a unified distributed operating system, enabling portable, highly resilient, and declarative workload management across any cloud.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_2","level":"junior","question":"Explain the core components of Kubernetes architecture.","tags":["KUBERNETES","Architecture","Core"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">A Kubernetes cluster consists of two distinct operational tiers: the <strong>Control Plane</strong> (which manages the cluster state, scheduling, and API) and <strong>Worker Nodes</strong> (which execute the container workloads).</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Control Plane Tier:</strong></li>\n  <li>• <code>kube-apiserver:</code> Front door for all REST calls; authenticates, authorizes, and validates requests.</li>\n  <li>• <code>etcd:</code> Distributed key-value store holding the complete cluster state.</li>\n  <li>• <code>kube-scheduler:</code> Assigns unscheduled pods to optimal worker nodes based on resources and constraints.</li>\n  <li>• <code>kube-controller-manager:</code> Runs core reconciliation loops (node, deployment, endpoints).</li>\n  <li>• <code>cloud-controller-manager:</code> Interfaces with underlying cloud provider APIs.</li>\n  <li><strong>Worker Node Tier:</strong></li>\n  <li>• <code>kubelet:</code> Node agent ensuring containers described in PodSpecs are running and healthy.</li>\n  <li>• <code>kube-proxy:</code> Manages Layer 4 network packet filtering and translation rules (iptables/IPVS).</li>\n  <li>• <code>Container Runtime:</code> OCI-compliant engine that runs containers (containerd, CRI-O).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># View all running control plane and node infrastructure components\nkubectl get pods -n kube-system -o wide</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">If the control plane goes down entirely, running workloads on worker nodes continue to run and serve user traffic! However, no new pods can be scheduled, auto-scaling halts, and failed pods cannot be replaced until the control plane recovers.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Draw the architecture clearly in mind: Control plane components coordinate through etcd via the API server; worker nodes communicate strictly with the API server via kubelet and kube-proxy.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_4","level":"junior","question":"What is the relationship between Kubernetes and Docker?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Containers","CRI"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Docker is a containerization technology used to package applications and their dependencies into standardized container images. Kubernetes is a container orchestrator that manages, scales, and coordinates thousands of those containers across clusters of machines.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Analogy:</strong> Docker builds the container ships; Kubernetes acts as the harbor master coordinating fleets of ships.</li>\n  <li><strong>Dockershim Deprecation (v1.24+):</strong> In early versions, Kubernetes communicated with Docker via a bridge called <code>dockershim</code>. In Kubernetes 1.24+, dockershim was completely removed in favor of the direct <strong>Container Runtime Interface (CRI)</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>OCI Standard:</strong> Docker produces Open Container Initiative (OCI) compliant images. Modern Kubernetes clusters use lightweight, dedicated CRI runtimes such as <strong>containerd</strong> or <strong>CRI-O</strong> to run those exact same Docker images without needing the heavy Docker desktop/daemon.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Inspect the container runtime used by your cluster nodes\nkubectl get nodes -o wide\n# Look at the CONTAINER-RUNTIME column (e.g. containerd://1.7.11, cri-o://1.28.0)</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Common misconception: 'Kubernetes deprecated Docker so I can no longer use Dockerfiles.' FALSE! Images built with <code>docker build</code> are 100% OCI-compliant and run seamlessly on modern containerd/CRI-O Kubernetes clusters.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Interview answer: 'Docker is an image packaging and developer tool. Kubernetes orchestrates containers at scale. With CRI standards, Kubernetes interacts directly with containerd/CRI-O, eliminating the redundant Docker daemon overhead.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_5","level":"junior","question":"Explain Namespaces in Kubernetes. When would you use them?","tags":["KUBERNETES","MultiTenancy","Administration"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Namespaces provide virtual clustering within a single physical Kubernetes cluster. They enable multi-tenant resource sharing, naming disambiguation, and granular access delegation across distinct projects, teams, or applications.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Default Namespaces:</strong></li>\n  <li>• <code>default:</code> The default target for objects with no specified namespace.</li>\n  <li>• <code>kube-system:</code> Reserved for control plane and infrastructure components (CoreDNS, kube-proxy, CNI).</li>\n  <li>• <code>kube-public:</code> Readable by all users (mostly holds cluster info).</li>\n  <li>• <code>kube-node-lease:</code> Holds node heartbeat leases for node health tracking.</li>\n  <li><strong>When to Use:</strong></li>\n  <li>• Separating different microservices or business units (e.g. <code>payments</code>, <code>catalog</code>, <code>analytics</code>).</li>\n  <li>• Environment segmentation in non-prod clusters (e.g. <code>dev</code>, <code>qa</code>, <code>staging</code>).</li>\n  <li>• Applying resource boundaries via <code>ResourceQuota</code> and security limits via <code>LimitRange</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Create a namespace with metadata\nkubectl create namespace payments-prod\n\n# Set your active namespace context so you don't need -n payments-prod every time\nkubectl config set-context --current --namespace=payments-prod</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Deleting a namespace with <code>kubectl delete ns &lt;name&gt;</code> is an irreversible cascading action: it permanently terminates every Pod, Deployment, Service, Secret, and PVC inside that namespace.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Explain: 'Namespaces are ideal for logical boundaries and soft multi-tenancy. For compliance, PCI-DSS, or untrusted code execution, physical cluster isolation is required.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_6","level":"junior","question":"What are Labels and Selectors in Kubernetes?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Core","Metadata"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\"><strong>Labels</strong> are key-value pairs attached to Kubernetes objects (Pods, Nodes, Services) used to organize and categorize them. <strong>Selectors</strong> are query filters that allow users and controllers to identify and group objects based on their labels.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Equality-Based Selectors:</strong> Match by exact key/value equality (<code>=</code>, <code>==</code>, <code>!=</code>). Example: <code>app=frontend,tier=web</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Set-Based Selectors:</strong> Support set operations (<code>in</code>, <code>notin</code>, <code>exists</code>). Example: <code>environment in (prod, staging)</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Controller Glue:</strong> Kubernetes objects are loosely coupled. A Service routes traffic to Pods because its <code>selector</code> matches the Pods' <code>labels</code>. A Deployment owns ReplicaSets via label matching.</li>\n  <li><strong>Recommended Standard Labels:</strong> The CNCF recommends standard labels: <code>app.kubernetes.io/name</code>, <code>app.kubernetes.io/instance</code>, <code>app.kubernetes.io/version</code>, <code>app.kubernetes.io/component</code>, <code>app.kubernetes.io/managed-by</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Query pods using equality selector\nkubectl get pods -l app=payment,environment=production\n\n# Query pods using set-based selector\nkubectl get pods -l 'environment in (prod, staging),tier!=database'\n\n# Label a running node\nkubectl label nodes worker-01 disktype=nvme</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">If you accidentally assign the same labels to Pods belonging to two different Deployments, a single Service will blindly load-balance traffic across BOTH deployments, causing severe intermittent bugs.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Core concept: 'Labels and Selectors are the core glue of Kubernetes. Everything from service routing, replication management, node affinity, and network policies is wired dynamically through label matching.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_8","level":"junior","question":"Differentiate between ClusterIP, NodePort, and LoadBalancer Service types.","tags":["KUBERNETES","Networking","Services"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Kubernetes Services expose applications running on Pods using three primary access modes, each building upon the previous layer's capabilities.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>1. ClusterIP (Default):</strong> Exposes the Service on an internal cluster-only virtual IP. Reachable ONLY from inside the cluster (other pods or node processes). Used for databases, backend APIs, and internal microservices.</li>\n  <li><strong>2. NodePort:</strong> Builds on top of ClusterIP. Allocates a static port from a dedicated cluster-wide range (default: <code>30000-32767</code>) on every worker node's physical IP address. External traffic hitting <code>&lt;NodeIP&gt;:&lt;NodePort&gt;</code> is forwarded to the ClusterIP.</li>\n  <li><strong>3. LoadBalancer:</strong> Builds on top of NodePort and ClusterIP. Asynchronously provisions an external Layer 4 cloud load balancer (AWS NLB/CLB, GCP Cloud LB, Azure LB) that routes incoming traffic to the NodePorts on worker nodes.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># ClusterIP Service (Internal Only)\napiVersion: v1\nkind: Service\nmetadata:\n  name: internal-db\nspec:\n  type: ClusterIP\n  selector:\n    app: postgres\n  ports:\n  - port: 5432\n    targetPort: 5432\n---\n# LoadBalancer Service (External Cloud LB)\napiVersion: v1\nkind: Service\nmetadata:\n  name: public-web\nspec:\n  type: LoadBalancer\n  selector:\n    app: web\n  ports:\n  - port: 80\n    targetPort: 8080</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Directly exposing dozens of <code>LoadBalancer</code> services in public cloud gets expensive fast ($20-$30/month per ELB). The cloud-native best practice is to expose one Ingress Controller via LoadBalancer, and route all apps through internal ClusterIP Services.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Hierarchy to remember: 'LoadBalancer creates a NodePort; NodePort creates a ClusterIP; ClusterIP routes to the Pod endpoints.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_9","level":"junior","question":"What is a Deployment in Kubernetes?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Workloads","Deployments"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">A <strong>Deployment</strong> is a higher-level declarative workload controller that manages the deployment, scaling, rolling updates, and self-healing of stateless applications running in Pods.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Three-Tier Hierarchy:</strong> Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pods. The Deployment manages multiple ReplicaSets (current and previous versions), while the active ReplicaSet maintains the exact number of Pod replicas.</li>\n  <li><strong>Reconciliation Loop:</strong> If a Pod crashes or a worker node catches fire, the Deployment's underlying ReplicaSet notices the count discrepancy and instructs Kubelet to schedule a replacement immediately.</li>\n  <li><strong>Deployment Strategies:</strong></li>\n  <li>• <code>RollingUpdate:</code> Gradually replaces old pods with new ones with zero downtime.</li>\n  <li>• <code>Recreate:</code> Kills all existing pods before starting new ones (useful when running two versions concurrently is prohibited by database migrations).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>apiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: web-api\n  labels:\n    app: web-api\nspec:\n  replicas: 3\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: web-api\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels:\n        app: web-api\n    spec:\n      containers:\n      - name: api\n        image: nginx:1.25-alpine\n        ports:\n        - containerPort: 80</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Never manage standalone Pods or bare ReplicaSets directly. Always use Deployments so you get automated rolling updates, rollback capabilities, and declarative version tracking.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Emphasize: 'Deployments are the cornerstone of stateless microservices in Kubernetes, managing ReplicaSets behind the scenes to provide automated self-healing and declarative rollouts.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_10","level":"junior","question":"Explain the difference between a ReplicaSet and a Deployment.","tags":["KUBERNETES","Workloads","Core"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">A <strong>ReplicaSet</strong> is a low-level controller whose sole responsibility is to maintain a stable set of identical running Pod replicas at any given time. A <strong>Deployment</strong> is a higher-level controller that manages ReplicaSets, enabling declarative rolling updates, rollbacks, and version histories.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>ReplicaSet Focus:</strong> Only cares about numbers: 'Are there exactly 3 pods with label <code>app=api</code> running?' If 4 exist, it deletes one; if 2 exist, it launches one.</li>\n  <li><strong>Deployment Focus:</strong> Manages lifecycles and software releases. When you update a container image in a Deployment, it creates a <em>new</em> ReplicaSet for version 2, scales it up, while progressively scaling down the old ReplicaSet for version 1.</li>\n  <li><strong>Rollback Capability:</strong> Deployments retain inactive ReplicaSets in etcd (configured via <code>revisionHistoryLimit</code>, default 10). Reverting a deployment simply scales up an old ReplicaSet and scales down the current one.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># View ReplicaSets managed by Deployments\nkubectl get replicasets\n\n# Inspect Deployment rollout history\nkubectl rollout history deployment/web-api\n\n# Undo deployment to previous ReplicaSet\nkubectl rollout undo deployment/web-api</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Do NOT edit or scale ReplicaSets directly if they are owned by a Deployment. The Deployment controller will immediately overwrite your manual changes to reconcile with the Deployment's top-level spec.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Simple summary: 'A ReplicaSet manages Pod counts; a Deployment manages ReplicaSets and version rollouts.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_12","level":"junior","question":"What is a DaemonSet and when would you use it?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Workloads","DaemonSet"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">A <strong>DaemonSet</strong> ensures that an exact single copy of a specific Pod runs on all (or targeted) worker nodes across the Kubernetes cluster. As nodes are added to the cluster, Pods are automatically scheduled onto them.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Automatic Provisioning:</strong> When a cluster autoscaler adds a new node, the DaemonSet controller detects the node and immediately deploys the daemon pod.</li>\n  <li><strong>Automatic Decommissioning:</strong> When a node is drained and deleted, the DaemonSet pods are automatically garbage collected.</li>\n  <li><strong>Common Use Cases:</strong></li>\n  <li>• <em>Log Collection Daemons:</em> Fluentd, Filebeat, Promtail, Vector running on every host to stream container logs.</li>\n  <li>• <em>Node Metrics Monitoring:</em> Prometheus <code>node-exporter</code>, Datadog agent, Dynatrace.</li>\n  <li>• <em>Cluster Networking (CNI):</em> Calico, Cilium, AWS VPC CNI node agents managing network namespaces.</li>\n  <li>• <em>Storage CSI Plugins:</em> CSI node drivers that mount block storage to host directories.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># View all running DaemonSets in kube-system\nkubectl get daemonsets -n kube-system\n\n# Check DaemonSet rollout status\nkubectl rollout status daemonset/cilium -n kube-system</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">If a worker node is cordoned or tainted with <code>NoSchedule</code>, DaemonSet pods will not schedule on it unless the DaemonSet explicitly defines matching tolerations.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">State: 'Use Deployments for user-facing applications that scale with traffic; use DaemonSets for node-level system agents that scale with node count.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_13","level":"junior","question":"Explain ConfigMaps and Secrets. What's the difference?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Configuration","Secrets"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">ConfigMaps and Secrets decouple runtime configurations and sensitive credentials from container image builds, following Twelve-Factor App methodology.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>ConfigMaps:</strong> Store plain text configuration parameters, JSON/XML files, environment variables, or scripts.</li>\n  <li><strong>Secrets:</strong> Store sensitive credentials like database passwords, API keys, and TLS certificates. Base64 encoded by default.</li>\n  <li><strong>Mounting Strategies:</strong></li>\n  <li>• <em>Environment Variables:</em> Injected at container startup. Fast, but changes require a Pod restart to take effect.</li>\n  <li>• <em>Volume Mounts:</em> Mounted as files inside a directory. Kubernetes automatically updates mounted files when the ConfigMap/Secret changes (via atomic symlink swaps by Kubelet), enabling live reloads.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>apiVersion: v1\nkind: Pod\nmetadata:\n  name: app-runner\nspec:\n  containers:\n  - name: app\n    image: app:v1\n    envFrom:\n    - configMapRef:\n        name: app-config\n    volumeMounts:\n    - name: secret-volume\n      mountPath: /etc/secrets\n      readOnly: true\n  volumes:\n  - name: secret-volume\n    secret:\n      secretName: app-secrets</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">If you mount a ConfigMap or Secret using <code>subPath</code> to inject a single file into an existing directory, automatic live updates DO NOT WORK. Kubelet cannot track file changes mounted via subPath.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Security note for interviews: 'Always enable etcd encryption at rest for Secrets, restrict RBAC access, and integrate with external KMS providers or HashiCorp Vault in enterprise production.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_15","level":"junior","question":"How does Kubernetes handle storage orchestration?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Storage","CSI"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Kubernetes abstracts physical storage infrastructure through a decoupled three-layer system: <strong>StorageClasses</strong>, <strong>PersistentVolumes (PV)</strong>, and <strong>PersistentVolumeClaims (PVC)</strong> powered by the Container Storage Interface (CSI).</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>PersistentVolume (PV):</strong> A cluster-level storage resource provisioned by an admin or dynamically by a StorageClass. It represents actual disk storage (EBS volume, NFS share, local NVMe).</li>\n  <li><strong>PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC):</strong> A request for storage by a developer/Pod. Specifies size (e.g. 50Gi) and access mode (e.g. ReadWriteOnce).</li>\n  <li><strong>StorageClass & Dynamic Provisioning:</strong> Instead of manual PV creation, StorageClasses define a provisioner plugin (e.g., <code>ebs.csi.aws.com</code>). When a PVC is created, the CSI driver automatically provisions the underlying cloud disk and creates a matching PV.</li>\n  <li><strong>Binding:</strong> The control plane automatically binds a PVC to a suitable PV 1:1.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>apiVersion: storage.k8s.io/v1\nkind: StorageClass\nmetadata:\n  name: fast-ssd\nprovisioner: ebs.csi.aws.com\nvolumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer # Wait for pod scheduling to match AZ\nparameters:\n  type: gp3\n---\napiVersion: v1\nkind: PersistentVolumeClaim\nmetadata:\n  name: mysql-pvc\nspec:\n  accessModes: [ \"ReadWriteOnce\" ]\n  storageClassName: fast-ssd\n  resources:\n    requests:\n      storage: 50Gi</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Always set <code>volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer</code> in multi-AZ cloud environments! If set to <code>Immediate</code>, the cloud disk may be provisioned in <code>us-east-1a</code> while the scheduler decides to run the Pod in <code>us-east-1b</code>, resulting in an unattachable volume failure.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Analogy: 'StorageClass is the menu; PVC is your order; PV is the meal delivered to your table.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_16","level":"junior","question":"Explain the Kubernetes networking model.","tags":["KUBERNETES","Networking","CNI"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">The Kubernetes networking model enforces a flat, non-NAT network topology across the entire cluster, guided by three fundamental rules implemented by Container Network Interface (CNI) plugins.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>The 3 Fundamental Invariants:</strong></li>\n  <li>1. Every Pod gets its own unique, real IP address.</li>\n  <li>2. All Pods can communicate with all other Pods on any node without Network Address Translation (NAT).</li>\n  <li>3. Agents on a node (e.g. kubelet) can communicate with all Pods on that same node.</li>\n  <li><strong>Simplification:</strong> Applications do not need to manage port mapping or dynamic port allocations; every container inside a Pod shares the Pod's network namespace and IP (communicating over <code>localhost</code>).</li>\n  <li><strong>CNI Implementations:</strong> Implemented via CNI plugins such as Calico (VXLAN/BGP), Cilium (eBPF), AWS VPC CNI (native ENI secondary IPs), and Flannel.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># View installed CNI configuration on a node\nls -la /etc/cni/net.d/\n\n# Test pod-to-pod direct ping across nodes without NAT\nkubectl exec -it pod-a -- ping &lt;pod-b-ip&gt;</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Without NetworkPolicies, the default Kubernetes network is wide open: any pod in any namespace can establish connections to any other pod in the cluster.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">State the 3 rules clearly: 'Unique IP per Pod, no NAT between pods across nodes, and nodes can communicate with pods on their host.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_17","level":"junior","question":"How does Pod-to-Pod communication work within the same node?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Networking","LinuxInternals"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Communication between Pods residing on the same physical or virtual worker node is handled directly through Linux network namespaces, virtual ethernet pairs (veth), and a software bridge.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Network Namespace Isolation:</strong> Each Pod runs in its own network namespace with its own IP and routing table.</li>\n  <li><strong>Virtual Ethernet (veth) Pair:</strong> Acts like a virtual ethernet cable. One end of the pair (<code>eth0</code>) is moved inside the Pod's network namespace; the other end (<code>vethXXXX</code>) stays in the root host namespace.</li>\n  <li><strong>Linux Bridge (cbr0 / docker0):</strong> All host-end <code>veth</code> interfaces connect to a local Linux software bridge (or OVS / eBPF interface).</li>\n  <li><strong>Packet Flow:</strong> Pod A sends an ARP request for Pod B's IP. The local bridge resolves the MAC address and directly forwards the ethernet frame to Pod B's veth interface at local memory bus speeds.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># View network namespaces and interfaces on a node\nip netns list\nip link show type veth\nbrctl show # (or 'bridge link')</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Packets between pods on the same node never hit physical network cards (NICs), NAT, or wire cables; they are purely kernel memory transfers.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Keywords: 'veth pair connects pod network namespace to the host bridge; traffic travels through local bridge switching without NAT.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_18","level":"junior","question":"How does Pod-to-Pod communication work across different nodes?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Networking","Overlay"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Pod-to-Pod communication across different nodes requires routing packets across the physical underlay network. This is achieved either via <strong>Overlay Networks</strong> (encapsulation) or <strong>Direct Flat Routing</strong>.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>1. Overlay Networks (VXLAN / Geneve - Calico, Flannel):</strong></li>\n  <li>• Pod A sends a packet destined for Pod B's IP on Node 2.</li>\n  <li>• The CNI on Node 1 intercepts the packet, encapsulates the entire inner IP packet inside an outer UDP packet (port 4789).</li>\n  <li>• The outer packet travels across standard physical switches using Node 1 and Node 2 physical IPs.</li>\n  <li>• Node 2 decapsulates the UDP packet and delivers the original packet to Pod B.</li>\n  <li><strong>2. Direct Routing (BGP / AWS VPC CNI):</strong></li>\n  <li>• No encapsulation overhead. AWS VPC CNI allocates real AWS VPC subnet secondary IPs directly to Pods. VPC route tables route packets natively with zero encapsulation penalty.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Check MTU size on overlay interfaces (VXLAN typically requires MTU 1450 vs 1500)\nip link show vxlan.calico</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Overlay networks introduce CPU encapsulation overhead and reduce MTU by 50 bytes. Misconfigured MTU causes silent packet drops on large payload requests (jumbo frames).</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Distinguish: 'Overlay networks encapsulate pod packets in UDP (VXLAN) for network portability; direct routing uses BGP or cloud VPC native IPs for line-rate throughput.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_20","level":"junior","question":"How does Kubernetes handle DNS for services and pods?","tags":["KUBERNETES","DNS","CoreDNS"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Kubernetes manages internal service discovery through an in-cluster DNS service called <strong>CoreDNS</strong>. CoreDNS continuously watches the API server and maintains dynamic DNS records for Services and Pods.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Service FQDN Format:</strong> <code>&lt;service-name&gt;.&lt;namespace&gt;.svc.cluster.local</code>.</li>\n  <li>• Normal Services resolve to the virtual <code>ClusterIP</code>.</li>\n  <li>• Headless Services (<code>clusterIP: None</code>) return individual A-records for all matching Pod IPs.</li>\n  <li><strong>Container DNS Config (<code>/etc/resolv.conf</code>):</strong> Every pod automatically receives <code>nameserver 10.96.0.10</code> pointing to CoreDNS, and a search domain list: <code>search &lt;ns&gt;.svc.cluster.local svc.cluster.local cluster.local</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>The ndots:5 Gotcha:</strong> Linux glibc queries every search path sequentially for any hostname containing fewer than 5 dots, causing up to 4 failed DNS lookups for external domains like <code>api.stripe.com</code>!</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Inspect DNS resolution configuration inside a pod\nkubectl exec -it my-pod -- cat /etc/resolv.conf\n\n# Query CoreDNS using dig\nkubectl exec -it my-pod -- dig +search my-service.prod.svc.cluster.local</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">The <code>ndots:5</code> default can overwhelm CoreDNS during traffic spikes. Mitigate this by deploying <strong>NodeLocal DNSCache</strong> on every node and using trailing dots for external domains (e.g. <code>api.github.com.</code>).</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Explain: 'CoreDNS resolves Service names to ClusterIPs. In high-scale clusters, optimize DNS latency using NodeLocal DNSCache DaemonSets to eliminate cross-node UDP queries.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_21","level":"junior","question":"Describe the process of a rolling update in Kubernetes.","tags":["KUBERNETES","Deployments","Rollout"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">A rolling update updates pod instances in an orderly, incremental fashion to prevent downtime when deploying new application versions or configuration changes.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>1. <strong>Trigger:</strong> Changing the Pod template in a Deployment (e.g. image tag, env var, secret ref).</li>\n  <li>2. <strong>New ReplicaSet Creation:</strong> Deployment creates a new ReplicaSet with hash suffix.</li>\n  <li>3. <strong>Scale Up:</strong> Deployment scales the new ReplicaSet up by <code>maxSurge</code>.</li>\n  <li>4. <strong>Health Verification:</strong> Kubelet pulls the new image and starts the container. It waits for <code>readinessProbe</code> to pass.</li>\n  <li>5. <strong>Scale Down:</strong> Once new pods are Ready, the old ReplicaSet is scaled down by <code>maxUnavailable</code>.</li>\n  <li>6. <strong>Loop:</strong> The process repeats until the new ReplicaSet reaches 100% and old reaches 0%.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Update deployment image\nkubectl set image deployment/api api=myrepo/api:v2.0.0\n\n# Watch rolling update progression in real-time\nkubectl rollout status deployment/api\n\n# Pause a rollout to perform canary inspection\nkubectl rollout pause deployment/api\nkubectl rollout resume deployment/api</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Updating non-template metadata (like <code>labels</code> on the Deployment itself, not the pod template) will NOT trigger a rolling update.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Emphasize that the Deployment controller mediates between two ReplicaSets simultaneously until the transition is 100% complete.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_22","level":"junior","question":"How do you perform a rollback of a Deployment?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Deployments","DisasterRecovery"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">When a newly rolled-out application version fails, crashes, or introduces critical bugs, Kubernetes allows instant, one-command rollbacks to any previous revision.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Revision History:</strong> Every successful change to a Deployment template increments the revision number and records the underlying ReplicaSet configuration.</li>\n  <li><strong>Instant Switch:</strong> A rollback does not recreate images; it simply tells the Deployment controller to scale up the previous ReplicaSet and scale down the broken one.</li>\n  <li><strong>Commands:</strong></li>\n  <li>• <code>kubectl rollout history deployment/&lt;name&gt;</code>: View revision list.</li>\n  <li>• <code>kubectl rollout undo deployment/&lt;name&gt;</code>: Rollback to immediately preceding version.</li>\n  <li>• <code>kubectl rollout undo deployment/&lt;name&gt; --to-revision=2</code>: Rollback to specific historical revision.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># View revision history with change causes\nkubectl rollout history deployment/frontend\n\n# Undo to previous revision\nkubectl rollout undo deployment/frontend\n\n# Undo to specific revision\nkubectl rollout undo deployment/frontend --to-revision=3</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">If your deployment relies on ConfigMaps or Secrets that were modified in-place, rolling back the Deployment will NOT revert the ConfigMap! Always version your ConfigMaps (e.g. <code>app-config-v2</code>) or use Helm/Kustomize hash suffixes.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Best practice: Use <code>kubernetes.io/change-cause</code> annotations to document what each revision introduced, making rollback decisions immediate during incidents.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_24","level":"junior","question":"What is Vertical Pod Autoscaling (VPA)?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Autoscaling","ResourceManagement"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">The <strong>Vertical Pod Autoscaler (VPA)</strong> automatically adjusts the CPU and memory requests and limits for containers in a workload based on historical usage patterns, eliminating the guesswork in resource sizing.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Components:</strong></li>\n  <li>• <em>VPA Recommender:</em> Scrapes historical and current resource metrics from Metrics Server / Prometheus and computes optimal requests.</li>\n  <li>• <em>VPA Updater:</em> Evicts pods that need resource adjustments if running in Auto mode.</li>\n  <li>• <em>VPA Admission Controller:</em> Mutating webhook that intercepts pod creation and injects the recommended requests/limits.</li>\n  <li><strong>Operating Modes:</strong></li>\n  <li>• <code>Off:</code> Generates recommendations only without touching pods (safest for production analysis).</li>\n  <li>• <code>Initial:</code> Assigns recommendations only when new pods are created.</li>\n  <li>• <code>Auto:</code> Evicts running pods and restarts them with new resource allocations.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>apiVersion: autoscaling.k8s.io/v1\nkind: VerticalPodAutoscaler\nmetadata:\n  name: api-vpa\nspec:\n  targetRef:\n    apiVersion: \"apps/v1\"\n    kind: Deployment\n    name: api-server\n  updatePolicy:\n    updateMode: \"Off\" # Recommendation mode only</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">CRITICAL: Do NOT use VPA and HPA on the same resource metric (e.g. CPU or Memory)! They will fight in an infinite loop: HPA will scale out replicas while VPA reduces pod sizes, destabilizing the entire service.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Production advice: 'Run VPA in Off mode in staging to calculate precise CPU/RAM baselines, then apply those figures to production manifests with HPA.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_25","level":"junior","question":"What is Cluster Autoscaler vs Karpenter?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Autoscaling","CloudInfrastructure"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Both are node autoscaling systems for Kubernetes, but they operate on fundamentally different architectural models.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Cluster Autoscaler (CA):</strong></li>\n  <li>• Tied to cloud provider Node Groups / Auto Scaling Groups (ASGs).</li>\n  <li>• Reactive: Watches for Pods in <code>Pending</code> state due to insufficient resources, then increments the ASG desired count by 1.</li>\n  <li>• Slow: Takes 2-5 minutes to spin up nodes because it waits for cloud ASG scaling.</li>\n  <li><strong>Karpenter (Next-Gen):</strong></li>\n  <li>• Group-less: Completely bypasses cloud ASGs.</li>\n  <li>• Evaluates the exact aggregate requirements of all pending pods (CPU, RAM, architecture, spot/on-demand, AZ) and calls EC2 fleet APIs directly.</li>\n  <li>• Launches rightsized compute in under 45 seconds.</li>\n  <li>• Automatically consolidates and terminates underutilized nodes to minimize cloud spend.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Karpenter NodePool Spec Example\napiVersion: karpenter.sh/v1beta1\nkind: NodePool\nmetadata:\n  name: default\nspec:\n  template:\n    spec:\n      requirements:\n      - key: karpenter.sh/capacity-type\n        operator: In\n        values: [\"spot\", \"on-demand\"]\n      - key: kubernetes.io/arch\n        operator: In\n        values: [\"arm64\", \"amd64\"]\n  disruption:\n    consolidationPolicy: WhenUnderutilized</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Cluster Autoscaler cannot mix multiple instance types within a single ASG effectively. Karpenter supports dynamic multi-architecture (Graviton ARM + x86) instance selection natively.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">State: 'Cluster Autoscaler is the battle-tested standard tied to static ASG node groups. Karpenter is the modern high-velocity autoscaler that provisions rightsized compute directly via cloud APIs in seconds.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_1_fix_deploy","level":"junior","question":"How do you inspect and diagnose a failing Deployment using kubectl?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Troubleshooting","Deployments"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">When a Deployment fails to roll out or pods fail to become ready, structured CLI inspection allows engineers to pinpoint whether the failure is due to image issues, configuration syntax, or node capacity.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>1. <strong>Check Deployment Overview:</strong> <code>kubectl get deployments</code> shows <code>READY</code>, <code>UP-TO-DATE</code>, and <code>AVAILABLE</code> counts. If <code>READY</code> is 0/3, rollout is blocked.</li>\n  <li>2. <strong>Inspect Rollout Status:</strong> <code>kubectl rollout status deployment/&lt;name&gt;</code> prints the active progress or stall reason.</li>\n  <li>3. <strong>Check ReplicaSets:</strong> <code>kubectl get rs -l app=&lt;name&gt;</code> verifies if the new ReplicaSet was generated.</li>\n  <li>4. <strong>Inspect Pod Lifecycle:</strong> <code>kubectl get pods -l app=&lt;name&gt;</code> to check for <code>ImagePullBackOff</code> or <code>CrashLoopBackOff</code>.</li>\n  <li>5. <strong>View Events:</strong> <code>kubectl describe deployment &lt;name&gt;</code> reveals scaling events and quota rejections.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Complete diagnostic chain for stuck deployment\nkubectl get deployment my-app\nkubectl rollout status deployment/my-app --timeout=30s\nkubectl describe deployment my-app\nkubectl get pods -l app=my-app -o wide</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">A Deployment may report <code>UP-TO-DATE: 3</code> but <code>AVAILABLE: 0</code>. This means all pods were scheduled with the new spec, but NONE of them are passing readiness probes.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Follow the hierarchy: Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pod → Container Logs.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_3_fix_rs","level":"junior","question":"How do you debug ReplicaSet failures and replica drift using kubectl?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Troubleshooting","Workloads"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">When a Deployment fails to create Pods, the failure often lives at the ReplicaSet level due to quota restrictions, invalid pod security standards, or namespace admission rejections.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Replica Drift:</strong> If <code>DESIRED: 3</code> but <code>CURRENT: 0</code> in <code>kubectl get rs</code>, the ReplicaSet controller is actively being rejected by the API server when attempting to create Pod objects.</li>\n  <li><strong>Diagnostic Action:</strong> Run <code>kubectl describe rs &lt;replicaset-name&gt;</code> and inspect the <strong>Events</strong> table.</li>\n  <li><strong>Common Causes:</strong></li>\n  <li>• <code>FailedCreate: Pod \"...\" is forbidden: exceeded quota</code> → ResourceQuota reached.</li>\n  <li>• <code>FailedCreate: violates PodSecurityStandard</code> → Container requests root privileges disallowed by cluster policy.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Inspect ReplicaSets and isolated events\nkubectl get rs\nkubectl describe rs &lt;replicaset-name&gt;</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">ReplicaSet errors do NOT show up in <code>kubectl logs</code> because the Pods were never created in the first place! You must inspect ReplicaSet events.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Always check <code>describe rs</code> if your deployment creates no pods whatsoever.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_5_fix_events","level":"junior","question":"How do you interpret kubectl describe pod Events to isolate root causes?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Troubleshooting","PodLifecycle"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">The <strong>Events</strong> section at the bottom of <code>kubectl describe pod</code> is the most vital diagnostic log in Kubernetes, recording chronologically ordered events from the API server, scheduler, and node kubelet.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Event Lifecycle:</strong></li>\n  <li>• <code>Scheduled:</code> Successfully assigned to node X by default-scheduler.</li>\n  <li>• <code>SuccessfulAttachVolume:</code> Cloud disk attached to worker node.</li>\n  <li>• <code>Pulling / Pulled:</code> Image download started and finished.</li>\n  <li>• <code>Created / Started:</code> Container runtime created process.</li>\n  <li>• <code>Unhealthy:</code> Readiness or liveness probe failure with HTTP error code.</li>\n  <li>• <code>FailedScheduling:</code> No suitable node found (CPU/RAM/Taints).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># View pod events sorted chronologically\nkubectl describe pod &lt;pod-name&gt; | grep -A 20 Events:\n\n# Query specific failure events across all pods in namespace\nkubectl get events --field-selector type=Warning</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Kubernetes events are stored in etcd with a short 1-hour TTL (time to live). Historical events from yesterday will not be visible in describe output; export events to Elasticsearch/Loki for auditing.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Read events from bottom to top to identify the initial trigger of container failure.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_6_fix_logs","level":"junior","question":"How do you inspect container logs across multi-container and crashed pods?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Troubleshooting","Observability"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\"><code>kubectl logs</code> retrieves stdout and stderr streams from containers inside pods, essential for debugging application runtime crashes and unhandled exceptions.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Multi-Container Pods:</strong> When a pod has multiple containers (e.g. app + envoy sidecar), you must specify the container name: <code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; -c &lt;container&gt;</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Previous Crashed Instances (<code>-p</code>):</strong> If a container is in <code>CrashLoopBackOff</code>, running standard <code>kubectl logs</code> shows the newly restarted empty instance. Adding <code>-p</code> (or <code>--previous</code>) prints the logs of the container instance that just crashed.</li>\n  <li><strong>Tail and Stream:</strong> <code>-f</code> streams logs live; <code>--tail=100</code> limits output lines.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># View previous crashed container logs (Vital for CrashLoopBackOff)\nkubectl logs &lt;pod-name&gt; -c &lt;container-name&gt; --previous\n\n# Stream logs across all pods matching a label selector\nkubectl logs -f -l app=order-service --all-containers=true --max-log-requests=10</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">If a pod is evicted or deleted, its local container logs on the node are immediately deleted. Centralized log shipping (Fluentbit → OpenSearch/Loki) is required for production audit trails.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Remember: <code>kubectl logs -p</code> is your best friend when debugging CrashLoopBackOff.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_8_fix_apiserver","level":"junior","question":"How do you diagnose API Server and Scheduler communication failures?","tags":["KUBERNETES","ControlPlane","Troubleshooting"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">When <code>kubectl</code> commands fail or pods remain unscheduled despite healthy nodes, the issue typically stems from API server throttling, etcd network partitioning, or expired certificates.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>API Server Health Check:</strong> Query the <code>/livez</code> and <code>/readyz</code> health endpoints with verbose logging to isolate which internal controller is degraded.</li>\n  <li><strong>Control Plane Static Pod Logs:</strong> On master nodes, inspect container logs in <code>/var/log/pods/kube-system_*</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Certificate Expiry:</strong> Kubeadm installs X.509 client/server certificates that expire annually. Check validity with <code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Check detailed health endpoints\nkubectl get --raw='/readyz?verbose'\n\n# Check certificate expiration across control plane components\nkubeadm certs check-expiration\n\n# Check scheduler connectivity to API server\nkubectl logs -n kube-system -l component=kube-scheduler</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">If etcd loses quorum (e.g. 2 nodes fail in a 3-node cluster), the API server switches to read-only or shuts down, rejecting all write mutations.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">State: 'Verify API health via /readyz?verbose, check certificate validity, and confirm etcd cluster quorum.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_27","level":"junior","question":"Explain Liveness and Readiness Probes. Why are they important?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Reliability","HealthChecks"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Liveness and Readiness probes automate workload resilience by verifying application responsiveness using HTTP GET, TCP Socket, gRPC, or exec command checks.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Liveness:</strong> Reboots unrecoverable containers. Protects against infinite loops, memory leaks, and deadlocks.</li>\n  <li><strong>Readiness:</strong> Controls traffic routing. Protects against cold cache starts, warmup latency, and service overloading.</li>\n  <li><strong>Probe Mechanisms:</strong></li>\n  <li>• <code>httpGet:</code> Checks HTTP status codes ($200-399$ is success).</li>\n  <li>• <code>tcpSocket:</code> Verifies port is open and accepting TCP handshakes.</li>\n  <li>• <code>grpc:</code> Native gRPC health checking protocol (v1.24+).</li>\n  <li>• <code>exec:</code> Runs a command inside container (exit 0 is success).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>readinessProbe:\n  httpGet:\n    path: /ready\n    port: 8080\n  initialDelaySeconds: 10\n  periodSeconds: 5\n  timeoutSeconds: 2\n  failureThreshold: 3\nlivenessProbe:\n  httpGet:\n    path: /live\n    port: 8080\n  initialDelaySeconds: 30\n  periodSeconds: 15\n  failureThreshold: 3</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Setting <code>timeoutSeconds</code> too low on busy pods causes false-positive failures during transient CPU spikes, leading to restart loops.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Always tune initialDelaySeconds and timeoutSeconds conservatively to avoid premature restarts.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_29","level":"junior","question":"Explain Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) in Kubernetes.","tags":["KUBERNETES","Security","RBAC"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) regulates access to Kubernetes API resources based on the roles assigned to individual human users or automated ServiceAccounts. It operates on the principle of least privilege.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Core RBAC Primitives:</strong></li>\n  <li>• <code>Role:</code> Defines permissions within a specific namespace (e.g., read pods in 'dev').</li>\n  <li>• <code>ClusterRole:</code> Defines permissions cluster-wide (e.g., read nodes, or access across all namespaces).</li>\n  <li>• <code>RoleBinding:</code> Binds a Role (or ClusterRole) to subjects (users, groups, ServiceAccounts) within a specific namespace.</li>\n  <li>• <code>ClusterRoleBinding:</code> Binds a ClusterRole cluster-wide across all namespaces.</li>\n  <li><strong>Rules Anatomy:</strong> Each rule specifies <code>apiGroups</code>, <code>resources</code> (e.g. pods, deployments), and <code>verbs</code> (get, list, watch, create, update, delete).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1\nkind: Role\nmetadata:\n  namespace: finance\n  name: pod-reader\nrules:\n- apiGroups: [\"\"] # \"\" indicates the core API group\n  resources: [\"pods\", \"pods/log\"]\n  verbs: [\"get\", \"list\", \"watch\"]\n---\napiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1\nkind: RoleBinding\nmetadata:\n  name: read-pods-binding\n  namespace: finance\nsubjects:\n- kind: User\n  name: alice@company.com\n  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io\nroleRef:\n  kind: Role\n  name: pod-reader\n  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Be careful when granting <code>escalate</code> or <code>bind</code> verbs on roles, or <code>create</code> on pods/exec; these permissions can allow a user to escalate their privileges to cluster-admin.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Quick test command: <code>kubectl auth can-i create deployments --as alice@company.com -n finance</code> is the standard way to verify RBAC permissions.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_31","level":"mid","question":"How do you secure access to the Kubernetes API server?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Security","ControlPlane"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">The Kubernetes API server is the control plane gateway; securing it requires defense-in-depth across network exposure, authentication, authorization, and admission validation.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>1. Network Perimeter:</strong> Disable public endpoint access. Keep the API server on private subnets, accessible only through a VPN, AWS DirectConnect, or authorized bastion host with IP allowlisting.</li>\n  <li><strong>2. Authentication (AuthN):</strong> Use OIDC (OpenID Connect) integrated with corporate SSO (Okta, Azure AD, Google Workspace). Disable legacy client certificate sharing and basic auth.</li>\n  <li><strong>3. Authorization (AuthZ):</strong> Enforce strict RBAC with least privilege. Disable ABAC. Regularly audit cluster-admin role bindings.</li>\n  <li><strong>4. Admission Controllers:</strong> Enforce Validating Admission Controllers (Kyverno, OPA Gatekeeper) and Pod Security Admission to block privileged containers.</li>\n  <li><strong>5. Audit Logging:</strong> Enable comprehensive API server audit logs streamed to a SIEM for anomaly detection.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Test whether unauthenticated requests can reach API server\ncurl -k https://&lt;api-server-ip&gt;:6443/api/v1/namespaces\n# Should return 401 Unauthorized\n\n# Test authentication status\nkubectl auth whoami</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Exposing the Kubernetes API server to <code>0.0.0.0/0</code> with default credentials or weak tokens is the #1 vector for cryptojacking and cluster takeover.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Architecture response: 'Private cluster endpoint, OIDC authentication, fine-grained RBAC, Admission Controllers, and centralized audit logging.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_32","level":"mid","question":"What are Pod Security Standards (PSS) and why are they important?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Security","PSS"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Pod Security Standards (PSS) define three built-in security profiles that prevent containers from compromising the host worker node, replacing the deprecated PodSecurityPolicies (PSP).</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>The Three Security Levels:</strong></li>\n  <li>1. <strong>Privileged:</strong> Unrestricted. Containers can run as root, share host PID/IPC/Network, and mount host filesystems. Used only for CNI and storage daemons.</li>\n  <li>2. <strong>Baseline:</strong> Minimally restrictive. Prevents known privilege escalations while allowing standard defaults (e.g. blocks hostNetwork, hostPath, privileged mode).</li>\n  <li>3. <strong>Restricted:</strong> Heavily hardened. Enforces rootless execution (<code>runAsNonRoot: true</code>), drops all Linux capabilities except <code>NET_BIND_SERVICE</code>, and disallows privilege escalation.</li>\n  <li><strong>Enforcement Modes:</strong> Applied via namespace labels: <code>enforce</code> (blocks pod), <code>audit</code> (logs violation), and <code>warn</code> (alerts user upon submission).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>apiVersion: v1\nkind: Namespace\nmetadata:\n  name: secure-apps\n  labels:\n    pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce: restricted\n    pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce-version: latest\n    pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn: restricted\n    pod-security.kubernetes.io/audit: restricted</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Applying <code>enforce: restricted</code> to an existing namespace containing legacy workloads running as root will prevent those pods from restarting if rescheduled.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Best practice: Start by applying <code>warn</code> and <code>audit</code> modes to baseline production namespaces, remediate developer manifests, then switch to <code>enforce</code>.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_34","level":"mid","question":"How do you collect logs from applications running in Kubernetes?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Observability","Logging"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Applications in Kubernetes should stream logs to <code>stdout</code> and <code>stderr</code>. The container runtime writes these to files in <code>/var/log/pods/</code> on the host node, where they are harvested by logging collectors.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>1. Node-Level Logging Agent (Recommended Standard):</strong> A DaemonSet (FluentBit, Vector, Promtail) runs on every node, tailing <code>/var/log/pods/*.log</code>, enriching lines with K8s metadata (pod name, namespace, labels), and forwarding to OpenSearch, Loki, or Datadog.</li>\n  <li><strong>2. Sidecar Container:</strong> A dedicated container inside the Pod streams or processes log files written to a shared <code>emptyDir</code> volume. Used for legacy apps that only write to disk files.</li>\n  <li><strong>3. Direct Application Export:</strong> Application code streams logs directly to external logging APIs via network SDKs (not cloud-native, tightly couples app to logging system).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Fluent Bit DaemonSet snippet mounting node log directory\nvolumeMounts:\n- name: varlogpods\n  mountPath: /var/log/pods\n  readOnly: true\nvolumes:\n- name: varlogpods\n  hostPath:\n    path: /var/log/pods</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Uncontrolled verbose logging (DEBUG mode in production) can fill the worker node's root filesystem, causing Kubelet to enter <code>DiskPressure</code> and evict pods.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Standard SRE answer: 'Standardize on stdout/stderr, collect with a lightweight DaemonSet like Fluent Bit or Vector, and forward to a centralized log lake like Loki or OpenSearch.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_35","level":"mid","question":"What is the role of Prometheus in Kubernetes monitoring?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Observability","Prometheus"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Prometheus is the de facto cloud-native monitoring and alerting standard in Kubernetes, utilizing a pull-based metrics model and multidimensional time-series database.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Pull-Based Scraping:</strong> Prometheus periodically scrapes HTTP endpoints (<code>/metrics</code>) exposed by cluster targets.</li>\n  <li><strong>Prometheus Operator & ServiceMonitors:</strong> The Prometheus Operator introduces <code>ServiceMonitor</code> and <code>PodMonitor</code> CRDs, allowing declarative configuration of scrape targets via label selectors.</li>\n  <li><strong>Core Metric Sources:</strong></li>\n  <li>• <code>cAdvisor:</code> Embedded inside Kubelet; provides container-level CPU, memory, network, and disk metrics.</li>\n  <li>• <code>kube-state-metrics (KSM):</code> Generates metrics about object states (e.g. deployment replica counts, pod phases, PVC status).</li>\n  <li>• <code>node-exporter:</code> Provides Linux host metrics (disk I/O, CPU, load average).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>apiVersion: monitoring.coreos.com/v1\nkind: ServiceMonitor\nmetadata:\n  name: api-monitor\n  namespace: production\nspec:\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: core-api\n  endpoints:\n  - port: metrics\n    interval: 15s\n    path: /metrics</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">High Cardinality Alert: Including dynamic variables like user IDs or email addresses in Prometheus metric labels causes memory explosion in the Prometheus TSDB.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Explain: 'Prometheus pulls time-series data, cAdvisor tracks container resource consumption, and kube-state-metrics tracks cluster object lifecycle states.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_36","level":"mid","question":"Describe a scenario where you would use a Custom Resource Definition (CRD) and Operator.","tags":["KUBERNETES","Architecture","Operators"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">A <strong>Custom Resource Definition (CRD)</strong> extends the Kubernetes API by registering new object kinds. An <strong>Operator</strong> combines a CRD with a custom controller that encodes human domain knowledge to automate complex application lifecycles.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Scenario: Clustered PostgreSQL Database:</strong></li>\n  <li>• Without an Operator, managing a primary-replica PostgreSQL cluster requires manual failover scripts, manual WAL archiving, and manual backup restores.</li>\n  <li>• With the <strong>CloudNativePG</strong> or <strong>Zalando Postgres Operator</strong>, you declare a single YAML manifest of kind <code>Cluster</code>.</li>\n  <li>• The Operator automatically provisions StatefulSets, sets up streaming replication, rotates credentials, initiates automated S3 backups, and triggers automated sub-30-second failovers if the primary node crashes.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>apiVersion: postgresql.cnpg.io/v1\nkind: Cluster\nmetadata:\n  name: prod-postgres\nspec:\n  instances: 3\n  storage:\n    size: 100Gi\n    storageClass: gp3\n  backup:\n    barmanObjectStore:\n      destinationPath: \"s3://company-db-backups/\"</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Do not write an Operator for simple stateless web services; Deployments, HPA, and standard Helm charts already handle their full lifecycle.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Definition: 'An Operator is software that packages operational human knowledge (backups, failovers, upgrades) into code running in a Kubernetes control loop.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_37","level":"mid","question":"Explain the concept of Taints and Tolerations in detail.","tags":["KUBERNETES","Scheduling","Core"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Taints and Tolerations repel pods from nodes, ensuring dedicated compute allocation (e.g. GPU-only nodes, master control plane nodes, or nodes undergoing maintenance).</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Taint:</strong> Applied to a Node. Has three fields: <code>Key</code>, <code>Value</code>, and <code>Effect</code>.</li>\n  <li>• <code>NoSchedule:</code> New pods without matching toleration are not placed on the node.</li>\n  <li>• <code>PreferNoSchedule:</code> Scheduler tries to avoid placing pods without matching toleration.</li>\n  <li>• <code>NoExecute:</code> Existing pods without matching toleration are immediately evicted.</li>\n  <li><strong>Toleration:</strong> Placed in a Pod's <code>spec.tolerations</code> to match the taint using either <code>Equal</code> or <code>Exists</code> operator.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>tolerations:\n- key: \"node.kubernetes.io/unreachable\"\n  operator: \"Exists\"\n  effect: \"NoExecute\"\n  tolerationSeconds: 300 # Stay 5 minutes before eviction on network disconnect</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Kubernetes automatically taints nodes with <code>node.kubernetes.io/not-ready:NoExecute</code> when node heartbeats fail. The default pod toleration keeps pods alive for 300s before failover.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">State: 'Taints repel pods; tolerations permit pods to overcome the repulsion. Combine with NodeAffinity to enforce strict placement.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_38","level":"mid","question":"What are Node Affinity and Anti-Affinity?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Scheduling","Affinity"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\"><strong>Node Affinity</strong> allows you to constrain which nodes your Pod is eligible to be scheduled on based on node labels. It is an expressive evolution of the older <code>nodeSelector</code>.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Two Scheduling Rules:</strong></li>\n  <li>• <code>requiredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:</code> Hard requirement. If no node matches the label criteria, the Pod stays in <code>Pending</code>.</li>\n  <li>• <code>preferredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:</code> Soft preference with weights (1-100). The scheduler tries to place the pod there, but falls back to other nodes if needed.</li>\n  <li><strong>Operators:</strong> Supports rich logic: <code>In</code>, <code>NotIn</code>, <code>Exists</code>, <code>DoesNotExist</code>, <code>Gt</code>, <code>Lt</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>affinity:\n  nodeAffinity:\n    requiredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:\n      nodeSelectorTerms:\n      - matchExpressions:\n        - key: topology.kubernetes.io/zone\n          operator: In\n          values: [\"us-east-1a\", \"us-east-1b\"]\n    preferredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:\n    - weight: 80\n      preference:\n        matchExpressions:\n        - key: instance-type\n          operator: In\n          values: [\"compute-optimized\"]</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\"><code>IgnoredDuringExecution</code> means if node labels change while the Pod is already running, Kubernetes will NOT evict the pod.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Contrast: 'Taints repel; NodeAffinity attracts.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_1_fix_sched_events","level":"junior","question":"How do you troubleshoot Pod scheduling failures via describe pod Events?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Troubleshooting","Scheduling"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">When a Pod fails to schedule, <code>kubectl describe pod</code> provides exact diagnostic reasons from the kube-scheduler filtering predicates.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Predicate Failure Messages:</strong></li>\n  <li>• <code>0/N nodes available: Insufficient memory/cpu</code> → All nodes have exhausted their allocatable capacity.</li>\n  <li>• <code>0/N nodes available: node(s) had untolerated taint</code> → Dedicated or master nodes repel the pod.</li>\n  <li>• <code>0/N nodes available: node(s) didn't match PodTopologySpread</code> → Violates zone spread constraints.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Filter specifically for scheduling failure events\nkubectl describe pod &lt;pod-name&gt; | grep -E \"(FailedScheduling|Warning)\"</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Remember that the scheduler allocates based on <strong>requests</strong>, not actual live usage. A node with 5% live CPU can still reject pods if total requests equal 100%.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">State: 'Check describe pod events first to immediately identify which scheduling predicate failed.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_2_fix_field_sel","level":"junior","question":"How do you query and filter Kubernetes events using field selectors?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Troubleshooting","CLI"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\"><code>kubectl get events</code> supports <code>--field-selector</code> to isolate specific operational errors across namespaces without noise.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Targeted Queries:</strong></li>\n  <li>• Filter by object: <code>involvedObject.name=&lt;pod-name&gt;</code>.</li>\n  <li>• Filter by type: <code>type=Warning</code>.</li>\n  <li>• Filter by reason: <code>reason=FailedCreate</code> or <code>reason=BackOff</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Query all warning events across all namespaces\nkubectl get events -A --field-selector type=Warning --sort-by='.lastTimestamp'\n\n# Query events specific to a pod\nkubectl get events --field-selector involvedObject.name=my-app-pod</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Events are ephemeral (TTL 1 hour). Always ship events to persistent storage for historical forensics.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Use <code>--sort-by='.lastTimestamp'</code> to view events in exact chronological sequence.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_3_fix_sched_logs","level":"junior","question":"How do you inspect Kube-Scheduler logs to diagnose placement rejections?","tags":["KUBERNETES","ControlPlane","Troubleshooting"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">When complex affinity, taints, or topology constraints fail silently, inspecting the <code>kube-scheduler</code> control plane logs reveals the exact scoring and filtering decisions.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>In managed Kubernetes (EKS/GKE), enable Control Plane Logging for the scheduler.</li>\n  <li>In self-managed clusters, check the static pod logs in <code>kube-system</code>.</li>\n  <li>Increase verbosity (<code>--v=4</code> or <code>--v=5</code>) to inspect each node's evaluation score.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># View scheduler logs\nkubectl logs -n kube-system -l component=kube-scheduler --tail=100</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Running scheduler logs at high verbosity (<code>--v=6</code>) in large clusters generates gigabytes of logs per minute.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Use scheduler logs when multiple custom scheduling profiles are configured.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_40","level":"mid","question":"How would you troubleshoot an application that is unreachable from outside the cluster?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Troubleshooting","Networking"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">When an external request fails to reach a pod, troubleshoot methodically from outside in: DNS → Cloud Load Balancer → Ingress Controller → Service / EndpointSlices → Pod.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Step 1: Ingress & DNS:</strong> Check external DNS resolves to Load Balancer IP. Verify Ingress rules match host and path.</li>\n  <li><strong>Step 2: Ingress Controller Health:</strong> Check ingress controller logs for upstream 502/503 errors.</li>\n  <li><strong>Step 3: Service & Endpoints:</strong> Run <code>kubectl get endpoints &lt;svc&gt;</code>. If empty, the Service selector does not match Pod labels or pods failed readiness probes.</li>\n  <li><strong>Step 4: Pod Connectivity:</strong> Exec into pod and verify application is listening on <code>targetPort</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Step 5: NetworkPolicies:</strong> Verify no CNI NetworkPolicy blocks ingress traffic.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Step-by-step diagnostic chain\nkubectl get ingress\nkubectl describe ingress &lt;ingress-name&gt;\nkubectl get endpoints &lt;service-name&gt;\nkubectl get pods -l app=&lt;selector&gt; -o wide</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">A 502 Bad Gateway from NGINX Ingress almost always means the Service has 0 healthy Endpoints (pods failed readiness probes).</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Walk through the request lifecycle: Client → DNS → ELB → Ingress → Service (Endpoints) → Pod.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_2_fix_ep","level":"junior","question":"How do you diagnose empty Service Endpoints (Endpoints: <none>)?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Networking","Troubleshooting"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">If <code>kubectl describe svc</code> reports <code>Endpoints: &lt;none&gt;</code>, the Service cannot forward traffic to any Pods.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Cause 1: Label Selector Mismatch:</strong> The Service's <code>spec.selector</code> does not match the Pods' <code>metadata.labels</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Cause 2: Readiness Probe Failure:</strong> Pods exist and are running, but their readiness probe is failing, so Kubelet removed them from the Endpoint list.</li>\n  <li><strong>Cause 3: Pods Crashed:</strong> All pods are in CrashLoopBackOff or Terminating.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Compare Service selector with Pod labels\nkubectl get svc &lt;service-name&gt; -o jsonpath='{.spec.selector}'\nkubectl get pods --show-labels</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">A Pod must be in <code>Running</code> phase AND pass its readiness probe to be added to Endpoints.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Remember: No endpoints means either label mismatch or failing readiness probes.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_3_fix_selector","level":"junior","question":"How do you verify and debug Pod label and Service selector mismatches?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Networking","CLI"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">A typo in a Service selector silently breaks routing without throwing any API server errors.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>The API server does not validate whether a Service selector matches any existing pods.</li>\n  <li>Use <code>kubectl get pods -l &lt;selector&gt;</code> passing the exact selector from the service manifest.</li>\n  <li>If 0 pods return, verify label spellings, casing, and environment tags.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Query pods using the exact selector defined on the service\nSELECTOR=$(kubectl get svc my-service -o jsonpath='{.spec.selector}' | sed 's/[{}]//g; s/\":\"/=/g; s/\",\"/,/g; s/\"//g')\nkubectl get pods -l \"$SELECTOR\"</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Labels are case-sensitive! <code>App=web</code> will not match <code>app=web</code>.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Always query <code>kubectl get pods -l &lt;selector&gt;</code> when creating a new Service.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_4_fix_ing_rules","level":"junior","question":"How do you debug Ingress resource routing rules and TLS termination?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Ingress","TLS"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Ingress routing bugs typically stem from pathType mismatch, ingressClassName omission, or invalid TLS Secret certificates.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>pathType:</strong> <code>Exact</code> matches exact path; <code>Prefix</code> matches prefix. Ensure pathType matches application routing.</li>\n  <li><strong>ingressClassName:</strong> In K8s 1.18+, specify <code>ingressClassName: nginx</code> instead of legacy annotations.</li>\n  <li><strong>TLS Verification:</strong> Check that the referenced Secret contains <code>tls.crt</code> and <code>tls.key</code> matching the requested host.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Inspect Ingress rules and TLS secret\nkubectl describe ingress &lt;ingress-name&gt;\nkubectl get secret &lt;tls-secret-name&gt; -o yaml</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">If the TLS Secret hostname does not match the Ingress <code>rules.host</code>, browsers will throw <code>SSL_ERROR_BAD_CERT_DOMAIN</code>.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Check describe ingress: verify Rules, Backends, and TLS sections are populated.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_5_fix_ing_ctrl","level":"junior","question":"How do you troubleshoot Ingress Controller crashes and upstream 502 errors?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Ingress","Troubleshooting"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">An Ingress Controller (e.g. ingress-nginx) acts as the reverse proxy gateway; inspecting its logs isolates backend communication errors.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>502 Bad Gateway:</strong> Upstream pod connection refused or pod crashed while serving request.</li>\n  <li><strong>504 Gateway Timeout:</strong> Upstream pod took longer than <code>proxy-read-timeout</code> (default 60s) to reply.</li>\n  <li><strong>Config Reload Failures:</strong> Check ingress controller logs for invalid Lua or NGINX syntax caused by bad annotations.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># View ingress controller error logs\nkubectl logs -n ingress-nginx -l app.kubernetes.io/name=ingress-nginx --tail=200 | grep -E \"(502|504|error)\"</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">A single misconfigured Ingress object with conflicting annotations can break configuration reloads across the entire controller.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Examine ingress controller logs; they provide immediate visibility into upstream HTTP error codes.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_6_fix_firewall","level":"junior","question":"How do you configure Security Groups and Firewalls for NodePort and LoadBalancer Services?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Networking","Security"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Exposing Kubernetes services to external traffic requires matching cloud network security groups with node port ranges.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>NodePort Range:</strong> By default <code>30000-32767</code>. Cloud firewall rules must permit ingress traffic from the Load Balancer to worker nodes on this range.</li>\n  <li><strong>Cloud Security Group Ingress:</strong> LoadBalancer controllers (AWS ALB / NLB) automatically inject rules, but manual security groups must permit traffic.</li>\n  <li><strong>Health Check Port:</strong> AWS NLB/ALB requires traffic access to node health check ports (e.g. 10256 for kube-proxy).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Verify kube-proxy healthz port is reachable\ncurl -I http://&lt;node-ip&gt;:10256/healthz</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Restricting cloud security groups to specific worker nodes can break if the cluster autoscaler spins up new nodes with different subnet tags.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Ensure security groups permit both the application port and the health check port.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_7_fix_dns_res","level":"junior","question":"How do you verify external DNS resolution for Kubernetes Ingress endpoints?","tags":["KUBERNETES","DNS","Ingress"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">External DNS records (Route53, Cloudflare) must resolve the domain to the cloud Load Balancer CNAME or public IP.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>Use <code>dig</code> or <code>nslookup</code> to verify public DNS propagation.</li>\n  <li>Integrate <strong>ExternalDNS</strong> controller in Kubernetes to automatically create DNS records when Ingress objects are deployed.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Verify DNS lookup against authoritative nameserver\ndig +trace api.mycompany.com\ndig @8.8.8.8 api.mycompany.com</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">DNS TTL caching can lead developers to believe their Ingress is broken when it is simply an unexpired local cache.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Use ExternalDNS to automate DNS record synchronization with cloud providers.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_8_fix_kubeproxy","level":"junior","question":"How do you debug kube-proxy synchronization and iptables/IPVS rule propagation?","tags":["KUBERNETES","KubeProxy","Networking"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">When a service is reachable on some nodes but unreachable on others, <code>kube-proxy</code> has likely failed to sync iptables or IPVS rules.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>Kube-proxy runs as a DaemonSet; check its pod logs on the affected worker node.</li>\n  <li>Verify sync errors or conntrack table exhaustion.</li>\n  <li>Inspect node iptables chains directly.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Check kube-proxy pod logs on specific node\nkubectl logs -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-proxy --tail=100 | grep -i error\n\n# Check conntrack table usage on node\nsudo conntrack -C</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">If the node conntrack table fills up, kube-proxy cannot establish new connections, dropping random packets.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Inspect kube-proxy sync period and check for conntrack table limits on busy nodes.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_42","level":"mid","question":"How does Kubernetes handle self-healing?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Architecture","SelfHealing"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Self-healing is a foundational design principle of Kubernetes, implemented through automated reconciliation loops operating at multiple infrastructure layers.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Container Layer:</strong> Kubelet restarts containers that exit with non-zero codes according to their <code>restartPolicy</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Pod Layer:</strong> Liveness probes detect stalled or deadlocked containers and trigger automatic restarts.</li>\n  <li><strong>Node Layer:</strong> If a worker node becomes unresponsive (fails heartbeats for > 5 minutes), the Node Controller evicts its pods and schedules replacements on healthy nodes.</li>\n  <li><strong>Replica Layer:</strong> ReplicaSets ensure that the exact desired number of Pods remain running at all times.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Simulate node failure by shutting down kubelet\nsudo systemctl stop kubelet\n# Watch node transition to NotReady and pod eviction after grace period</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Pods with attached cloud storage (EBS) cannot be rescheduled on a new node until the volume is successfully detached from the failed node, which may take minutes in cloud environments.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Self-healing operates at container, pod, replica, and node layers through continuous control loops.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_44","level":"mid","question":"Explain how kubectl exec works internally.","tags":["KUBERNETES","ControlPlane","Internals"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\"><code>kubectl exec</code> establishes a bidirectional interactive terminal session into a running container through a multi-hop streaming connection.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>1. <strong>Client to API Server:</strong> <code>kubectl</code> sends an HTTP POST to <code>/api/v1/namespaces/{ns}/pods/{name}/exec</code>, upgrading the connection to SPDY or WebSockets.</li>\n  <li>2. <strong>API Server to Kubelet:</strong> The API server authenticates and authorizes the request (RBAC: <code>pods/exec</code>), then opens a streaming connection to the Kubelet on port 10250.</li>\n  <li>3. <strong>Kubelet to Container Runtime:</strong> Kubelet calls the Container Runtime Interface (CRI) <code>Exec</code> method (containerd/CRI-O).</li>\n  <li>4. <strong>Runtime Process Creation:</strong> The runtime forks the process inside the container's Linux namespaces and pipes <code>stdin</code>, <code>stdout</code>, and <code>stderr</code> back through the stream.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Interactive terminal into pod\nkubectl exec -it my-pod -- /bin/sh\n\n# Non-interactive command execution\nkubectl exec my-pod -- ps aux</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">In high-security environments, restrict RBAC access to <code>pods/exec</code>; it grants direct interactive shell access into the container environment.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Follow the path: kubectl (SPDY/WebSocket) → API Server → Kubelet (port 10250) → Container Runtime (CRI).</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_45","level":"mid","question":"What is GitOps and how does it relate to Kubernetes?","tags":["KUBERNETES","GitOps","ArgoCD"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\"><strong>GitOps</strong> is an operational framework where Git repositories act as the single source of truth for declared infrastructure and application state in Kubernetes.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Pull vs Push Model:</strong> Instead of external CI pipelines (Jenkins) pushing changes into the cluster via <code>kubectl apply</code>, a pull-based GitOps agent (<strong>ArgoCD</strong> or <strong>Flux</strong>) runs <em>inside</em> the cluster.</li>\n  <li><strong>Drift Detection:</strong> The GitOps operator continuously compares the desired state in Git with the live cluster state. If drift occurs (e.g. an engineer manually edits a deployment), it alerts or automatically reconciles back to Git.</li>\n  <li><strong>Security Benefit:</strong> No external CI system requires admin credentials to the Kubernetes API server; the cluster pulls configurations internally.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1\nkind: Application\nmetadata:\n  name: order-service\n  namespace: argocd\nspec:\n  project: default\n  source:\n    repoURL: https://github.com/company/k8s-manifests.git\n    targetRevision: HEAD\n    path: apps/order-service\n  destination:\n    server: https://kubernetes.default.svc\n    namespace: prod\n  syncPolicy:\n    automated:\n      prune: true\n      selfHeal: true</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Enable <code>selfHeal: true</code> with care; if a rogue script commits bad configs to Git, ArgoCD will immediately replicate the breakage across all clusters.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">GitOps core pillars: Declarative specifications, Version-controlled single source of truth, Automated pulls, and Continuous reconciliation.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_46","level":"mid","question":"Scenario: Your application's Pods are constantly restarting (CrashLoopBackOff). How do you troubleshoot?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Troubleshooting","CrashLoopBackOff"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\"><code>CrashLoopBackOff</code> means a container repeatedly starts, fails/crashes, and Kubernetes waits with exponential backoff before restarting it again.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Step 1: Check Exit Code:</strong> Run <code>kubectl describe pod &lt;name&gt;</code> and check <code>lastState.terminated.exitCode</code>.</li>\n  <li>• <code>Exit Code 0:</code> Application finished its job (e.g. run-once script misconfigured as a Deployment).</li>\n  <li>• <code>Exit Code 1:</code> Application runtime error or unhandled exception.</li>\n  <li>• <code>Exit Code 137:</code> OOMKilled (Out of Memory).</li>\n  <li>• <code>Exit Code 143:</code> SIGTERM graceful shutdown.</li>\n  <li><strong>Step 2: Inspect Previous Logs:</strong> Run <code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; -p</code> to see why the previous container crashed.</li>\n  <li><strong>Step 3: Check Environment & Secrets:</strong> Missing ConfigMaps or Secrets frequently cause instant startup crashes.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># 1. View previous crashed container logs\nkubectl logs &lt;pod-name&gt; --previous\n\n# 2. Inspect exit code and termination reason\nkubectl get pod &lt;pod-name&gt; -o jsonpath='{.status.containerStatuses[*].lastState.terminated}'</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">If <code>kubectl logs -p</code> returns empty, the container is crashing before stdout buffers flush, or the entrypoint command binary does not exist in the image.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Follow the 3-step triage: Exit code → Previous logs (`-p`) → Environment/secret references.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_1_fix_restarts","level":"junior","question":"How do you analyze Pod restart counts and exit codes to classify failures?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Troubleshooting","PodLifecycle"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">The <code>RESTARTS</code> column in <code>kubectl get pods</code> indicates container instability. Correlating restarts with container exit codes isolates root causes quickly.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>• <code>Exit Code 137:</code> Process received SIGKILL ($128+9$), typically OOMKilled.</li>\n  <li>• <code>Exit Code 139:</code> Segmentation fault ($128+11$), binary memory corruption.</li>\n  <li>• <code>Exit Code 143:</code> Process received SIGTERM ($128+15$), killed by Kubernetes due to failed liveness probe or scaling down.</li>\n  <li>• <code>Exit Code 1:</code> Application fatal exception.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Query pods with highest restart counts across cluster\nkubectl get pods -A --sort-by='.status.containerStatuses[0].restartCount'</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Frequent restarts reset CPU and memory metrics in Prometheus, making performance profiling difficult during restart loops.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Memorize: 137 = OOMKill / SIGKILL; 143 = Graceful SIGTERM; 1 = App error.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_3_fix_app_logs","level":"junior","question":"How do you perform application log analysis for crashing containers?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Observability","Troubleshooting"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">When a container crashes immediately upon boot, standard live log streaming fails; specific flags must be used to capture startup logs.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>Use <code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous</code> to read the stdout/stderr from the immediately prior instance.</li>\n  <li>If the container is crash-looping too fast, add a temporary debug sleep entrypoint in the pod spec (<code>command: ['sleep', '3600']</code>) to inspect the filesystem interactively.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Stream logs with timestamps\nkubectl logs &lt;pod-name&gt; -p --timestamps</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Remember that previous logs are only preserved for 1 restart cycle. If it restarts twice, the oldest crash log is overwritten.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Use <code>--timestamps</code> to correlate application crash logs with external API failures.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_4_fix_prev_logs","level":"junior","question":"How do you inspect previous container crash logs using kubectl logs --previous?","tags":["KUBERNETES","CLI","Troubleshooting"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">The <code>--previous</code> (or <code>-p</code>) flag retrieves logs from the previous instance of the container if it has been restarted.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>When a container fails, the Kubelet archives its log file locally on the node under <code>/var/log/pods/</code> before launching the new instance.</li>\n  <li><code>kubectl logs -p</code> requests this archived log file from Kubelet.</li>\n  <li>Essential for debugging panic stack traces and startup initialization exceptions.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>kubectl logs my-app-7b6c89-x9kz4 -c backend -p</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">If the pod was deleted and recreated (e.g. by a Deployment controller), <code>--previous</code> will return an error because it's an entirely new pod.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Always use <code>-p</code> first whenever you see CrashLoopBackOff.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_5_fix_res_limits","level":"junior","question":"How do you diagnose resource limit starvation and out-of-memory crashes?","tags":["KUBERNETES","ResourceManagement","Troubleshooting"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Resource starvation occurs when containers are constrained by overly restrictive memory limits or CPU throttling.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>Inspect <code>lastState.terminated.reason</code> in <code>describe pod</code> for <code>OOMKilled</code>.</li>\n  <li>Check Linux CFS bandwidth quota throttling metrics in Prometheus: <code>container_cpu_cfs_throttled_periods_total</code>.</li>\n  <li>Adjust <code>limits.memory</code> to accommodate peak memory allocations.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Query pods killed by OOM\nkubectl get pods -A -o json | jq -r '.items[] | select(.status.containerStatuses[].lastState.terminated.reason==\"OOMKilled\") | .metadata.name'</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">CPU limits do not crash containers (they throttle), whereas memory limits crash containers immediately (OOMKill).</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Use jq to filter OOMKilled pods across the cluster in seconds.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_6_fix_code_bugs","level":"junior","question":"How do you debug application configuration errors and startup exceptions in Pods?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Troubleshooting","Containers"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Startup configuration errors (missing database connection strings, unparsed environment variables, permission errors) cause immediate crash loops.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>Verify ConfigMap and Secret key names match the container's expected environment variable keys.</li>\n  <li>Use <code>kubectl exec</code> on a running healthy instance to inspect environment variables: <code>printenv</code>.</li>\n  <li>Verify network reachability to external databases from inside the pod.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Verify environment variables inside container\nkubectl exec &lt;pod-name&gt; -- printenv | grep DB_</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Referencing a non-existent ConfigMap key in <code>valueFrom.configMapKeyRef</code> prevents the Pod from even starting (status: <code>CreateContainerConfigError</code>).</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Check for <code>CreateContainerConfigError</code> in events to identify missing secrets or configmaps.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_48","level":"mid","question":"Scenario: A new application deployment is failing, and the Pods are stuck in ImagePullBackOff. How do you troubleshoot?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Troubleshooting","Images"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\"><code>ImagePullBackOff</code> indicates that Kubelet cannot pull the specified container image from the container registry.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Step 1: Check Describe Events:</strong> <code>kubectl describe pod &lt;pod&gt;</code> prints the exact error from Kubelet.</li>\n  <li><strong>Common Causes:</strong></li>\n  <li>• <code>repository does not exist or may require 'docker login'</code> → Private registry authentication missing; needs <code>imagePullSecrets</code>.</li>\n  <li>• <code>manifest for tag not found</code> → Image tag misspelled or was not pushed to registry.</li>\n  <li>• <code>net/http: TLS handshake timeout</code> → Node cannot route to the internet/ECR/ACR (missing NAT gateway or firewall).</li>\n  <li>• <code>toomanyrequests: You have reached your pull rate limit</code> → Docker Hub rate limit exceeded.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Check exact pull error\nkubectl describe pod &lt;pod-name&gt; | grep -A 5 \"Failed to pull image\"\n\n# Create imagePullSecret for private Docker registry\nkubectl create secret docker-registry regcred   --docker-server=https://index.docker.io/v1/   --docker-username=myuser   --docker-password=mypass   --docker-email=my@email.com</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">In AWS EKS, worker nodes require the <code>AmazonEC2ContainerRegistryReadOnly</code> IAM policy attached to their node role to pull from private ECR without explicit imagePullSecrets.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Check 3 things: 1. Image tag spelling. 2. Registry authentication (imagePullSecrets / IAM role). 3. Node outbound internet/NAT gateway connectivity.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_49","level":"mid","question":"Scenario: You have a database running as a StatefulSet, and you need to scale storage without data loss.","tags":["KUBERNETES","StatefulSet","Storage"],"source":"K8s Production Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Scaling storage for StatefulSets requires resizing the underlying PersistentVolumeClaims (PVCs) while preserving ordinal data mappings.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Prerequisite:</strong> The StorageClass must have <code>allowVolumeExpansion: true</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Step 1: Expand PVCs directly:</strong> Update the <code>spec.resources.requests.storage</code> in each individual PVC (<code>data-db-0</code>, <code>data-db-1</code>).</li>\n  <li><strong>Step 2: Volume Expansion:</strong> The CSI driver expands the cloud block storage (e.g. AWS EBS).</li>\n  <li><strong>Step 3: Filesystem Resize:</strong> When the Pod restarts (or online if supported), the Kubelet expands the filesystem (ext4/xfs).</li>\n  <li><strong>Step 4: Update StatefulSet Manifest:</strong> Update the StatefulSet definition to match future replicas.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># 1. Verify StorageClass supports volume expansion\nkubectl get sc gp3 -o jsonpath='{.allowVolumeExpansion}' # must be true\n\n# 2. Resize individual PVC\nkubectl patch pvc data-db-0 -p '{\"spec\":{\"resources\":{\"requests\":{\"storage\":\"200Gi\"}}}}'\n\n# 3. Watch PVC status transition to FileSystemResizePending -&gt; Bound\nkubectl get pvc data-db-0 -w</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Cloud providers (AWS EBS) do NOT support shrinking disks! You can only increase storage size, never decrease it.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Always ensure <code>allowVolumeExpansion: true</code> is enabled on StorageClasses before deploying production databases.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_50","level":"mid","question":"Scenario: You want to ensure that a critical application always has at least 3 replicas running during node upgrades.","tags":["KUBERNETES","Reliability","PDB"],"source":"K8s Production Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">To guarantee high availability during voluntary disruptions (e.g. AMI rolling updates, node drains), deploy a <strong>PodDisruptionBudget (PDB)</strong>.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>A PDB with <code>minAvailable: 3</code> ensures the Eviction API rejects any eviction attempt that would drop available replicas below 3.</li>\n  <li>The cluster upgrade automation drains one node at a time, waiting for replacement pods to schedule and become Ready on other nodes before proceeding.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>apiVersion: policy/v1\nkind: PodDisruptionBudget\nmetadata:\n  name: critical-api-pdb\nspec:\n  minAvailable: 3\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: critical-api</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">If your deployment only has 3 replicas and one dies involuntarily, <code>minAvailable: 3</code> will block node drains permanently. Set total replicas to at least 4 or 5.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Always pair PDBs with adequate replica counts (Total Replicas > minAvailable).</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_51","level":"mid","question":"Scenario: You notice high CPU utilization on one of your worker nodes. How do you troubleshoot?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Troubleshooting","Performance"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">High node CPU utilization threatens node stability and causes noisy-neighbor performance degradation. Troubleshoot by isolating which Pod, process, or kernel thread is consuming cycles.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>1. Identify the stressed node: <code>kubectl top nodes</code>.</li>\n  <li>2. Identify the offending Pod: <code>kubectl top pods -A --sort-by='cpu'</code>.</li>\n  <li>3. Profile inside the container: <code>kubectl exec -it &lt;pod&gt; -- top</code>.</li>\n  <li>4. Check for runaway threads, infinite loops, or unthrottled background tasks.</li>\n  <li>5. Remediate: Enforce CPU limits, scale out with HPA, or drain the node.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># 1. Identify high-CPU nodes\nkubectl top nodes --sort-by='cpu'\n\n# 2. List top CPU-consuming pods across cluster\nkubectl top pods -A --sort-by='cpu' | head -n 10</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">A container without CPU limits can consume 100% of the entire node's CPU, starving system daemons (kubelet, containerd) and causing the node to become <code>NotReady</code>.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Always enforce LimitRanges with default CPU limits in multi-tenant namespaces to prevent runaway processes from consuming entire nodes.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_1_fix_top_nodes","level":"junior","question":"How do you isolate high-CPU worker nodes using kubectl top nodes?","tags":["KUBERNETES","CLI","Monitoring"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\"><code>kubectl top nodes</code> queries the Metrics Server to display real-time CPU (in millicores) and Memory utilization across all cluster nodes.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>Displays both absolute millicores (e.g. <code>3200m</code>) and percentage of total node capacity.</li>\n  <li>Requires <strong>Metrics Server</strong> to be installed and healthy in <code>kube-system</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>kubectl top nodes --sort-by='cpu'</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">If Metrics Server is not running, <code>kubectl top</code> will error with: <code>Metrics API not available</code>.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Use <code>--sort-by='cpu'</code> to sort nodes from highest to lowest utilization instantly.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_3_fix_top_pods","level":"junior","question":"How do you identify CPU-intensive Pods using kubectl top pods?","tags":["KUBERNETES","CLI","Performance"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\"><code>kubectl top pods</code> allows engineers to rapidly find resource hogs across all namespaces.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>Pass <code>-A</code> to query across all namespaces.</li>\n  <li>Use <code>--sort-by='cpu'</code> to identify top consumers.</li>\n  <li>Add <code>--containers</code> to drill down to specific container processes inside multi-container pods.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>kubectl top pods -A --sort-by='cpu' --containers | head -n 20</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Metrics Server aggregates metrics over a 15-second window; rapid sub-second CPU spikes may not show up in top output.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">The <code>--containers</code> flag is invaluable for multi-container pods with sidecars.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_4_fix_cpu_logs","level":"junior","question":"How do you check application logs of CPU-intensive Pods to detect thread deadlocks?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Troubleshooting","Observability"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">CPU spikes are frequently caused by infinite loops, unhandled JSON parsing bombs, or thread contention.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>Stream logs during the CPU spike: <code>kubectl logs -f &lt;pod&gt;</code>.</li>\n  <li>Look for repeating error loops, database reconnection storms, or GC pause logging.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>kubectl logs -f &lt;high-cpu-pod&gt; --tail=100</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Heavy logging itself consumes CPU! Printing thousands of log lines per second can exacerbate CPU exhaustion.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Correlate the exact timestamp of CPU spike with application log events.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_5_fix_exec_top","level":"junior","question":"How do you profile process CPU consumption inside a container using kubectl exec?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Troubleshooting","Profiling"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Executing into a running container allows direct operating system process inspection using standard Linux tools.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>Run <code>kubectl exec -it &lt;pod&gt; -- top</code> or <code>ps aux</code>.</li>\n  <li>For distroless or minimal containers without a shell, use <strong>kubectl debug</strong> to attach an ephemeral container with debugging utilities.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Attach ephemeral debugging container with top/htop/perf\nkubectl debug -it &lt;pod-name&gt; --image=nicolaka/netshoot --target=&lt;container-name&gt;</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Minimal production images (distroless, scratch) do not have <code>top</code> or <code>sh</code> installed; use <code>kubectl debug</code> instead.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Always mention ephemeral debug containers (<code>kubectl debug</code>) for modern distroless environments.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_6_fix_cpu_res","level":"junior","question":"How do you mitigate worker node CPU saturation using node draining and eviction?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Operations","Reliability"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">When a worker node is CPU-saturated and unresponsive, immediate remediation prevents cascading failures across adjacent workloads.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>1. <strong>Cordon Node:</strong> Mark unschedulable so no new pods land on it: <code>kubectl cordon &lt;node&gt;</code>.</li>\n  <li>2. <strong>Drain Workloads:</strong> Evict non-critical workloads to trigger rescheduling on healthy nodes: <code>kubectl drain &lt;node&gt; --ignore-daemonsets</code>.</li>\n  <li>3. <strong>Scale Compute:</strong> Increment Auto Scaling Group capacity or let Karpenter launch a larger instance.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Cordon and drain saturated node\nkubectl cordon worker-04\nkubectl drain worker-04 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Always include <code>--ignore-daemonsets</code> when draining; otherwise the drain command will fail because DaemonSets cannot be evicted.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Remember: Cordon stops new pods; Drain evicts existing pods.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_52","level":"mid","question":"Scenario: Your development team wants to deploy a new service, but they need isolated RBAC and resource quotas.","tags":["KUBERNETES","MultiTenancy","Governance"],"source":"K8s Production Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Multi-tenant onboarding requires provisioning a dedicated Namespace, scoping developer RBAC, enforcing ResourceQuotas, and applying default network isolation.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>1. <strong>Namespace Creation:</strong> <code>kubectl create ns team-alpha</code>.</li>\n  <li>2. <strong>ResourceQuota:</strong> Cap CPU requests (e.g. 10 cores) and Memory (e.g. 32Gi) to prevent budget overruns.</li>\n  <li>3. <strong>LimitRange:</strong> Enforce mandatory default requests/limits so developers cannot deploy unconstrained pods.</li>\n  <li>4. <strong>RBAC:</strong> Create a <code>RoleBinding</code> binding the team's group to the <code>admin</code> or <code>edit</code> cluster role within that namespace.</li>\n  <li>5. <strong>NetworkPolicy:</strong> Apply default-deny ingress to isolate pods from other teams.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>apiVersion: v1\nkind: LimitRange\nmetadata:\n  name: default-limits\n  namespace: team-alpha\nspec:\n  limits:\n  - default:\n      cpu: \"500m\"\n      memory: \"512Mi\"\n    defaultRequest:\n      cpu: \"100m\"\n      memory: \"128Mi\"\n    type: Container</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Without a LimitRange, applying a ResourceQuota means ANY pod manifest that omits requests or limits will be immediately rejected upon submission.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Standard onboarding recipe: Namespace + ResourceQuota + LimitRange + RBAC RoleBinding + NetworkPolicy.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_53","level":"mid","question":"What is the Cloud Native Computing Foundation (CNCF)?","tags":["KUBERNETES","CNCF","Ecosystem"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">The <strong>Cloud Native Computing Foundation (CNCF)</strong> is a sub-foundation of the Linux Foundation established in 2015 to foster and sustain open-source, vendor-neutral cloud-native ecosystems.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Maturity Levels:</strong> Projects graduate through three tiers:</li>\n  <li>• <em>Sandbox:</em> Early-stage, experimental projects.</li>\n  <li>• <em>Incubating:</em> Stable, adopted projects with proven governance.</li>\n  <li>• <em>Graduated:</em> Mature, production-grade projects that pass strict security audits and widespread enterprise adoption (e.g. Kubernetes, Prometheus, Envoy, containerd).</li>\n  <li><strong>Vendor Neutrality:</strong> Ensures that critical infrastructure standards remain open and not controlled by a single cloud vendor (Google, AWS, Microsoft).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Explore CNCF landscape online\n# https://landscape.cncf.io</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Just because a tool is in the CNCF does not mean it is production-ready; always check whether it is 'Sandbox' or 'Graduated'.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Mention that Kubernetes was the very first project graduated by the CNCF.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_54","level":"mid","question":"Briefly explain the purpose of some popular CNCF projects related to Kubernetes.","tags":["KUBERNETES","CNCF","Ecosystem"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">The CNCF ecosystem provides essential building blocks that complement Kubernetes across networking, storage, security, packaging, and observability.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>• <strong>Helm:</strong> The package manager for Kubernetes; templates and manages multi-manifest applications.</li>\n  <li>• <strong>Prometheus:</strong> Systems monitoring and time-series alerting engine.</li>\n  <li>• <strong>Envoy:</strong> High-performance C++ L7 proxy forming the data plane for service meshes (Istio).</li>\n  <li>• <strong>containerd:</strong> Industry-standard core container runtime.</li>\n  <li>• <strong>Cilium:</strong> eBPF-powered networking, security, and observability (CNI).</li>\n  <li>• <strong>Fluentd / Fluent Bit:</strong> Unified logging layer and data collector.</li>\n  <li>• <strong>Harbor:</strong> Trusted cloud-native container image registry with vulnerability scanning.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Install application using Helm package manager\nhelm repo add bitnami https://charts.bitnami.com/bitnami\nhelm install my-redis bitnami/redis</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Tools often overlap (e.g. Istio vs Linkerd, Fluent Bit vs Vector, Helm vs Kustomize). Choose based on team operational familiarity.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Highlight key graduated projects: Helm, Prometheus, Envoy, containerd, and Cilium.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_56","level":"mid","question":"What are some real-world challenges you've faced working with Kubernetes in production?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Production","SRE"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Operating Kubernetes at enterprise scale introduces distributed systems challenges across networking, storage, noisy neighbors, and upgrades.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>1. DNS Bottlenecks (ndots:5):</strong> CoreDNS overloading during traffic surges caused by default Linux search domain lookups.</li>\n  <li><strong>2. CFS CPU Quota Throttling:</strong> Aggressive CPU limits causing micro-throttling and latency spikes despite low overall node CPU usage.</li>\n  <li><strong>3. Multi-AZ EBS Storage Lockouts:</strong> Pods failing to schedule during node failover because the underlying EBS volume is locked to a specific Availability Zone.</li>\n  <li><strong>4. PDB Deadlocks During Upgrades:</strong> Misconfigured PodDisruptionBudgets blocking automated EKS/GKE node group rolling upgrades.</li>\n  <li><strong>5. Conntrack Table Exhaustion:</strong> High packet volume overflowing Linux netfilter connection tracking tables, dropping random TCP connections.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Monitor conntrack usage on worker nodes\ncat /proc/sys/net/netfilter/nf_conntrack_count\ncat /proc/sys/net/netfilter/nf_conntrack_max</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Never treat Kubernetes like a set-and-forget virtual machine hypervisor; production clusters require continuous tuning of kernel parameters, DNS caches, and CNI drivers.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Interview answer: Highlight 2 specific challenges (e.g. CFS CPU throttling and multi-AZ volume affinity) and explain the exact architectural solutions you implemented.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_57","level":"mid","question":"Explain the complete lifecycle of a Pod in Kubernetes.","tags":["KUBERNETES","PodLifecycle","Core"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">A Pod progresses through distinct operational phases from API submission to final termination.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>1. Pending:</strong> Pod manifest accepted in etcd, but pending scheduling or downloading images.</li>\n  <li><strong>2. ContainerCreating:</strong> Kubelet allocates network namespace (CNI), mounts volumes (CSI), and pulls images.</li>\n  <li><strong>3. Running:</strong> Bound to a node; at least one container is running or in the process of starting.</li>\n  <li><strong>4. Succeeded:</strong> All containers in the Pod terminated successfully with exit code 0 (common for Jobs).</li>\n  <li><strong>5. Failed:</strong> At least one container terminated with non-zero exit code or was terminated by the system.</li>\n  <li><strong>6. Terminating:</strong> Pod marked for deletion; receives <code>SIGTERM</code>, executes <code>preStop</code> hook, removed from Service Endpoints, and killed with <code>SIGKILL</code> after <code>terminationGracePeriodSeconds</code> (default 30s).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Inspect pod phase and container states\nkubectl get pod &lt;pod-name&gt; -o jsonpath='{.status.phase}'</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Pod phase is NOT container state. A Pod can be in <code>Running</code> phase while its container is crash-looping inside.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Clarify: 'Pod Phase is a high-level summary (Pending, Running, Succeeded, Failed); container status tracks actual process execution (Waiting, Running, Terminated).'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_58","level":"mid","question":"What are Init Containers vs Native Sidecar Containers in Kubernetes?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Containers","Workloads"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Init Containers run before main application containers start. In Kubernetes 1.28+, native Sidecar Containers run alongside main containers throughout the Pod lifecycle.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Init Containers:</strong> Run sequentially to completion (must exit 0) before any app container starts. Used for database migrations, waiting for external dependencies, or generating configuration files.</li>\n  <li><strong>Native Sidecar Containers (K8s 1.28+):</strong> Defined in <code>initContainers</code> with <code>restartPolicy: Always</code>. Kubelet starts them, waits for their startup probe to pass, then starts the main app containers. They remain running for the life of the pod (e.g. Istio proxy, Vault agent, log shipper) and terminate cleanly after app containers exit.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>apiVersion: v1\nkind: Pod\nmetadata:\n  name: app-with-sidecar\nspec:\n  initContainers:\n  - name: vault-agent # Native Sidecar\n    image: vault:1.15\n    restartPolicy: Always\n  - name: db-migration # Standard Init Container\n    image: company/migrator:v1\n    command: [\"npm\", \"run\", \"migrate\"]\n  containers:\n  - name: web-app\n    image: company/web:v1</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Before native sidecars, jobs with Envoy/Istio sidecars would never finish because the sidecar proxy remained running forever after the batch job finished! Native sidecars resolve this issue completely.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Mention Kubernetes 1.28+ native sidecars: <code>restartPolicy: Always</code> inside <code>initContainers</code>.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_59","level":"mid","question":"Explain resource requests and limits in Kubernetes and CFS bandwidth quota throttling.","tags":["KUBERNETES","Performance","LinuxInternals"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Resource management governs how the Linux kernel completely fair scheduler (CFS) and cgroups allocate CPU time and physical RAM.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>CPU CFS Quota:</strong> Kubernetes translates CPU limits into Linux CFS bandwidth quota (<code>cpu.cfs_quota_us</code> / <code>cpu.cfs_period_us</code>, default period 100ms).</li>\n  <li>• A limit of <code>cpu: 1000m</code> (1 core) gives the container 100ms of run time per 100ms period.</li>\n  <li>• If a multi-threaded application consumes its 100ms quota within the first 20ms, it is <strong>throttled</strong> for the remaining 80ms, creating severe tail latency!</li>\n  <li><strong>Memory Cgroups:</strong> Maps to <code>memory.max</code>. Unlike CPU, memory cannot be throttled; when exceeded, kernel invokes OOM Killer.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Check CPU throttling metrics in cgroups v2\ncat /sys/fs/cgroup/cpu.stat</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Many modern high-performance architectures (Uber, Datadog, CERN) run with CPU limits disabled or set very high, relying on Requests + HPA to prevent CFS latency penalties.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Show mastery: Explain how CFS quota period slicing works and why aggressive CPU limits cause latency spikes even on underutilized nodes.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_60","level":"mid","question":"What is ReadWriteMany (RWX) PersistentVolume access mode, and what storage systems support it?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Storage","CSI"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\"><strong>ReadWriteMany (RWX)</strong> allows a volume to be mounted simultaneously with read and write permissions by multiple Pods running across multiple different worker nodes.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Access Modes Breakdown:</strong></li>\n  <li>• <code>ReadWriteOnce (RWO):</code> Mountable as read-write by a single node only (e.g. AWS EBS, Azure Disk).</li>\n  <li>• <code>ReadOnlyMany (ROX):</code> Mountable as read-only by multiple nodes.</li>\n  <li>• <code>ReadWriteMany (RWX):</code> Mountable as read-write by multiple nodes simultaneously.</li>\n  <li>• <code>ReadWriteOncePod (RWOP):</code> Mountable by exactly one single Pod (K8s 1.22+).</li>\n  <li><strong>Supported RWX Storage Systems:</strong> Network file systems and shared distributed filesystems: AWS EFS, Azure Files, Google Cloud Filestore, NFS, CephFS, and GlusterFS.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>apiVersion: v1\nkind: PersistentVolumeClaim\nmetadata:\n  name: shared-assets-pvc\nspec:\n  accessModes:\n  - ReadWriteMany\n  storageClassName: efs-sc # AWS EFS CSI Driver\n  resources:\n    requests:\n      storage: 100Gi</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Attempting to mount an AWS EBS volume as RWX across multiple nodes will fail with <code>Multi-Attach error for volume</code> because EBS is block storage attachable to only one EC2 instance at a time.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">State: 'Block storage (EBS/Disk) is RWO; Shared file storage (EFS/NFS/CephFS) is RWX.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_61","level":"mid","question":"How do you perform application-level (Layer 7) load balancing in Kubernetes?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Networking","Ingress"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Application-level (L7) load balancing operates on HTTP/HTTPS request headers, cookies, paths, and gRPC methods, implemented via Ingress Controllers, Gateway API, or Service Meshes.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>1. Ingress Controller (NGINX, Traefik, ALB):</strong> Terminates TLS and routes HTTP traffic based on path rules (<code>/api</code> vs <code>/web</code>) or hostnames (<code>app.com</code>).</li>\n  <li><strong>2. Gateway API:</strong> The next-generation declarative standard dividing responsibilities across Platform Providers (GatewayClass), Cluster Operators (Gateway), and Developers (HTTPRoute, GRPCRoute).</li>\n  <li><strong>3. Service Mesh (Istio / Linkerd):</strong> Implements mTLS, weighted traffic splitting (e.g. 90% v1, 10% v2 for canary), circuit breaking, fault injection, and retries.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>apiVersion: gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: HTTPRoute\nmetadata:\n  name: api-routing\nspec:\n  parentRefs:\n  - name: public-gateway\n  rules:\n  - matches:\n    - path: { type: PathPrefix, value: /v2 }\n    backendRefs:\n    - name: api-v2-svc\n      port: 8080</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Standard Kubernetes ClusterIP Services only balance Layer 4 TCP connections. For long-lived HTTP/2 or gRPC connections, L4 load balancing sends all traffic to a single pod; L7 proxies (Envoy) are required.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Explain: 'Standard K8s Services operate at L4. For gRPC and HTTP header routing, use Ingress, Gateway API, or an Envoy-based Service Mesh.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_62","level":"mid","question":"What is the difference between an emptyDir volume and a hostPath volume?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Storage","Core"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Both are node-local volumes, but they differ fundamentally in lifecycle, scoping, and security.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>emptyDir:</strong></li>\n  <li>• Created when a Pod is assigned to a node; exists only as long as that Pod runs.</li>\n  <li>• Shared between containers in the same Pod (great for scratch space, caching, or shared build artifacts).</li>\n  <li>• Deleted permanently when the Pod is removed.</li>\n  <li>• Can be backed by RAM: <code>medium: Memory</code> (tmpfs).</li>\n  <li><strong>hostPath:</strong></li>\n  <li>• Mounts a file or directory directly from the host node's physical filesystem into the Pod.</li>\n  <li>• Persists even after the Pod terminates (stored on the node).</li>\n  <li>• Major security hazard: containers can read host system files (<code>/var/log</code>, <code>/etc/kubernetes</code>, Docker sockets).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># RAM-backed scratch space via emptyDir\nvolumes:\n- name: cache-volume\n  emptyDir:\n    medium: Memory\n    sizeLimit: 1Gi</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">hostPath breaks workload portability. If the Pod is rescheduled to another node, its data does not follow it.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Use emptyDir for temporary pod scratch space; restrict hostPath to privileged system DaemonSets (log shippers, monitoring agents).</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_63","level":"mid","question":"How would you troubleshoot an issue where a Service is not routing traffic to backend Pods?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Troubleshooting","Networking"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">When a Service fails to route traffic to backend Pods, use a disciplined step-by-step diagnostic workflow across selectors, endpoints, probes, and ports.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>1. Check Endpoints:</strong> <code>kubectl get endpoints &lt;svc&gt;</code>. If empty, the issue is selector mismatch or failing readiness probes.</li>\n  <li><strong>2. Check Pod Labels:</strong> Verify pod labels match the Service selector exactly.</li>\n  <li><strong>3. Verify Readiness Probes:</strong> Pods must be Ready (1/1) to receive service traffic.</li>\n  <li><strong>4. Port Mapping:</strong> Verify <code>targetPort</code> matches the container's listening port.</li>\n  <li><strong>5. Kube-proxy:</strong> Verify kube-proxy is running and has generated local iptables/IPVS rules on worker nodes.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Diagnostic runbook\nkubectl get endpoints &lt;service-name&gt;\nkubectl get pods -l &lt;selector-key&gt;=&lt;selector-val&gt;\nkubectl describe svc &lt;service-name&gt;</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">A typo in <code>targetPort</code>: If container listens on 8080 but targetPort is set to 80, connections will be dropped with 'Connection Refused'.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Golden sequence: Endpoints → Selectors → Readiness Probes → Port Mapping → Kube-proxy.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_1_fix_svc_ep","level":"junior","question":"How do you diagnose empty Service Endpoints (<none>) caused by failing readiness probes?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Networking","HealthChecks"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">When pods are running but Service Endpoints display <code>&lt;none&gt;</code>, failing readiness probes are the most frequent culprit.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>Run <code>kubectl get pods -l app=&lt;name&gt;</code> and check the <code>READY</code> column (e.g. <code>0/1</code>).</li>\n  <li>Inspect events using <code>kubectl describe pod</code> to find probe failure messages: <code>Unhealthy: Readiness probe failed</code>.</li>\n  <li>Fix the health endpoint path, port, or backend dependency.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>kubectl get pods -l app=my-app\nkubectl describe pod &lt;failing-pod&gt; | grep -A 5 \"Readiness\"</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">A container in <code>Running</code> phase will NOT receive traffic until its readiness probe returns HTTP 200-399.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Check the READY column in kubectl get pods: 0/1 means readiness probe failure.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_2_fix_svc_labels","level":"junior","question":"How do you verify Pod label matches with kubectl get pods -l?","tags":["KUBERNETES","CLI","Labels"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\"><code>kubectl get pods -l</code> filters pods using label queries to verify whether any pods match a given Service selector.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>Inspect the Service's selector: <code>kubectl get svc &lt;svc&gt; -o jsonpath='{.spec.selector}'</code>.</li>\n  <li>Pass that selector to <code>kubectl get pods -l</code>.</li>\n  <li>Compare returned pods with expected workload instances.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Query pods using service selector\nkubectl get pods -l app=backend,tier=api --show-labels</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Ensure no extra or missing label keys exist in the deployment template.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Use <code>--show-labels</code> to visually verify all metadata on running pods.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_3_fix_ports","level":"junior","question":"How do you debug port vs targetPort vs containerPort mapping mismatches in Services?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Networking","Ports"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Port definitions in Kubernetes Services can be confusing; understanding each field prevents connection refused errors.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>• <code>port:</code> The port exposed by the Service ClusterIP (what clients connect to).</li>\n  <li>• <code>targetPort:</code> The port on the Pod/container that the Service forwards traffic to.</li>\n  <li>• <code>containerPort:</code> Purely informational documentation in the PodSpec (does not open ports).</li>\n  <li>• <code>nodePort:</code> The port opened on every worker node (30000-32767) for external access.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>ports:\n- port: 80          # Clients connect to Service IP on port 80\n  targetPort: 8080  # Service forwards to Container port 8080\n  protocol: TCP</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">If <code>targetPort</code> is omitted, it defaults to the exact same value as <code>port</code>.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Rule of thumb: Clients connect to <code>port</code>; container listens on <code>targetPort</code>.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_4_fix_net_conn","level":"junior","question":"How do you test network connectivity to a Service ClusterIP using ephemeral debug containers?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Networking","Debugging"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\"><code>kubectl debug</code> launches ephemeral containers with pre-installed diagnostic tools (curl, dig, nc, tcpdump) to test in-cluster networking.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>Deploy a temporary debug container in the same namespace.</li>\n  <li>Test DNS resolution with <code>nslookup</code> / <code>dig</code>.</li>\n  <li>Test Layer 4 TCP connectivity with <code>nc -zv</code>.</li>\n  <li>Test Layer 7 HTTP connectivity with <code>curl -Iv</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Launch interactive netshoot debug container\nkubectl run netshoot-test --rm -it --image=nicolaka/netshoot -- /bin/bash\n# Inside the container:\ncurl -Iv http://my-service.prod.svc.cluster.local:80\nnc -zv my-service 80</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Remember that ClusterIPs are NOT pingable via ICMP! Kube-proxy only translates TCP/UDP packets, so <code>ping &lt;cluster-ip&gt;</code> will fail even when the Service is 100% healthy.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Never use ping to test a Kubernetes Service ClusterIP; always use curl or nc!</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_65","level":"mid","question":"Explain tolerations and nodeSelector in Pod scheduling.","tags":["KUBERNETES","Scheduling","Core"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\"><code>nodeSelector</code> is the simplest form of node selection constraint, while <code>tolerations</code> allow pods to schedule onto tainted nodes.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>nodeSelector:</strong> Specifies a map of key-value pairs matching node labels. If a node does not have all specified labels, the pod cannot be scheduled there.</li>\n  <li><strong>Tolerations:</strong> Overcome node taints. A pod with a toleration is allowed to schedule on a tainted node.</li>\n  <li><strong>Working Together:</strong> To isolate batch workloads on specific compute instances: Taint the node <code>workload=batch:NoSchedule</code>, label the node <code>workload=batch</code>, and configure the Pod with both matching toleration and nodeSelector.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>spec:\n  nodeSelector:\n    disktype: ssd\n  tolerations:\n  - key: \"special\"\n    operator: \"Equal\"\n    value: \"true\"\n    effect: \"NoSchedule\"</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">nodeSelector only supports exact matching; for advanced logic (OR, NOT, in set), use <code>nodeAffinity</code>.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">nodeSelector attracts; tolerations permit entry through taints.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_66","level":"mid","question":"Scenario: You have a legacy application that requires a specific host kernel module or physical device. How do you deploy it?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Containers","Hardware"],"source":"K8s Production Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Deploying hardware-accelerated or kernel-dependent workloads requires Device Plugins, privileged containers, or Node Feature Discovery (NFD).</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>1. Node Feature Discovery (NFD):</strong> Detects host kernel versions, instruction sets (AVX), and labels nodes automatically.</li>\n  <li><strong>2. Kubernetes Device Plugins:</strong> Advertise specialized hardware (NVIDIA GPUs, FPGAs, InfiniBand) to the kubelet as allocatable resources.</li>\n  <li><strong>3. Privileged DaemonSet for Kernel Modules:</strong> A privileged init container mounts <code>/lib/modules</code> and runs <code>modprobe &lt;module&gt;</code> on the host node.</li>\n  <li><strong>4. SecurityContext Capabilities:</strong> Grant only specific Linux capabilities (e.g. <code>CAP_SYS_ADMIN</code>, <code>CAP_NET_ADMIN</code>) instead of full root privileged mode.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>securityContext:\n  capabilities:\n    add: [\"SYS_RAWIO\", \"NET_ADMIN\"]\nvolumeMounts:\n- name: host-dev\n  mountPath: /dev\nvolumes:\n- name: host-dev\n  hostPath: { path: /dev }</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Running fully privileged containers in shared multi-tenant clusters violates security standards. Scope access strictly using specific Linux capabilities and Device Plugins.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Best practice: Use Kubernetes Device Plugins and Node Feature Discovery to schedule hardware-dependent workloads safely.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_67","level":"mid","question":"Scenario: Your company is moving from a monolithic application to microservices on Kubernetes: What is your migration strategy?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Architecture","Migration"],"source":"K8s Production Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Migrating from a monolith to microservices on Kubernetes should follow the <strong>Strangler Fig Pattern</strong> to de-risk delivery.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Phase 1: Lift-and-Shift Monolith:</strong> Containerize the monolith, run it on Kubernetes as a baseline Deployment behind an Ingress controller.</li>\n  <li><strong>Phase 2: Carve Out First Microservice:</strong> Extract a distinct, high-value, decoupled domain (e.g., Notification or Authentication service).</li>\n  <li><strong>Phase 3: Route Traffic via Ingress:</strong> Use Ingress path-based routing (e.g. <code>/auth/*</code> → new microservice, <code>/*</code> → legacy monolith).</li>\n  <li><strong>Phase 4: Shared Services & Database Decoupling:</strong> Break shared database schemas into independent domain databases (Database-per-service).</li>\n  <li><strong>Phase 5: Observability & Tracing:</strong> Implement OpenTelemetry and distributed tracing to monitor cross-service latency.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Monitor distributed traffic tracing with OpenTelemetry / Jaeger</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Do not attempt a 'Big Bang' rewrite: extracting services incrementally behind an Ingress proxy ensures continuous production delivery.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">State the Strangler Fig Pattern clearly: Route traffic via Ingress, carve out services incrementally, and isolate database schemas.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_68","level":"mid","question":"Scenario: You need to restrict network access to a sensitive database Pod, allowing only specific frontends: How do you configure this?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Security","NetworkPolicy"],"source":"K8s Production Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Network access in Kubernetes is open by default; restricting access requires deploying a CNI plugin with NetworkPolicy enforcement and creating ingress policies.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>1. <strong>CNI Requirement:</strong> Ensure a network plugin supporting NetworkPolicy is active (Calico, Cilium, AWS VPC CNI with network policy enabled).</li>\n  <li>2. <strong>Pod Selector:</strong> Target the database pods (<code>app: postgres-db</code>).</li>\n  <li>3. <strong>Ingress Rules:</strong> Specify allowed traffic sources using <code>podSelector</code> (e.g. <code>app: payment-api</code>) on specific ports (e.g. 5432).</li>\n  <li>4. <strong>Default Deny:</strong> Once a NetworkPolicy selects a pod, all unlisted traffic is dropped automatically.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: NetworkPolicy\nmetadata:\n  name: db-access-policy\n  namespace: prod\nspec:\n  podSelector:\n    matchLabels:\n      role: database\n  policyTypes:\n  - Ingress\n  ingress:\n  - from:\n    - podSelector:\n        matchLabels:\n          role: backend-api\n    ports:\n    - protocol: TCP\n      port: 5432</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Applying a NetworkPolicy in a cluster running a basic CNI like Flannel will have ZERO effect! Flannel does not support NetworkPolicy enforcement; you must install Calico or Cilium.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Crucial detail: 'Network policies are additive and default-deny once selected. Ensure your CNI supports policy enforcement.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_1_fix_np_def","level":"junior","question":"How do you define default-deny NetworkPolicies in a Kubernetes namespace?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Security","NetworkPolicy"],"source":"K8s Production Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">A <strong>Default Deny</strong> policy isolates all pods in a namespace, blocking all incoming or outgoing connections unless explicitly whitelisted.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>Target all pods with an empty podSelector: <code>podSelector: {}</code>.</li>\n  <li>Specify <code>policyTypes: [Ingress, Egress]</code> with empty rule lists.</li>\n  <li>Any new pod launched in the namespace is immediately isolated from network traffic.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: NetworkPolicy\nmetadata:\n  name: default-deny-all\n  namespace: production\nspec:\n  podSelector: {}\n  policyTypes:\n  - Ingress\n  - Egress</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">A default-deny egress policy blocks CoreDNS lookups! You must explicitly allow UDP/TCP port 53 to <code>kube-system</code> CoreDNS pods.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Always allow DNS egress on port 53 when creating default-deny egress policies.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_3_fix_np_rules","level":"junior","question":"How do you configure namespaceSelector and podSelector in NetworkPolicy rules?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Security","NetworkPolicy"],"source":"K8s Production Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">NetworkPolicy rules support fine-grained cross-namespace traffic rules using both <code>namespaceSelector</code> and <code>podSelector</code>.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>AND vs OR Semantics (YAML Gotcha):</strong></li>\n  <li>• <em>AND (Same Array Item):</em> Specifying namespaceSelector and podSelector under the SAME bullet item requires pods to match both.</li>\n  <li>• <em>OR (Separate Array Items):</em> Specifying them with separate dashes (<code>-</code>) allows traffic matching EITHER rule.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>ingress:\n- from:\n  # Traffic allowed from pods with role=frontend inside namespaces labeled env=prod (AND)\n  - namespaceSelector:\n      matchLabels:\n        env: prod\n    podSelector:\n      matchLabels:\n        role: frontend</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">YAML dash indentation errors will inadvertently turn an AND rule into an OR rule, exposing services across namespaces.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Watch indentation: Same list item = AND; separate dashes = OR.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_4_fix_np_cni","level":"junior","question":"How do CNI plugins like Calico and Cilium enforce NetworkPolicies?","tags":["KUBERNETES","CNI","Networking"],"source":"K8s Deep Dive","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Standard Kubernetes does not enforce NetworkPolicies; the responsibility falls entirely on CNI plugins running on worker nodes.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Calico:</strong> Uses Linux <code>iptables</code> or IP sets on each node. The Felix agent watches the API server and programs kernel packet filter chains.</li>\n  <li><strong>Cilium:</strong> Bypasses iptables completely, compiling NetworkPolicies into <strong>eBPF programs</strong> loaded directly into the Linux kernel socket layer, enabling Layer 7 (HTTP/DNS) policy filtering with near-zero latency.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Check Cilium network policy status\ncilium status\ncilium bpf policy list</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">AWS VPC CNI did not support NetworkPolicies historically; in K8s 1.25+, AWS added native network policy enforcement.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Compare: 'Calico programs iptables; Cilium programs eBPF filters directly in kernel memory.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_70","level":"mid","question":"A developer reports that their Pod cannot write to its mounted volume. What are your troubleshooting steps?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Storage","Troubleshooting"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Volume write failures typically stem from Linux filesystem permissions, read-only mounts, or underlying storage quota exhaustion.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>1. Check PVC & PV Status:</strong> Verify the PVC is <code>Bound</code> (not <code>Pending</code>).</li>\n  <li><strong>2. Inspect Mount Flags:</strong> Check if <code>readOnly: true</code> was set in <code>volumeMounts</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>3. Verify In-Container UID/GID:</strong> Non-root containers running as UID 10001 often lack write permission to directories created with root ownership (UID 0).</li>\n  <li><strong>4. Fix with fsGroup:</strong> Configure <code>securityContext.fsGroup</code> so Kubelet changes the group ownership of the mounted volume to match the container's group.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>spec:\n  securityContext:\n    fsGroup: 2000 # Kubelet recursively chowns volume files to GID 2000\n  containers:\n  - name: app\n    securityContext:\n      runAsUser: 1000\n      runAsGroup: 2000</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Setting <code>fsGroup</code> on massive volumes containing millions of small files can cause Pods to hang in ContainerCreating for 20+ minutes while Kubelet recursively runs chown on every file! Use <code>fsGroupChangePolicy: OnRootMismatch</code> to fix this.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Remember: <code>securityContext.fsGroup</code> fixes Linux volume permission denied issues.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_2_fix_pvc_status","level":"junior","question":"How do you check PVC/PV binding status and VolumeAttachment events?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Storage","CLI"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">When persistent storage fails to mount, inspect the PVC binding state and cluster VolumeAttachment objects.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>Run <code>kubectl get pvc -n &lt;ns&gt;</code> to verify the phase is <code>Bound</code>.</li>\n  <li>Check <code>kubectl get volumeattachments</code> to confirm the cloud disk has attached to the specific EC2/worker node.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Inspect VolumeAttachment status\nkubectl get volumeattachments\nkubectl describe pvc &lt;pvc-name&gt;</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">A PVC stuck in <code>Pending</code> with <code>WaitForFirstConsumer</code> is waiting for the scheduler to place the Pod first; this is normal behavior!</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Check describe pvc: look for FailedBinding or WaitingForFirstConsumer events.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_3_fix_sc_dyn","level":"junior","question":"How do you debug StorageClass dynamic provisioning and CSI provisioner driver logs?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Storage","CSI"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Dynamic volume provisioning is executed by CSI controller sidecar pods running in <code>kube-system</code>.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>If dynamic provisioning fails, inspect the <code>csi-provisioner</code> container logs in the CSI controller pod (e.g., <code>ebs-csi-controller</code>).</li>\n  <li>Look for IAM permission errors (e.g. <code>ec2:CreateVolume unauthorized</code>) or cloud quota limits.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Inspect AWS EBS CSI provisioner logs\nkubectl logs -n kube-system -l app=ebs-csi-controller -c csi-provisioner --tail=100</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Missing cloud IAM roles on CSI driver ServiceAccounts is the #1 cause of dynamic volume provisioning failure.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Check CSI driver logs in kube-system when PVCs fail to provision.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_4_fix_storage_cloud","level":"junior","question":"How do you verify underlying cloud storage quotas, IOPS, and disk attach limits?","tags":["KUBERNETES","CloudInfrastructure","Storage"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Worker nodes have hard limits on the number of EBS/block storage volumes they can attach concurrently.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>AWS EC2 instances have maximum volume attachment limits (typically 28 to 64 EBS volumes per instance depending on type).</li>\n  <li>If a node hits its attachment limit, new Pods mounting PVCs will fail with <code>AttachVolume.Attach failed: volume limit exceeded</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Check allocatable volume limits on a node\nkubectl get node &lt;node-name&gt; -o jsonpath='{.status.allocatable}' | grep -i attachable-volumes</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">High-density pod nodes (many small pods sharing one node) will run out of EBS volume attachment slots long before they run out of CPU/memory.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Be aware of cloud instance volume attachment limits in high-density clusters.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_5_fix_kubelet_logs","level":"junior","question":"How do you check Kubelet volume mount logs and CSI node driver events on a worker node?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Storage","Kubelet"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">After cloud storage attaches to a node, the local Kubelet and CSI node plugin format and mount the filesystem.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>Inspect Kubelet systemd logs on the worker node: <code>journalctl -u kubelet | grep -i mount</code>.</li>\n  <li>Check CSI node driver pod logs in <code>kube-system</code>.</li>\n  <li>Look for filesystem formatting errors or mount timeout exceptions.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>journalctl -u kubelet -n 100 | grep -i \"volume\"</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">A corrupted filesystem on an existing disk will cause Kubelet mount operations to hang indefinitely.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Check node journalctl logs to isolate OS-level disk mount failures.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_6_fix_fsgroup","level":"junior","question":"How do you fix container volume write permission denied errors using securityContext fsGroup?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Security","Storage"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Containers running as non-root users cannot write to volumes owned by root (UID 0) unless <code>fsGroup</code> is configured.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>Configure <code>securityContext.fsGroup: &lt;GID&gt;</code> at the Pod level.</li>\n  <li>Kubernetes automatically assigns group ownership of the volume directory and files to that GID.</li>\n  <li>Use <code>fsGroupChangePolicy: OnRootMismatch</code> to prevent slow startup times on large volumes.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>spec:\n  securityContext:\n    fsGroup: 3000\n    fsGroupChangePolicy: \"OnRootMismatch\"\n  containers:\n  - name: my-app\n    image: my-app:v1</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">fsGroup only works on volume plugins that support POSIX group ownership changes (e.g. ext4, xfs); it does not apply to CIFS/SMB.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Always use <code>fsGroupChangePolicy: OnRootMismatch</code> with fsGroup in production.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_7_fix_selinux","level":"junior","question":"How do SELinux and AppArmor security modules impact Kubernetes volume mounts?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Security","LinuxInternals"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Linux Mandatory Access Control (MAC) systems like SELinux and AppArmor enforce kernel-level file access restrictions.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>On RHEL/CentOS systems with SELinux enforcing, container mounts require the <code>:z</code> or <code>:Z</code> relabeling flag.</li>\n  <li>In Kubernetes, configure <code>securityContext.seLinuxOptions</code> to assign correct SELinux level categories.</li>\n  <li>Check node <code>/var/log/audit/audit.log</code> for AVC denial messages.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Check for SELinux AVC denials on host node\nausearch -m avc -ts recent</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Disabling SELinux completely is an insecure anti-pattern; resolve labels properly using K8s securityContext.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Check audit.log for AVC denials if volume permissions fail despite correct chown.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_72","level":"senior","question":"How do you manage certificates and TLS in Kubernetes?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Security","TLS"],"source":"K8s Production Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Production Kubernetes requires automated TLS certificate provisioning and rotation across two distinct domains: Internal Cluster PKI and Public Ingress TLS.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>1. Internal Control Plane PKI:</strong> Managed via Kubeadm or cloud provider CA. X.509 client certificates expire every 12 months; renewed with <code>kubeadm certs renew all</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>2. Ingress & Public TLS:</strong> Automated via <strong>cert-manager</strong>.</li>\n  <li>• <code>ClusterIssuer:</code> Configures integration with Let's Encrypt (ACME), HashiCorp Vault, or Private Enterprise CA.</li>\n  <li>• <code>Certificate CRD:</code> Requests certificates with automated HTTP-01 or DNS-01 challenge validation.</li>\n  <li>• Automates renewal 30 days prior to expiry and writes TLS Secrets read by Ingress controllers.</li>\n  <li><strong>3. Service-to-Service mTLS:</strong> Enforced transparently via Service Mesh (Istio/Linkerd) using short-lived SPIFFE/SPIRE certificates.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>apiVersion: cert-manager.io/v1\nkind: ClusterIssuer\nmetadata:\n  name: letsencrypt-prod\nspec:\n  acme:\n    server: https://acme-v02.api.letsencrypt.org/directory\n    email: security@company.com\n    privateKeySecretRef:\n      name: letsencrypt-prod-account-key\n    solvers:\n    - http01:\n        ingress:\n          class: nginx</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">HTTP-01 challenges fail for internal/private clusters not exposed to the public internet; use DNS-01 challenges with Route53/Cloudflare API credentials instead.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Mention the dual-layer strategy: 'cert-manager for public Ingress certificates, and mTLS via service mesh for zero-trust pod-to-pod communication.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_73","level":"senior","question":"Explain Kubernetes Operators in detail. What problems do they solve?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Architecture","Operators"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">An Operator is an application-specific controller that extends the Kubernetes API to create, configure, and manage complex stateful applications automatically.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Components:</strong> Custom Resource Definition (CRD) + Custom Controller running an active reconciliation loop.</li>\n  <li><strong>Informers & WorkQueues:</strong> The controller watches for events, enqueues changes, and executes a <code>Reconcile()</code> function to achieve desired state.</li>\n  <li><strong>Capabilities:</strong></li>\n  <li>• Automated backups and point-in-time restores.</li>\n  <li>• Automated minor/major version database upgrades.</li>\n  <li>• High availability failover (e.g. electing a new PostgreSQL primary).</li>\n  <li>• Dynamic configuration reloading without downtime.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Framework for building Operators in Go\nkubebuilder init --domain company.com\nkubebuilder create api --group apps --version v1 --kind Database</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Operators must be idempotent: running the Reconcile function multiple times with the same input must produce identical results without side effects.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Operator definition: 'A method of packaging, deploying, and managing a Kubernetes application by combining CRDs with a controller that automates human operational knowledge.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_74","level":"senior","question":"What is kubeconfig and how is it structured and used?","tags":["KUBERNETES","CLI","Authentication"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">A <code>kubeconfig</code> file (default: <code>~/.kube/config</code>) stores cluster connectivity details, user credentials, and context mappings used by <code>kubectl</code> and client SDKs.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Three Core Sections:</strong></li>\n  <li>1. <strong>clusters:</strong> API server endpoints and CA certificates.</li>\n  <li>2. <strong>users:</strong> Client credentials (X.509 client certs, bearer tokens, or exec credential plugins like <code>aws eks get-token</code>).</li>\n  <li>3. <strong>contexts:</strong> Triplet combining a Cluster, User, and default Namespace.</li>\n  <li>• <code>current-context:</code> Points to the currently active context.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>apiVersion: v1\nkind: Config\nclusters:\n- cluster:\n    certificate-authority-data: LS0t...\n    server: https://k8s-prod.company.com:6443\n  name: prod-cluster\ncontexts:\n- context:\n    cluster: prod-cluster\n    user: aws-sre\n    namespace: production\n  name: prod-context\ncurrent-context: prod-context\nusers:\n- name: aws-sre\n  user:\n    exec:\n      apiVersion: client.authentication.k8s.io/v1beta1\n      command: aws\n      args: [\"eks\", \"get-token\", \"--cluster-name\", \"prod-cluster\"]</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Embedding raw static admin tokens inside kubeconfig files is a security risk; use short-lived SSO exec plugins (<code>aws eks get-token</code>, <code>gke-gcloud-auth-plugin</code>).</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Use <code>kubectx</code> and <code>kubens</code> CLI utilities to switch contexts and namespaces rapidly.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_75","level":"senior","question":"How does Kubernetes handle garbage collection?","tags":["KUBERNETES","ControlPlane","Internals"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Kubernetes garbage collection cleans up unneeded cluster resources, including terminated containers, dead Pods, unused container images, and cascading child objects.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>1. Owner References & Cascading Deletion:</strong> Child objects (Pods) specify an <code>ownerReference</code> pointing to their parent (ReplicaSet). Deleting a parent can delete children (<code>Foreground</code> or <code>Background</code> cascading deletion) or leave them orphaned.</li>\n  <li><strong>2. Pod Garbage Collector:</strong> Cleans up terminated Pods (Completed/Failed) once count exceeds <code>--terminated-pod-gc-threshold</code> (default 12,500).</li>\n  <li><strong>3. Kubelet Image Garbage Collection:</strong> Tracks local disk usage on worker nodes:</li>\n  <li>• High threshold (default 85%): Kubelet starts deleting unused images (oldest first).</li>\n  <li>• Low threshold (default 80%): Image deletion stops once disk drops below this floor.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Delete deployment while preserving running pods (Orphan deletion)\nkubectl delete deployment my-app --cascade=orphan\n\n# Configure image GC thresholds in Kubelet configuration\n# imageGCHighThresholdPercent: 80\n# imageGCLowThresholdPercent: 70</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">If worker nodes run out of disk space before Kubelet image GC kicks in, nodes report <code>DiskPressure</code> and begin evicting active workloads.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Explain: 'OwnerReferences handle cascading API object cleanup; Kubelet handles local node image and dead container disk reclamation.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_79","level":"senior","question":"What are best practices for writing Dockerfiles and container images for Kubernetes?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Docker","Security"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Production container images for Kubernetes must be secure, lightweight, fast-booting, and compliant with rootless execution standards.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>1. Multi-Stage Builds:</strong> Compile source code in a heavy build stage, copy only the compiled binary into a minimal runtime stage.</li>\n  <li><strong>2. Distroless or Minimal Base Images:</strong> Use Google Distroless, Chainguard, or Alpine to minimize CVE vulnerability surface.</li>\n  <li><strong>3. Run as Non-Root:</strong> Explicitly declare an unprivileged user (<code>USER 10001:10001</code>).</li>\n  <li><strong>4. Proper Signal Handling (PID 1):</strong> Ensure the application or init process properly handles <code>SIGTERM</code> for graceful shutdown.</li>\n  <li><strong>5. Clean Up Caches:</strong> Clear package manager caches (<code>rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*</code>) in the same RUN layer.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> DOCKERFILE</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Build stage\nFROM golang:1.22-alpine AS builder\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY . .\nRUN CGO_ENABLED=0 go build -ldflags=\"-w -s\" -o server .\n\n# Final minimal runtime stage\nFROM gcr.io/distroless/static-debian12:nonroot\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY --from=builder /app/server .\nUSER nonroot:nonroot\nEXPOSE 8080\nENTRYPOINT [\"/app/server\"]</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Running containers as root (UID 0) allows attackers who exploit container breakouts to gain root access to the underlying host node.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Key points: Multi-stage builds, Distroless/minimal base, Non-root user, and SIGTERM signal propagation.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_80","level":"senior","question":"How do you secure your Kubernetes cluster at the container image level?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Security","DevSecOps"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Image security requires proactive scanning, cryptographic signing, and admission enforcement before images reach cluster nodes.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>1. CI/CD Static Vulnerability Scanning:</strong> Scan images during build using <strong>Trivy</strong>, Grype, or Snyk to block images with Critical CVEs.</li>\n  <li><strong>2. Image Signing & Provenance:</strong> Cryptographically sign container images using <strong>Cosign (Sigstore)</strong> to verify publisher identity.</li>\n  <li><strong>3. Admission Enforcement:</strong> Deploy an Admission Controller (<strong>Kyverno</strong> or <strong>OPA Gatekeeper</strong>) that rejects any Pod whose image lacks a valid Cosign cryptographic signature.</li>\n  <li><strong>4. Immutable Tags:</strong> Prohibit mutable tags like <code>:latest</code>; mandate cryptographic SHA256 digests (<code>@sha256:...</code>).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>apiVersion: kyverno.io/v1\nkind: ClusterPolicy\nmetadata:\n  name: verify-image-signatures\nspec:\n  validationFailureAction: Enforce\n  rules:\n  - name: check-image-cosign\n    match:\n      resources:\n        kinds: [\"Pod\"]\n    verifyImages:\n    - imageReferences: [\"myrepo.azurecr.io/*\"]\n      attestors:\n      - entries:\n        - keys:\n            publicKeys: |\n              -----BEGIN PUBLIC KEY-----\n              MFkwEwYHKoZIzj0CAQYIKoZIzj0DAQcDQgAE...\n              -----END PUBLIC KEY-----</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Using <code>image: my-app:latest</code> makes rollbacks unreliable and prevents Kubernetes from detecting whether a new image exists if <code>imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent</code>.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">State the pipeline: Scan in CI (Trivy) → Sign image (Cosign) → Enforce at cluster gate (Kyverno/OPA).</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_81","level":"senior","question":"What is the significance of the targetPort and port fields in a Kubernetes Service?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Networking","Services"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\"><code>port</code> and <code>targetPort</code> decouple external client service contracts from internal container process implementations.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>port:</strong> The port number on the Service's virtual IP (ClusterIP). Clients inside the cluster connect to this port (e.g. port 80).</li>\n  <li><strong>targetPort:</strong> The port on the underlying Pod where the container process is listening (e.g. port 8080).</li>\n  <li><strong>Named Ports:</strong> You can assign a string name to container ports in the PodSpec (e.g. <code>name: http-web</code>), and reference that name in the Service's <code>targetPort</code>. This allows updating application ports without changing Service manifests.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>apiVersion: v1\nkind: Service\nmetadata:\n  name: web-svc\nspec:\n  ports:\n  - name: http\n    port: 80           # Exposed on ClusterIP\n    targetPort: web-ui # Matches container port name</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">If targetPort references a numeric port that the container is not listening on, connections will fail with 'Connection Refused' with zero error messages in Kubernetes events.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Recommend using Named Ports (e.g. targetPort: http) for loose coupling between Services and Deployments.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_82","level":"senior","question":"How do you effectively manage secrets in Kubernetes at enterprise scale?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Security","Secrets"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Enterprise secret management eliminates plaintext Git storage, leverages external KMS providers, and integrates with centralized secret vaults.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>1. External Secrets Operator (ESO):</strong> Syncs secrets from AWS Secrets Manager, Azure Key Vault, or GCP Secret Manager into native Kubernetes Secrets automatically.</li>\n  <li><strong>2. HashiCorp Vault Agent:</strong> Injects dynamic, short-lived database credentials directly into container memory via sidecars or CSI drivers.</li>\n  <li><strong>3. Sealed Secrets:</strong> Asymmetric encryption allowing developers to safely commit encrypted secrets to public Git repositories; only the in-cluster controller can decrypt them.</li>\n  <li><strong>4. KMS Encryption at Rest:</strong> Encrypts etcd database storage with hardware security modules (AWS KMS / Cloud HSM).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>apiVersion: external-secrets.io/v1beta1\nkind: ExternalSecret\nmetadata:\n  name: stripe-credentials\nspec:\n  refreshInterval: 1h\n  secretStoreRef:\n    name: aws-secretsmanager\n    kind: ClusterSecretStore\n  target:\n    name: stripe-k8s-secret # Native K8s Secret created automatically\n  data:\n  - secretKey: STRIPE_API_KEY\n    remoteRef:\n      key: prod/stripe/api-key</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Never commit base64 encoded secrets to Git repositories! Base64 is trivially reversible and constitutes a major security incident.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Recommend External Secrets Operator (ESO) as the cloud-native standard for bridging cloud vaults to Kubernetes.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_83","level":"senior","question":"What is kube-proxy and what are its modes of operation?","tags":["KUBERNETES","KubeProxy","Networking"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\"><code>kube-proxy</code> is a node network agent that implements the Kubernetes Service abstraction by programming packet translation rules.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>1. iptables Mode (Default):</strong> Generates netfilter iptables chains for every Service and Endpoint. Efficient up to ~5,000 services, but rule inspection is $O(n)$ sequential, causing high CPU and latency in massive clusters.</li>\n  <li><strong>2. IPVS Mode (Linux Virtual Server):</strong> Uses IPVS kernel hash tables ($O(1)$ lookup time). Supports advanced balancing algorithms (Least Connections, Weighted, Round Robin) and scales smoothly to 50,000+ services.</li>\n  <li><strong>3. Userspace Mode (Obsolete):</strong> Proxied packets through user space; deprecated due to severe performance penalties.</li>\n  <li><strong>4. eBPF Replacement (Cilium):</strong> Bypasses kube-proxy completely, handling load balancing directly in Linux socket programs.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Check current kube-proxy configuration\nkubectl get configmap -n kube-system kube-proxy -o yaml | grep mode</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">IPVS requires specific Linux kernel modules (<code>ip_vs</code>, <code>ip_vs_rr</code>) to be pre-loaded on worker nodes before kube-proxy boots.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Summarize: 'iptables mode is $O(n)$ sequential; IPVS mode is $O(1)$ hashed; Cilium eBPF eliminates kube-proxy entirely for bare-metal performance.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_84","level":"senior","question":"Explain the concept of Immutable fields in Kubernetes API objects.","tags":["KUBERNETES","API","Core"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Immutable fields are properties of Kubernetes objects that cannot be modified after initial creation.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Why Immutability Exists:</strong> Prevents breaking running workloads, ensures consistent controller state, and protects against distributed race conditions.</li>\n  <li><strong>Common Immutable Fields:</strong></li>\n  <li>• <code>spec.selector</code> in Deployments and StatefulSets (cannot change selectors on existing deployments).</li>\n  <li>• <code>spec.clusterIP</code> in Services (cannot re-assign cluster IP).</li>\n  <li>• <code>spec.storageClassName</code> in PersistentVolumeClaims.</li>\n  <li><strong>Immutable Secrets & ConfigMaps:</strong> In K8s 1.21+, you can explicitly declare <code>immutable: true</code> on ConfigMaps/Secrets. This improves cluster performance by telling Kubelet to stop polling etcd for updates, dramatically reducing API server load.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>apiVersion: v1\nkind: ConfigMap\nmetadata:\n  name: static-config\nimmutable: true # Kubelet will stop watching this object for changes\ndata:\n  VERSION: \"2.4.0\"</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Attempting to edit an immutable field with <code>kubectl apply</code> will fail with: <code>field is immutable</code>. You must delete and recreate the resource.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Mention that setting <code>immutable: true</code> on ConfigMaps significantly reduces API server CPU usage in large clusters.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_85","level":"senior","question":"What is kubectl drain used for, and how does it coordinate with cordon and PDBs?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Operations","Reliability"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\"><code>kubectl drain</code> safely evicts all Pods from a worker node so maintenance (kernel upgrade, hardware replacement, node reboot) can be performed without service downtime.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>1. Automated Cordon:</strong> Marks the node as <code>SchedulingDisabled</code> so no new pods land on it.</li>\n  <li><strong>2. Eviction API Call:</strong> Respects PodDisruptionBudgets (PDBs). If evicting a pod violates a PDB, the drain waits.</li>\n  <li><strong>3. Graceful Termination:</strong> Sends <code>SIGTERM</code> and waits for <code>terminationGracePeriodSeconds</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>4. Essential Flags:</strong></li>\n  <li>• <code>--ignore-daemonsets:</code> Necessary because DaemonSets cannot be rescheduled elsewhere.</li>\n  <li>• <code>--delete-emptydir-data:</code> Necessary if pods mount local emptyDir volumes.</li>\n  <li>• <code>--force:</code> Evicts standalone pods not managed by a controller.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Drain node safely\nkubectl drain node-worker-01 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data\n\n# Restore node to service after maintenance\nkubectl uncordon node-worker-01</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Never use <code>kubectl delete pod --force</code> during node drain; force deletion bypasses graceful termination and the Eviction API.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Workflow: Cordon (stop scheduling) → Drain (evict gracefully respecting PDBs) → Maintenance → Uncordon (resume scheduling).</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_86","level":"senior","question":"How would you troubleshoot network latency issues between Pods in a Kubernetes cluster?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Networking","Troubleshooting"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Network latency troubleshooting requires isolating whether latency occurs in the Linux network stack, CNI overlay, physical underlay, or DNS.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>1. Same Node vs Cross Node:</strong> Test if latency occurs between pods on the same node (veth bridge) versus pods on different nodes (CNI overlay/physical NIC).</li>\n  <li><strong>2. DNS Latency Check:</strong> Check if latency is caused by CoreDNS lookups (ndots:5 delay).</li>\n  <li><strong>3. MTU Size:</strong> Ensure overlay network MTU (VXLAN 1450) matches physical interface MTU.</li>\n  <li><strong>4. Conntrack Saturation:</strong> Check if node conntrack tables are saturated, causing dropped packet retries.</li>\n  <li><strong>5. Packet Capture:</strong> Use <code>kubectl debug</code> to run <code>tcpdump</code> inside the pod network namespace.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Check MTU and conntrack table on host\nip link show\nsudo conntrack -S</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Misconfigured MTU (e.g. 1500 on overlay when underlay is 1500) causes packet fragmentation, silently degrading performance by 80%+.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Isolate the layer: DNS → Same Node vs Cross Node → CNI MTU → Node Conntrack → Physical underlay.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_1_fix_net_scope","level":"junior","question":"How do you isolate cross-node vs same-node latency using network benchmarking?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Networking","Performance"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Comparing latency between pods on the same host vs different hosts immediately tells you if the problem is in the CNI/network wire or container stack.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>If same-node latency is high: Problem is container CPU throttling, cgroups, or application runtime.</li>\n  <li>If cross-node latency is high: Problem is CNI encapsulation, cloud VPC routing, or cross-Availability-Zone latency.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Benchmarking with iperf3\nkubectl run iperf-server --image=networkstatic/iperf3 -- -s\nkubectl run iperf-client --rm -it --image=networkstatic/iperf3 -- -c &lt;server-ip&gt;</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Cross-AZ network latency in cloud providers (AWS/GCP) adds 1-2ms per request; cross-region adds 30-70ms.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Test both same-node and cross-node latency to pinpoint the layer.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_3_fix_net_health","level":"junior","question":"How do you check worker node network interface saturation, packet drops, and conntrack limits?","tags":["KUBERNETES","LinuxInternals","Troubleshooting"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Network packet drops on host interfaces cause TCP retransmissions that manifest as erratic latency spikes in applications.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>Use <code>netstat -s</code> or <code>ip -s link</code> to look for dropped RX/TX packets.</li>\n  <li>Check Linux conntrack table saturation: <code>cat /proc/sys/net/netfilter/nf_conntrack_count</code>.</li>\n  <li>Check cloud network bandwidth credits (AWS T3/T4 burstable credits or ENI limits).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Check dropped packets on node\nip -s link show eth0\n# Check conntrack saturation percentage\nsysctl net.netfilter.nf_conntrack_count net.netfilter.nf_conntrack_max</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Reaching the cloud instance ENI bandwidth ceiling results in silent packet drops at the hypervisor level.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Check rx_dropped, tx_dropped, and nf_conntrack_count on the host node.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_4_fix_net_cloud","level":"junior","question":"How do you diagnose cloud VPC networking latency, cross-AZ traffic, and ENI saturation?","tags":["KUBERNETES","CloudInfrastructure","Networking"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Cloud VPC limits (AWS ENI thresholds, cross-Availability Zone transit latency) directly impact cluster networking performance.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>Cross-AZ traffic incurs cloud latency (1-2ms) and data transfer costs; use <strong>Topology Aware Routing</strong> to keep traffic within the same AZ.</li>\n  <li>Check cloud VPC flow logs for rejected packets or security group blocks.</li>\n  <li>In AWS EKS, verify whether worker nodes are reaching their maximum Elastic Network Interface (ENI) or IP address limits.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Enable Topology Aware Routing on a Service\nkubectl annotate service my-service service.kubernetes.io/topology-mode=auto</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Cross-AZ data transfer fees in AWS can rapidly become a massive cloud bill for high-throughput microservices.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Use <code>service.kubernetes.io/topology-mode=auto</code> to keep traffic local to the same AZ whenever possible.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_5_fix_mesh_latency","level":"junior","question":"How do you debug Service Mesh (Istio/Envoy) proxy sidecar overhead and connection pool exhaustion?","tags":["KUBERNETES","ServiceMesh","Performance"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Service mesh proxies (Envoy sidecars) add two extra network hops per request (client envoy → server envoy). Misconfigured connection pools cause 503 errors and latency spikes.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>Check Envoy proxy metrics: <code>upstream_cx_overflow</code>, <code>upstream_cx_connect_timeout</code>, and <code>upstream_rq_pending_overflow</code>.</li>\n  <li>Tune Istio <code>DestinationRule</code> connection pool sizes (<code>maxConnections</code>, <code>http1MaxPendingRequests</code>).</li>\n  <li>Enable Envoy access logging to isolate client vs server proxy latency.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Query Envoy statistics via admin port\nkubectl exec &lt;pod-name&gt; -c istio-proxy -- pilot-agent request GET /stats | grep \"upstream_cx\"</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Default Envoy connection pool limits are conservative (e.g. 1024 connections); high-throughput services will drop traffic under load without tuning.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Check <code>pilot-agent request GET /stats</code> to inspect live Envoy connection pool saturation.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_6_fix_bench_tools","level":"junior","question":"How do you benchmark Kubernetes network throughput and latency using iperf3 and netperf?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Networking","Benchmarking"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Standardizing on network benchmarking tools allows SREs to measure raw CNI throughput and latency baselines.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>Deploy a server Pod and a client Pod pinned to different worker nodes via <code>nodeSelector</code>.</li>\n  <li>Run <code>iperf3</code> for maximum bandwidth (Gbps) and packet transfer rates.</li>\n  <li>Run <code>netperf</code> for TCP Request/Response transaction latency (RR mode).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Run iperf3 bandwidth benchmark\nkubectl run iperf-srv --image=networkstatic/iperf3 -- -s\nkubectl run iperf-cli --rm -it --image=networkstatic/iperf3 -- -c &lt;srv-ip&gt; -t 30 -P 4</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Always test multi-stream traffic (<code>-P 4</code> or <code>-P 8</code>) to evaluate multi-core CPU distribution.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Establish network performance baselines using iperf3 after any CNI upgrade or cloud instance migration.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_7_fix_tcpdump","level":"junior","question":"How do you perform deep packet capture inside Pod network namespaces using kubectl debug and tcpdump?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Troubleshooting","Networking"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">When application logs fail to reveal the cause of dropped connections, capturing raw PCAP files inside the pod's network namespace is required.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>In Kubernetes 1.23+, use <strong>kubectl debug</strong> to attach an ephemeral container (like <code>nicolaka/netshoot</code>) sharing the target pod's network namespace.</li>\n  <li>Run <code>tcpdump</code> to capture traffic to a <code>.pcap</code> file.</li>\n  <li>Stream the pcap directly to Wireshark on your local workstation.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Attach ephemeral debugging container and run tcpdump\nkubectl debug -it &lt;target-pod&gt; --image=nicolaka/netshoot -- tcpdump -i eth0 -n \"port 8080\" -w - | wireshark -k -i -</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Running tcpdump on high-throughput production pods can consume heavy CPU and generate multi-gigabyte PCAPs; always filter by port or IP.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Pipe <code>tcpdump</code> from <code>kubectl debug</code> directly into local Wireshark for real-time packet inspection.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_8_fix_coredns_lat","level":"junior","question":"How do you resolve CoreDNS lookup latency, 5-second DNS delay, and NodeLocal DNSCache?","tags":["KUBERNETES","DNS","Performance"],"source":"K8s Troubleshooting","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">The infamous '5-second DNS delay' in Kubernetes is caused by a race condition in Linux kernel netfilter/conntrack during concurrent A and AAAA DNS lookups.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Root Cause:</strong> Linux glibc sends concurrent IPv4 (A) and IPv6 (AAAA) DNS queries over UDP using the same source port. Netfilter conntrack drops the second packet due to port race, triggering a 5-second timeout.</li>\n  <li><strong>Solution 1: NodeLocal DNSCache:</strong> A DaemonSet running local DNS caching agents on every node, switching DNS from UDP to TCP and eliminating cross-node DNS hops.</li>\n  <li><strong>Solution 2: Pod DNS Options:</strong> Set <code>single-request-reopen</code> in the Pod's <code>dnsConfig</code> to force sequential socket creation.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>spec:\n  dnsConfig:\n    options:\n    - name: single-request-reopen\n    - name: ndots\n      value: \"2\"</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Do not reduce <code>ndots</code> globally without testing; setting ndots too low will break short service discovery (e.g. <code>http://my-service</code>).</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Deploy <strong>NodeLocal DNSCache</strong> on all production clusters to eliminate DNS latency and conntrack race conditions.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_87","level":"senior","question":"What are PreStop hooks and PostStart hooks in a container lifecycle?","tags":["KUBERNETES","PodLifecycle","Reliability"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Lifecycle hooks allow containers to execute management commands or HTTP calls immediately after creation (PostStart) or immediately prior to termination (PreStop).</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>PostStart Hook:</strong> Executes asynchronously immediately after container creation. Note: It is not guaranteed to run before the container ENTRYPOINT.</li>\n  <li><strong>PreStop Hook (Critical):</strong> Executes synchronously before the container receives <code>SIGTERM</code>. Kubelet blocks the termination process until the hook completes or <code>terminationGracePeriodSeconds</code> expires.</li>\n  <li><strong>Why PreStop is Essential for Zero Downtime:</strong> When a Pod terminates, removing its IP from kube-proxy iptables takes 2-5 seconds. A <code>preStop: sleep 10</code> hook keeps the container alive and processing inflight connections while network routes are dismantled.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>lifecycle:\n  preStop:\n    exec:\n      command: [\"/bin/sh\", \"-c\", \"sleep 15; nginx -s quit\"]</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">If the PreStop hook hangs, Kubelet will wait until <code>terminationGracePeriodSeconds</code> expires, then send a hard <code>SIGKILL</code>.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Always configure <code>preStop: sleep 10</code> on web services to prevent dropped connections during rolling deployments.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_88","level":"senior","question":"How does Kubernetes handle resource limits and requests on a Pod with multiple containers?","tags":["KUBERNETES","ResourceManagement","Containers"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">For multi-container Pods, scheduling requests and limits are calculated as the sum of all container requirements, while runtime cgroups are enforced individually.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Scheduling (Aggregate Sum):</strong> The scheduler calculates Pod resource request as: <code>Pod Request = Sum(App Containers Requests) + Max(Init Containers Requests)</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Runtime (Individual Container Cgroups):</strong> Kubelet creates separate cgroups for each container inside the Pod. If Container A exceeds its memory limit, Container A is OOMKilled; Container B continues running.</li>\n  <li><strong>Pod Overhead:</strong> Kubernetes adds a small runtime overhead for the container runtime and sandbox.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>spec:\n  containers:\n  - name: app\n    resources: { requests: { memory: \"1Gi\" } }\n  - name: envoy-sidecar\n    resources: { requests: { memory: \"256Mi\" } }\n  # Total Pod Request = 1.25 GiB</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Heavy sidecars (e.g. Istio proxy, Splunk, Datadog) multiply resource requirements across thousands of pods. Rightsize sidecar resources carefully.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">State: 'Scheduling is based on the aggregate sum; cgroups and limits are enforced on each container independently.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_89","level":"senior","question":"What is a mutating webhook vs a validating webhook in the Admission Controller chain?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Security","AdmissionControllers"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Admission Webhooks are HTTP callbacks that intercept API server requests prior to object persistence in etcd, enabling automated mutation and policy enforcement.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Execution Order:</strong></li>\n  <li>1. Authentication & Authorization (RBAC).</li>\n  <li>2. <strong>Mutating Admission Webhooks:</strong> Executed first. Can modify the object (e.g. Istio automatically injecting Envoy sidecars, injecting default resource limits).</li>\n  <li>3. Schema Validation.</li>\n  <li>4. <strong>Validating Admission Webhooks:</strong> Executed last. Evaluates the final object and returns allow (200) or deny (400) (e.g. Kyverno/OPA Gatekeeper blocking root containers).</li>\n  <li>5. Persistence to etcd.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>apiVersion: admissionregistration.k8s.io/v1\nkind: ValidatingWebhookConfiguration\nmetadata:\n  name: block-privileged-pods\nwebhooks:\n- name: validate.security.company.com\n  rules:\n  - operations: [\"CREATE\", \"UPDATE\"]\n    apiGroups: [\"\"]\n    apiVersions: [\"v1\"]\n    resources: [\"pods\"]\n  clientConfig:\n    service:\n      name: policy-checker\n      namespace: security\n      path: /validate\n  failurePolicy: Fail # or Ignore</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">If <code>failurePolicy: Fail</code> is set on a webhook, and the webhook service pod crashes, the API server will reject ALL pod creations across the entire cluster!</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Order is critical: Mutating webhooks run first to modify manifests; Validating webhooks run second to enforce strict security policies.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_90","level":"senior","question":"Explain the concept of VolumeClaimTemplates in StatefulSets.","tags":["KUBERNETES","StatefulSet","Storage"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\"><code>volumeClaimTemplates</code> in a StatefulSet dynamically provision a dedicated, isolated PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC) for every individual pod replica.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Predictable Naming:</strong> Generates PVCs named <code>&lt;template-name&gt;-&lt;statefulset-name&gt;-&lt;ordinal&gt;</code> (e.g. <code>data-kafka-0</code>, <code>data-kafka-1</code>).</li>\n  <li><strong>Persistent Binding:</strong> If pod <code>kafka-1</code> crashes or is rescheduled to another node, the StatefulSet controller ensures it reattaches to the exact same PVC <code>data-kafka-1</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Lifecycle Decoupling:</strong> Scaling down a StatefulSet leaves the PVCs untouched in etcd, preventing accidental data destruction.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>volumeClaimTemplates:\n- metadata:\n    name: datadir\n  spec:\n    accessModes: [ \"ReadWriteOnce\" ]\n    storageClassName: \"gp3\"\n    resources:\n      requests:\n        storage: 50Gi</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">VolumeClaimTemplates cannot be modified on an existing StatefulSet manifest; updating storage size requires patching the generated PVCs directly.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">State: 'VolumeClaimTemplates automate dedicated PVC provisioning per pod ordinal, binding storage permanently to that pod identity.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_91","level":"senior","question":"What is the difference between a Job and a CronJob in Kubernetes?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Workloads","Batch"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">A <strong>Job</strong> runs a batch workload to successful completion (exit code 0). A <strong>CronJob</strong> manages Jobs on a recurring schedule using standard crontab syntax.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Job Primitives:</strong></li>\n  <li>• <code>completions:</code> Number of pods that must finish successfully.</li>\n  <li>• <code>parallelism:</code> How many pods run concurrently.</li>\n  <li>• <code>backoffLimit:</code> Number of retries before marking the Job as failed (default 6).</li>\n  <li>• <code>activeDeadlineSeconds:</code> Maximum duration the Job is permitted to run before termination.</li>\n  <li><strong>CronJob Concurrency Policies:</strong></li>\n  <li>• <code>Allow (Default):</code> Can run concurrent jobs if previous run hasn't finished.</li>\n  <li>• <code>Forbid:</code> Skips new run if previous job is still running (essential for database backups).</li>\n  <li>• <code>Replace:</code> Cancels running job and starts new one.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>apiVersion: batch/v1\nkind: CronJob\nmetadata:\n  name: nightly-backup\nspec:\n  schedule: \"0 2 * * *\" # Daily at 2 AM\n  concurrencyPolicy: Forbid\n  successfulJobsHistoryLimit: 3\n  failedJobsHistoryLimit: 5\n  jobTemplate:\n    spec:\n      backoffLimit: 2\n      template:\n        spec:\n          restartPolicy: OnFailure\n          containers:\n          - name: backup\n            image: backup-tool:v1</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Always set <code>restartPolicy: OnFailure</code> or <code>Never</code> for Jobs; <code>restartPolicy: Always</code> is prohibited by the Job controller.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Highlight <code>concurrencyPolicy: Forbid</code> to prevent duplicate concurrent runs during long-running tasks.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_92","level":"senior","question":"How can you ensure High Availability of the Kubernetes control plane?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Architecture","HighAvailability"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">A highly available Kubernetes control plane eliminates single points of failure across etcd, API servers, schedulers, and controllers across multiple Availability Zones.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>1. etcd Quorum (Raft):</strong> Deploy an odd number of etcd members (minimum 3, recommended 5 in enterprise). A 3-node cluster tolerates 1 failure; a 5-node cluster tolerates 2 failures.</li>\n  <li><strong>2. Multi-Master API Servers:</strong> Run multiple active-active <code>kube-apiserver</code> instances behind an external Layer 4 Load Balancer (AWS NLB / HAProxy).</li>\n  <li><strong>3. Leader Election:</strong> <code>kube-scheduler</code> and <code>kube-controller-manager</code> use leader election leases in etcd; only one active instance reconciles while standby instances remain hot.</li>\n  <li><strong>4. Stacked vs External Topology:</strong></li>\n  <li>• <em>Stacked:</em> etcd runs as static pods on the same control plane nodes.</li>\n  <li>• <em>External:</em> etcd runs on dedicated standalone compute nodes for maximum I/O isolation.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Check leader election endpoints\nkubectl get leases -n kube-system</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Running an even number of etcd nodes (e.g. 4) provides NO additional fault tolerance over 3 nodes (both tolerate only 1 failure: quorum is 3), while increasing network split-brain risks.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">State the Raft quorum formula: $Quorum = \\lfloor N/2 \\rfloor + 1$. Always maintain an odd number of etcd members across separate availability zones.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_93","level":"senior","question":"What is kubeadm and how does it bootstrap Kubernetes clusters?","tags":["KUBERNETES","ClusterManagement","Kubeadm"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\"><code>kubeadm</code> is the official Kubernetes tool for bootstrapping conformant, production-ready clusters adhering to best practice defaults.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>kubeadm init:</strong> Executes preflight checks, generates internal PKI certificates, generates kubeconfig files, deploys static pod manifests for control plane components (etcd, apiserver, scheduler, controller-manager), and installs CoreDNS and kube-proxy.</li>\n  <li><strong>kubeadm join:</strong> Bootstraps a worker node by validating the join token and discovery token CA hash, fetching cluster-info, and configuring local Kubelet.</li>\n  <li><strong>Cluster Upgrades:</strong> Supports structured zero-downtime upgrades: <code>kubeadm upgrade plan</code> → <code>kubeadm upgrade apply</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Initialize control plane with custom pod CIDR\nkubeadm init --pod-network-cidr=192.168.0.0/16 --control-plane-endpoint=\"k8s-lb:6443\" --upload-certs\n\n# Join worker node\nkubeadm join k8s-lb:6443 --token &lt;token&gt; --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:&lt;hash&gt;</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">A newly initialized cluster will report nodes as <code>NotReady</code> until a third-party CNI network plugin (Calico/Cilium) is installed!</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Summarize: 'kubeadm init bootstraps the control plane and PKI; kubeadm join connects worker nodes securely.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_94","level":"senior","question":"Describe a situation where you would use a ServiceAccount and projected token authentication.","tags":["KUBERNETES","Security","Authentication"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">A <strong>ServiceAccount</strong> provides an internal identity for processes running inside a Pod to authenticate securely to the Kubernetes API server or cloud provider APIs.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Use Case: Prometheus Operator or Ingress Controller:</strong> These workloads need to query the API server for Services and Endpoints.</li>\n  <li><strong>Bound ServiceAccount Tokens (K8s 1.22+):</strong> Kubernetes uses the <code>TokenRequest</code> API to mount short-lived, cryptographically signed OIDC tokens into <code>/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token</code> via projected volumes.</li>\n  <li><strong>Cloud Identity Federation (IRSA / Workload Identity):</strong> Cloud providers (AWS, Azure, GCP) federate with the K8s OIDC provider. Pods assuming a ServiceAccount automatically exchange their bound token for temporary cloud IAM credentials without managing static AWS access keys!</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>apiVersion: v1\nkind: ServiceAccount\nmetadata:\n  name: aws-s3-reader\n  annotations:\n    eks.amazonaws.com/role-arn: arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/S3ReaderRole</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">If an application does not need to talk to the API server, always set <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code> to prevent stolen token attacks if the pod is compromised.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Highlight: 'Modern Kubernetes utilizes Bound Service Account Tokens with OIDC federation to cloud IAM, eliminating static cloud keys entirely.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_1_fix_sa_create","level":"junior","question":"How do you create and scope ServiceAccounts for Kubernetes workloads?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Security","ServiceAccount"],"source":"K8s Production Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Never use the <code>default</code> ServiceAccount for application workloads; always create dedicated ServiceAccounts with least-privilege RBAC.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>Create dedicated ServiceAccounts per application microservice.</li>\n  <li>Disable automated token mounting if API access is unneeded: <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code>.</li>\n  <li>Bind the ServiceAccount to specific Roles using RoleBindings.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>apiVersion: v1\nkind: ServiceAccount\nmetadata:\n  name: checkout-sa\n  namespace: prod\nautomountServiceAccountToken: false</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">The <code>default</code> ServiceAccount in every namespace has no default permissions, but any role bound to it accidentally affects all unassigned pods.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Always create dedicated ServiceAccounts for every microservice.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_3_fix_crb_bind","level":"junior","question":"How do you bind ServiceAccounts with Roles and ClusterRoles using RoleBindings?","tags":["KUBERNETES","RBAC","Security"],"source":"K8s Production Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">RoleBindings attach access permissions to a ServiceAccount, granting either namespace-scoped or cluster-scoped access.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>RoleBinding:</strong> Grants permissions within one namespace.</li>\n  <li><strong>ClusterRoleBinding:</strong> Grants permissions across all namespaces (use with caution).</li>\n  <li>You can bind a <code>ClusterRole</code> with a namespace <code>RoleBinding</code> to reuse standard roles (like <code>view</code>) within a single namespace.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1\nkind: RoleBinding\nmetadata:\n  name: app-view-binding\n  namespace: prod\nsubjects:\n- kind: ServiceAccount\n  name: checkout-sa\n  namespace: prod\nroleRef:\n  kind: ClusterRole\n  name: view # Standard K8s built-in role\n  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Granting <code>ClusterRoleBinding</code> with <code>cluster-admin</code> to an application ServiceAccount creates a massive security liability.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Bind built-in ClusterRoles (view, edit) using namespace-scoped RoleBindings.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_95","level":"senior","question":"What is kubectl diff and how does Server-Side Apply manage configuration drift?","tags":["KUBERNETES","CLI","GitOps"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\"><code>kubectl diff</code> previews differences between your local manifest and the live cluster state. <strong>Server-Side Apply (SSA)</strong> tracks field-level ownership directly in the API server.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>kubectl diff:</strong> Sends your manifest to the API server as a dry-run apply, retrieves the generated live state, and prints a colored unified diff against etcd.</li>\n  <li><strong>Server-Side Apply (K8s 1.22+):</strong> Replaces client-side <code>kubectl.kubernetes.io/last-applied-configuration</code> annotations.</li>\n  <li>• Uses <code>managedFields</code> in object metadata to track which manager (Helm, ArgoCD, HPA) owns each individual field.</li>\n  <li>• Detects field ownership conflicts cleanly instead of silently overwriting changes.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Preview changes before applying\nkubectl diff -f deployment.yaml\n\n# Apply using Server-Side Apply with a manager identifier\nkubectl apply --server-side --field-manager=my-ci-pipeline -f deployment.yaml</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Client-side apply often wipes out fields added dynamically by operators (like HPA replica counts); Server-Side Apply prevents this by respecting field ownership.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Highlight Server-Side Apply (SSA) and field management as the foundation of modern GitOps.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_96","level":"senior","question":"How would you secure a Kubernetes cluster from a network perspective (Defense-in-Depth)?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Security","Networking"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">A comprehensive network security architecture enforces defense-in-depth across the perimeter, control plane, node interfaces, and pod-to-pod communications.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>1. Private Control Plane:</strong> API server endpoints restricted to private VPC subnets with authorized CIDR blocks.</li>\n  <li><strong>2. Default Deny NetworkPolicies:</strong> Block all cross-namespace and pod-to-pod traffic by default.</li>\n  <li><strong>3. Service Mesh mTLS:</strong> Enforce STRICT mutual TLS with cryptographic identity validation between all microservices.</li>\n  <li><strong>4. Egress Control:</strong> Route all outbound traffic through NAT Gateways and egress proxies to restrict public destination IPs.</li>\n  <li><strong>5. eBPF Network Security (Cilium):</strong> Enforce Layer 7 DNS-aware network policies (e.g. only allow egress to <code>*.api.stripe.com</code>).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>apiVersion: \"cilium.io/v2\"\nkind: CiliumNetworkPolicy\nmetadata:\n  name: allow-stripe-only\nspec:\n  endpointSelector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: payment\n  egress:\n  - toFQDNs:\n    - matchPattern: \"*.stripe.com\"\n    toPorts:\n    - ports:\n      - port: \"443\"\n        protocol: TCP</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Standard Kubernetes NetworkPolicies cannot filter traffic based on DNS domain names; they only support IP CIDRs. Use Cilium for L7 FQDN egress filtering.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Deliver the 5 pillars: Private API endpoint, Default-deny NetworkPolicies, mTLS, L7 DNS Egress filtering, and Egress NAT gateways.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_97","level":"senior","question":"What is the Container Runtime Interface (CRI) and how does it execute containers?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Architecture","CRI"],"source":"K8s 100 Guide","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">The <strong>Container Runtime Interface (CRI)</strong> is a gRPC plugin interface that allows Kubelet to interact with various container runtimes without needing to recompile Kubernetes.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>High-Level vs Low-Level Runtimes:</strong></li>\n  <li>• <em>High-Level (CRI Runtimes):</em> <strong>containerd</strong> and <strong>CRI-O</strong>. Handle image pulling, image unpack, storage snapshots, and network namespace configuration.</li>\n  <li>• <em>Low-Level (OCI Runtimes):</em> <strong>runc</strong> and <strong>crun</strong>. Handle raw Linux kernel namespace creation, cgroup resource limits, and process execution.</li>\n  <li><strong>Sandboxed Runtimes (gVisor / Kata):</strong> CRI supports alternative runtimes (<code>RuntimeClass</code>) that run containers in lightweight VMs (Kata) or user-space kernel emulators (gVisor) for hard multi-tenant security.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Query CRI runtime status using crictl\ncrictl info\ncrictl pods\ncrictl images</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Do NOT use <code>docker</code> commands to troubleshoot modern Kubernetes nodes; Docker daemon is absent. Use <strong>crictl</strong>.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">State the flow: Kubelet → CRI (gRPC) → containerd → OCI Runtime (runc) → Linux kernel cgroups/namespaces.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_1_fix_bg_arch","level":"junior","question":"Explain the architecture and workflow of a Blue/Green Deployment in Kubernetes.","tags":["KUBERNETES","Deployments","BlueGreen"],"source":"K8s Production Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">A Blue/Green deployment provisions two identical production environments: Blue (active production) and Green (new release), cutting over traffic instantaneously via a Service.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>1. Deploy Blue Deployment: <code>app: web, version: blue</code>.</li>\n  <li>2. Service points to <code>version: blue</code>.</li>\n  <li>3. Deploy Green Deployment: <code>app: web, version: green</code> alongside Blue.</li>\n  <li>4. Run smoke tests directly against Green via a private test service.</li>\n  <li>5. Update the production Service selector to <code>version: green</code> to cut over 100% of user traffic in a single atomic transaction.</li>\n  <li>6. Keep Blue running temporarily for instant rollback if issues arise.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>apiVersion: v1\nkind: Service\nmetadata:\n  name: web-service\nspec:\n  selector:\n    app: web\n    version: green # Updated from blue to green instantaneously</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Blue/Green requires double compute capacity during rollout because both complete environments run concurrently.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Highlight the benefit: Zero downtime and instantaneous atomic cutover / instant rollback.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_2_fix_bg_svc","level":"junior","question":"How do you switch Service selectors and execute instant rollbacks in Blue/Green deployments?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Deployments","CLI"],"source":"K8s Production Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Switching a Service selector is an atomic operation in etcd; kube-proxy immediately updates iptables rules across all nodes.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>Patch the Service selector using <code>kubectl patch</code>.</li>\n  <li>To rollback: Re-apply the patch with <code>version=blue</code>.</li>\n  <li>Rollback completes in milliseconds with zero pod restarts.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Atomic cutover to Green\nkubectl patch svc web-service -p '{\"spec\":{\"selector\":{\"version\":\"green\"}}}'\n\n# Instant rollback to Blue\nkubectl patch svc web-service -p '{\"spec\":{\"selector\":{\"version\":\"blue\"}}}'</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Existing active HTTP keep-alive connections may take a few seconds to drain from the old environment.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Use <code>kubectl patch svc</code> for atomic, scripted traffic cutover.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_4_fix_bg_teardown","level":"junior","question":"When and how do you safely teardown the legacy environment in Blue/Green deployments?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Deployments","Operations"],"source":"K8s Production Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Once the Green environment proves stable under production load, the old Blue Deployment is decommissioned to reclaim cluster resources.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>Maintain the Blue environment for a defined bake period (e.g., 2 hours).</li>\n  <li>Scale Blue replicas to 0 first (allowing rapid recovery if needed).</li>\n  <li>Delete the Blue Deployment manifest after verification.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Scale down Blue deployment after verification\nkubectl scale deployment/web-blue --replicas=0\n\n# Delete old deployment\nkubectl delete deployment/web-blue</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Do not delete Blue immediately after traffic cutover; preserve it for at least 1 hour in case subtle bugs emerge under load.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Scale replicas to 0 first; delete the deployment only after the bake period expires.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_1_fix_canary_arch","level":"junior","question":"Explain the architecture and workflow of a Canary Deployment in Kubernetes.","tags":["KUBERNETES","Deployments","Canary"],"source":"K8s Production Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">A Canary deployment rolls out a new release to a small fraction of real users (e.g. 5%), verifies telemetry metrics, and gradually ramps traffic to 100%.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Two Implementations:</strong></li>\n  <li>• <em>Replica Ratio (Primitive):</em> Run 9 replicas of v1 and 1 replica of v2 under the same Service. 10% of requests randomly land on v2.</li>\n  <li>• <em>Ingress / Service Mesh (Modern):</em> Run separate v1 and v2 deployments; configure Ingress annotations (NGINX canary) or Istio VirtualService weights to precisely shift traffic (e.g. 5% → 25% → 50% → 100%).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># NGINX Ingress Canary Specification\napiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: Ingress\nmetadata:\n  name: api-canary\n  annotations:\n    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/canary: \"true\"\n    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/canary-weight: \"10\" # 10% traffic to canary\nspec:\n  rules:\n  - host: api.company.com\n    http:\n      paths:\n      - path: /\n        pathType: Prefix\n        backend:\n          service: { name: api-canary-svc, port: { number: 80 } }</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Simple replica ratio scaling can cause canary pods to be overwhelmed if 1 canary pod is asked to handle 10% of high traffic volume.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Recommend using Ingress annotations or Argo Rollouts rather than basic pod replica ratios for canary delivery.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_2_fix_canary_ingress","level":"junior","question":"How do you configure Ingress annotations and Service Meshes for Canary traffic splitting?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Ingress","Canary"],"source":"K8s Production Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Advanced canary routing leverages HTTP headers, cookies, or percentage weights to route specific test users to the canary version.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Header-Based Canary:</strong> Route only internal employees sending <code>X-Canary: always</code> header.</li>\n  <li><strong>Cookie-Based Canary:</strong> Pin users to a version using session cookies.</li>\n  <li><strong>Weight-Based Canary:</strong> Route a percentage of random traffic.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>metadata:\n  annotations:\n    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/canary: \"true\"\n    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/canary-by-header: \"X-Canary\"\n    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/canary-by-header-value: \"alpha-testers\"</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Ensure CORS headers and session cookies are maintained consistently across both canary and stable versions.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Use header-based canary routing for internal team validation before exposing traffic to public users.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_3_fix_canary_shift","level":"junior","question":"How do you automate progressive traffic weight shifting during a Canary rollout?","tags":["KUBERNETES","GitOps","Canary"],"source":"K8s Production Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Progressive delivery tools (<strong>Argo Rollouts</strong>, <strong>Flagger</strong>) automate the step-by-step weight shifting and metric evaluation.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>Declare steps: <code>setWeight: 10%</code> → <code>pause: {duration: 10m}</code> → <code>setWeight: 50%</code>.</li>\n  <li>Between steps, the controller runs Prometheus metric analysis queries.</li>\n  <li>If metric checks pass, it advances; if errors exceed threshold, it initiates automatic rollback.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1\nkind: Rollout\nmetadata:\n  name: checkout-rollout\nspec:\n  strategy:\n    canary:\n      steps:\n      - setWeight: 10\n      - pause: { duration: 15m }\n      - setWeight: 30\n      - pause: { duration: 30m }\n      - setWeight: 100</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Always configure adequate pause durations between traffic shifts so statistical sample sizes in Prometheus are reliable.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Standard tool: Argo Rollouts with automated metric analysis.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_4_fix_canary_metrics","level":"junior","question":"How do you automate Canary rollback based on Prometheus error rates and latency?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Observability","Canary"],"source":"K8s Production Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Automated analysis eliminates human bias by rolling back canary deployments the moment error rates or latency thresholds are breached.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>Define an <strong>AnalysisTemplate</strong> that queries Prometheus every 1 minute.</li>\n  <li>Track HTTP 5xx error rate: <code>sum(rate(http_requests_total{status=~\"5..\"}[2m])) / sum(rate(http_requests_total[2m])) &lt; 0.01</code>.</li>\n  <li>Track p99 latency: <code>histogram_quantile(0.99, sum(rate(http_request_duration_seconds_bucket[2m])) by (le)) &lt; 0.5</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1\nkind: AnalysisTemplate\nmetadata:\n  name: success-rate\nspec:\n  metrics:\n  - name: success-rate\n    interval: 1m\n    successCondition: result[0] &gt;= 0.99\n    failureLimit: 2\n    provider:\n      prometheus:\n        address: http://prometheus.monitoring:9090\n        query: sum(rate(http_requests{status=\"200\"}[1m])) / sum(rate(http_requests[1m]))</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Low-traffic services can skew percentage error rates (1 failed request out of 2 total requests = 50% error rate). Set minimum request thresholds.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Use Prometheus AnalysisTemplates in Argo Rollouts to trigger instant automatic rollbacks on 5xx errors.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"k100_5_fix_canary_promote","level":"junior","question":"How do you promote a Canary deployment to fully replace the stable version?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Deployments","Canary"],"source":"K8s Production Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Once all analysis phases succeed, the progressive delivery controller scales the stable deployment to the new version and cleans up canary resources.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>In Argo Rollouts, the rollout updates the stable ReplicaSet to the new pod template.</li>\n  <li>Traffic shift is moved to 100%.</li>\n  <li>The canary proxy rules are dismantled.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Promote a paused rollout manually\nkubectl argo rollouts promote checkout-rollout</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Ensure GitOps repositories reflect the new image tag in Git; otherwise, the next ArgoCD sync will revert the promotion!</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Ensure GitOps state matches the promoted canary version.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"pg_148","level":"mid","question":"What is the difference between a Pod and a Deployment in Kubernetes?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Workloads","Core"],"source":"K8s Core Architecture","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">A <strong>Pod</strong> is the basic atomic execution unit representing running containers. A <strong>Deployment</strong> is a declarative supervisor controller that manages the scaling, self-healing, and lifecycle of Pods.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Pod:</strong> Ephemeral. If a standalone Pod crashes or its worker node dies, Kubernetes will NEVER recreate or reschedule it.</li>\n  <li><strong>Deployment:</strong> Continuous self-healing. Manages ReplicaSets. If a Pod is deleted, the Deployment immediately creates a replacement. Provides rolling updates, version histories, and declarative scaling.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Standalone Pod (DO NOT USE IN PRODUCTION)\napiVersion: v1\nkind: Pod\nmetadata: { name: ephemeral-pod }\nspec:\n  containers: [{ name: app, image: nginx }]</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Never deploy raw Pod manifests directly in production. Always wrap them in a Deployment, StatefulSet, or DaemonSet.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Analogy: 'A Pod is a single worker; a Deployment is the manager ensuring the required number of workers are always on the floor.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"kub_extra_1","level":"senior","question":"How do you configure and troubleshoot Cilium eBPF for high-performance Kubernetes networking?","tags":["KUBERNETES","eBPF","Cilium"],"source":"K8s Production Deep Dive","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\"><strong>Cilium</strong> replaces legacy iptables and kube-proxy with extended Berkeley Packet Filter (eBPF) programs running directly in the Linux kernel.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>kube-proxy Replacement:</strong> Bypasses netfilter completely, handling Service load balancing at the socket layer (<code>BPF host-routing</code>).</li>\n  <li><strong>Hubble Observability:</strong> Provides deep packet inspection, Layer 7 flow logs, service dependency graphs, and DNS metrics without code instrumentation.</li>\n  <li><strong>Troubleshooting:</strong> Use <code>cilium status</code>, <code>cilium monitor</code> to inspect kernel packet drops, and <code>cilium bpf lb list</code> to inspect BPF load balancer maps.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Verify Cilium eBPF status and kube-proxy replacement\ncilium status --verbose\ncilium bpf lb list # Inspect eBPF load balancer table\nhubble observe --namespace prod --follow</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Requires a modern Linux kernel (5.4+, recommended 5.15+). Running on older kernels disables advanced BPF socket load balancing.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">State: 'Cilium uses eBPF to execute networking, security, and observability directly in the Linux kernel, eliminating iptables overhead.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"kub_extra_2","level":"senior","question":"How do you architect and manage Multi-Cluster Kubernetes topologies at enterprise scale?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Architecture","MultiCluster"],"source":"K8s Production Deep Dive","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Multi-cluster architectures partition workloads across separate physical clusters for blast-radius containment, compliance, multi-region disaster recovery, and latency optimization.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Cross-Cluster Networking:</strong> Implemented via <strong>Cilium ClusterMesh</strong> or <strong>Submariner</strong> to establish encrypted WireGuard/IPsec tunnels between cluster Pod CIDRs.</li>\n  <li><strong>Multi-Cluster Ingress:</strong> Use Global Server Load Balancing (AWS Route53 latency routing, Cloudflare) or Multi-Cluster Ingress (MCI).</li>\n  <li><strong>Centralized GitOps:</strong> A management cluster running ArgoCD orchestrates deployments across fleet clusters via ApplicationSets.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1\nkind: ApplicationSet\nmetadata:\n  name: fleet-deployments\nspec:\n  generators:\n  - clusters: {} # Deploys application across all registered fleet clusters</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Pod and Service CIDRs across peered clusters MUST NOT overlap; plan your cluster IP allocations carefully before establishing mesh connectivity.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Highlight: 'Centralized ArgoCD ApplicationSets for deployment, and Cilium ClusterMesh with non-overlapping CIDRs for cross-cluster networking.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"kub_extra_3","level":"senior","question":"How do you audit and harden Kubernetes clusters against CIS Benchmarks using kube-bench?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Security","Compliance"],"source":"K8s Production Deep Dive","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">The <strong>Center for Internet Security (CIS) Kubernetes Benchmark</strong> defines over 100 objective security guidelines for control plane and node configurations.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>kube-bench:</strong> An automated open-source Go tool from Aqua Security that checks whether control plane and worker nodes meet CIS standards.</li>\n  <li><strong>Key CIS Requirements:</strong></li>\n  <li>• Control plane file permissions (<code>/etc/kubernetes/pki</code> must be 600 or 644, owned by root).</li>\n  <li>• API Server flags: Disable <code>--anonymous-auth=false</code>, enforce <code>--authorization-mode=Node,RBAC</code>.</li>\n  <li>• Kubelet configuration: Disable anonymous auth, enforce read-only port closure (port 10255 = 0).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Run kube-bench as a Job on worker nodes\nkubectl apply -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/aquasecurity/kube-bench/main/job.yaml\nkubectl logs -l app=kube-bench</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">In managed cloud Kubernetes (EKS/GKE), control plane nodes are managed by the cloud vendor; CIS checks focus primarily on worker node Kubelet and workload configs.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Run kube-bench as part of continuous CI/CD security audits to ensure compliance.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"kub_extra_4","level":"senior","question":"How do you configure Velero for cluster disaster recovery and persistent volume backups?","tags":["KUBERNETES","DisasterRecovery","Backup"],"source":"K8s Production Deep Dive","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\"><strong>Velero</strong> is the industry-standard backup and disaster recovery tool for Kubernetes, backing up both etcd object metadata and physical storage volumes.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Metadata Backup:</strong> Queries API server and archives JSON manifests to object storage (AWS S3, Azure Blob, GCS).</li>\n  <li><strong>Volume Backup:</strong> Leverages the Container Storage Interface (CSI) <code>VolumeSnapshot</code> API to take point-in-time storage snapshots.</li>\n  <li><strong>Restore:</strong> Recreates complete cluster state, namespaces, Secrets, PVCs, and workloads in a new cluster.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Backup complete production namespace including volumes\nvelero backup create prod-backup-$(date +%F) --include-namespaces prod --csi-snapshot-timeout 20m\n\n# Restore into a new disaster recovery cluster\nvelero restore create --from-backup prod-backup-2026-10-01</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Always test backup restores regularly; an untested backup is not a backup! Practice disaster recovery drills in an isolated staging cluster.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">State: 'Velero backs up cluster metadata to S3 and uses CSI VolumeSnapshots for storage disks.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"kub_extra_5","level":"senior","question":"How do you configure cert-manager with Let's Encrypt and Cloudflare DNS01 validation?","tags":["KUBERNETES","TLS","Security"],"source":"K8s Production Deep Dive","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Automating wildcard and internal SSL/TLS certificates requires cert-manager with DNS-01 challenge validation.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>DNS-01 vs HTTP-01:</strong> DNS-01 works for internal clusters and wildcard certificates (<code>*.company.com</code>) by creating dynamic TXT records in Cloudflare/Route53.</li>\n  <li>cert-manager provisions the certificate, writes it to a native TLS Secret, and automatically renews it 30 days before expiration.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>apiVersion: cert-manager.io/v1\nkind: ClusterIssuer\nmetadata:\n  name: cloudflare-dns\nspec:\n  acme:\n    server: https://acme-v02.api.letsencrypt.org/directory\n    email: admin@company.com\n    privateKeySecretRef: { name: letsencrypt-account }\n    solvers:\n    - dns01:\n        cloudflare:\n          apiTokenSecretRef:\n            name: cloudflare-api-token\n            key: api-token</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Rate limits: Let's Encrypt has strict production rate limits (50 certs per domain per week). Use the Let's Encrypt Staging environment during testing.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Use DNS-01 challenges for wildcard certificates and private internal endpoints.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"kub_extra_6","level":"senior","question":"How do you build custom Kubernetes Controllers using KubeBuilder and Go?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Development","Operators"],"source":"K8s Production Deep Dive","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\"><strong>KubeBuilder</strong> is the official SDK for building production-grade Kubernetes APIs and Controllers using Go.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Core Components:</strong></li>\n  <li>• <code>Scheme:</code> Registers Go structs as Kubernetes API types.</li>\n  <li>• <code>Manager:</code> Initializes shared Informers, Webhooks, and Clients.</li>\n  <li>• <code>Reconciler:</code> Implements <code>Reconcile(ctx, req) (Result, error)</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Level-Triggered Design:</strong> The Reconciler must be idempotent; it fetches the current object, evaluates desired state, applies diffs, and updates status.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> GO</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>func (r *AppReconciler) Reconcile(ctx context.Context, req ctrl.Request) (ctrl.Result, error) {\n    var app myv1.Application\n    if err := r.Get(ctx, req.NamespacedName, &amp;app); err != nil {\n        return ctrl.Result{}, client.IgnoreNotFound(err)\n    }\n    // Reconcile desired state...\n    return ctrl.Result{RequeueAfter: time.Minute * 10}, nil\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Never make blocking external network calls inside the Reconcile function without timeouts; a hanging reconciler blocks the worker thread queue.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Emphasize level-triggered reconciliation: 'Controllers react to desired state diffs, not individual historical events.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"kub_extra_7","level":"senior","question":"How do you configure the Kubernetes Descheduler to rebalance fragmented worker nodes?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Operations","Optimization"],"source":"K8s Production Deep Dive","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">The default scheduler only evaluates nodes at <em>pod creation time</em>. As nodes join or leave, pods become unevenly distributed. The <strong>Descheduler</strong> evicts pods to trigger rebalancing.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>Runs as a CronJob or Deployment evaluating cluster balance.</li>\n  <li><strong>Key Strategies:</strong></li>\n  <li>• <code>RemoveDuplicates:</code> Ensures replicas of the same deployment are not clumped on one node.</li>\n  <li>• <code>LowNodeUtilization:</code> Evicts pods from over-utilized nodes so they land on under-utilized nodes.</li>\n  <li>• <code>RemovePodsViolatingTopologySpreadConstraint:</code> Enforces zone spread.</li>\n  <li>Always respects PodDisruptionBudgets (PDBs).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>apiVersion: \"descheduler/v1alpha2\"\nkind: DeschedulerPolicy\nprofiles:\n- name: default\n  pluginConfig:\n  - name: \"RemoveDuplicates\"\n  - name: \"LowNodeUtilization\"\n    args:\n      thresholds: { \"cpu\": 20, \"memory\": 20 }\n      targetThresholds: { \"cpu\": 50, \"memory\": 50 }</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">The Descheduler only EVICTS pods; it relies on the standard kube-scheduler to place the newly created replacement pods.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Deploy Descheduler to continuously eliminate node fragmentation and enforce topology spread.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"kub_extra_8","level":"senior","question":"How do you perform zero-downtime database maintenance on StatefulSets?","tags":["KUBERNETES","StatefulSet","Databases"],"source":"K8s Production Deep Dive","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Maintaining clustered databases (PostgreSQL, MySQL, Kafka) on StatefulSets requires ordered, replica-first maintenance.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>1. <strong>Update Read Replicas First:</strong> Upgrade pods in reverse ordinal order (<code>db-2</code>, <code>db-1</code>).</li>\n  <li>2. <strong>Promote Replica:</strong> Trigger database failover so an upgraded replica becomes the new primary.</li>\n  <li>3. <strong>Upgrade Former Primary:</strong> Finally upgrade <code>db-0</code> once it is running in replica mode.</li>\n  <li>Use <code>partition</code> in <code>updateStrategy.rollingUpdate</code> to control exactly which ordinals are updated.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>spec:\n  updateStrategy:\n    type: RollingUpdate\n    rollingUpdate:\n      partition: 2 # Only pods with ordinal &gt;= 2 will be updated</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Never blindly apply rolling updates to a primary database StatefulSet without an Operator handling automated primary failover.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Use <code>partition</code> in StatefulSet rolling updates to canary test database upgrades on individual ordinals.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"kub_extra_9","level":"senior","question":"How do you configure Pod Topology Spread Constraints to ensure multi-AZ resilience?","tags":["KUBERNETES","HighAvailability","Scheduling"],"source":"K8s Production Deep Dive","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\"><strong>Topology Spread Constraints</strong> evenly distribute Pods across failure domains (Availability Zones, nodes, racks) with fine-grained skew control.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>• <code>maxSkew:</code> Maximum allowable difference in pod count between any two topology domains (e.g. <code>maxSkew: 1</code>).</li>\n  <li>• <code>topologyKey:</code> Node label defining the failure domain (e.g. <code>topology.kubernetes.io/zone</code>).</li>\n  <li>• <code>whenUnsatisfiable:</code> <code>DoNotSchedule</code> (hard enforcement) or <code>ScheduleAnyway</code> (soft preference).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>topologySpreadConstraints:\n- maxSkew: 1\n  topologyKey: topology.kubernetes.io/zone\n  whenUnsatisfiable: DoNotSchedule\n  labelSelector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: web-api</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">If an AWS AZ suffers an outage and capacity is lost, <code>whenUnsatisfiable: DoNotSchedule</code> with <code>maxSkew: 1</code> will prevent new pods from launching in surviving zones! Use <code>ScheduleAnyway</code> if survival outweighs strict balance.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Standard HA pattern: Topology Spread Constraints with <code>topologyKey: topology.kubernetes.io/zone</code> and <code>maxSkew: 1</code>.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"kub_extra_10","level":"senior","question":"How do you configure KEDA (Kubernetes Event-driven Autoscaling) for Kafka or SQS scaling?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Autoscaling","KEDA"],"source":"K8s Production Deep Dive","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\"><strong>KEDA</strong> scales Kubernetes workloads based on external event sources (Kafka lag, AWS SQS queue depth, RabbitMQ queues), enabling scaling from 0 to thousands.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>Acts as a custom metrics adapter for the standard HPA.</li>\n  <li><strong>Scale to Zero:</strong> Unlike standard HPA (which cannot scale to 0), KEDA scales deployments to 0 replicas when queues are empty, saving massive cloud costs.</li>\n  <li>When an event arrives, KEDA scales to 1, then lets HPA manage dynamic scaling.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>apiVersion: keda.sh/v1alpha1\nkind: ScaledObject\nmetadata:\n  name: sqs-scaler\nspec:\n  scaleTargetRef:\n    name: order-processor\n  minReplicaCount: 0 # Scales to zero when idle\n  maxReplicaCount: 30\n  triggers:\n  - type: aws-sqs-queue\n    metadata:\n      queueURL: https://sqs.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/123/orders\n      queueLength: \"20\" # 1 replica per 20 messages</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Scaling from 0 has a cold-start penalty (pod scheduling + image pull). For latency-critical APIs, set <code>minReplicaCount: 1</code>.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">State: 'KEDA extends HPA with event-driven triggers and scale-to-zero capabilities for queues and event streams.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"kub_extra_11","level":"senior","question":"How do you configure Cilium Layer 7 HTTP and DNS NetworkPolicies?","tags":["KUBERNETES","Security","Cilium"],"source":"K8s Production Deep Dive","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Cilium eBPF enforces Layer 7 security policies, inspecting HTTP methods, URL paths, and DNS FQDN queries.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>Standard Kubernetes NetworkPolicies only operate at Layer 3/4 (IP and port).</li>\n  <li>Cilium transparently redirects L7 traffic to an embedded Envoy proxy without sidecars, enforcing policies like 'Allow only GET /health' or 'Allow egress only to *.stripe.com'.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>apiVersion: \"cilium.io/v2\"\nkind: CiliumNetworkPolicy\nmetadata:\n  name: l7-api-policy\nspec:\n  endpointSelector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: backend\n  ingress:\n  - fromEndpoints:\n    - matchLabels:\n        app: frontend\n    toPorts:\n    - ports:\n      - port: \"8080\"\n        protocol: TCP\n      rules:\n        http:\n        - method: \"GET\"\n          path: \"/api/v1/public/.*\"</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">L7 inspection incurs minor CPU overhead for proxy evaluation; apply L7 rules selectively to sensitive endpoints.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Highlight: 'Cilium provides sidecarless L7 HTTP path and DNS domain filtering directly in eBPF.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"kub_extra_12","level":"senior","question":"How do you architect multi-tenant Kubernetes clusters using vCluster and virtual control planes?","tags":["KUBERNETES","MultiTenancy","Architecture"],"source":"K8s Production Deep Dive","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\"><strong>vCluster</strong> provides virtual Kubernetes clusters running inside a single namespace of a host cluster, offering true hard multi-tenancy without physical cluster sprawl.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>Each tenant gets their own dedicated, virtual control plane (API server, etcd) inside their namespace.</li>\n  <li>Tenants can create their own CRDs, Namespaces, and cluster-admin RBAC roles without impacting the host cluster.</li>\n  <li>Workloads are synced down and scheduled onto the host cluster's worker nodes.</li>\n  <li>Reduces infrastructure cost by 70% compared to spinning up separate cloud clusters.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Create a virtual cluster for Team Alpha\nvcluster create team-alpha-cluster -n team-alpha\nvcluster connect team-alpha-cluster</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Virtual clusters share the host cluster's Linux kernel; untrusted tenant code should still be sandboxed with gVisor or Kata Containers.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Recommend vCluster for dev/staging environments to provide complete cluster independence at namespace cost.</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"kub_extra_13","level":"senior","question":"How do you conduct Kubernetes Chaos Engineering using Chaos Mesh?","tags":["KUBERNETES","SRE","ChaosEngineering"],"source":"K8s Production Deep Dive","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\"><strong>Chaos Engineering</strong> proactively injects controlled failures (pod crashes, network latency, packet loss, node reboots) into production to verify self-healing resilience.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Chaos Mesh:</strong> A cloud-native chaos engineering platform using CRDs and eBPF/iptables.</li>\n  <li><strong>Simulations:</strong></li>\n  <li>• <code>PodChaos:</code> Randomly kills pods to test ReplicaSet and PDB resilience.</li>\n  <li>• <code>NetworkChaos:</code> Injects 100ms latency, 20% packet drop, or network partitions to test circuit breakers.</li>\n  <li>• <code>StressChaos:</code> Maxes out CPU/memory to verify HPA and OOMKill handling.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>apiVersion: chaos-mesh.org/v1alpha1\nkind: NetworkChaos\nmetadata:\n  name: inject-latency\nspec:\n  action: delay\n  mode: fixed\n  value: \"2\"\n  selector:\n    namespaces: [\"staging\"]\n    labelSelectors: { \"app\": \"payment\" }\n  delay:\n    latency: \"150ms\"\n    jitter: \"20ms\"\n  duration: \"5m\"</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Always define automated abort conditions (Steady State Hypothesis) so chaos experiments auto-terminate if business SLOs drop.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">State: 'Chaos engineering verifies that our self-healing, PDBs, and retry mechanisms work before real production outages strike.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"kub_extra_14","level":"senior","question":"How do you conduct a complete Kubernetes disaster recovery drill for etcd loss?","tags":["KUBERNETES","DisasterRecovery","etcd"],"source":"K8s Production Deep Dive","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">A disaster recovery drill tests the complete restoration of a corrupt or dead etcd cluster from a point-in-time snapshot.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li>1. <strong>Snapshot Creation:</strong> Regular automated hourly snapshots via <code>etcdctl snapshot save</code>.</li>\n  <li>2. <strong>Stop API Servers:</strong> Prevent writes during restoration.</li>\n  <li>3. <strong>Restore etcd:</strong> Run <code>etcdctl snapshot restore</code> across all control plane nodes with matching cluster tokens.</li>\n  <li>4. <strong>Restart Control Plane:</strong> Restart kube-apiserver, kube-scheduler, and kube-controller-manager.</li>\n  <li>5. <strong>Verify Workload State:</strong> Kubelet reconnects and reconciles running workloads against the restored etcd database.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># 1. Save etcd snapshot\nETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt   --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt   --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key   snapshot save /backup/etcd-snapshot.db\n\n# 2. Restore etcd snapshot to new data directory\nETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot restore /backup/etcd-snapshot.db   --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Any objects created between the snapshot timestamp and the crash will be lost; take frequent automated snapshots and store them offsite in S3.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Documented disaster recovery drill: Stop API server → Restore snapshot with etcdctl → Point etcd data-dir → Restart static pods → Verify cluster state.</p>\n</div>"}];
